(window.GAMES = window.GAMES || {})[2001] = [{"t":"007: Agent Under Fire","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"November 13"},{"t":"1821: The Struggle for Freedom","p":["Windows"],"d":"Metal Fin","u":"Metal Fin","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1821:_The_Struggle_for_Freedom"},{"t":"21: Two One","p":["Windows","Dreamcast"],"d":"BasiL","u":"BasiL, PrincessSoft","g":"Visual novel","r":"May 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/21:_Two_One","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cocp7v.jpg","s":"21: Two One is an erotic visual novel adventure game."},{"t":"3D Pocket Pool","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Aardvark Software","u":"Virgin Interactive","g":"Sports simulation – pool","r":"March 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3D_Pocket_Pool","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co203v.jpg","s":"The game itself is a typical billiard variation. You can see your character and your enemy interacting at the top of the screen. Also the game has a freely rotatable 3D billiard table and realtime physics."},{"t":"4x4 Evo 2","p":["Windows","Xbox"],"d":"Terminal Reality","u":"Gathering of Developers, Aspyr, Universal Interactive, BAM! Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"October 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/4x4_Evo_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9ebj.jpg","s":"Professional drivers get paid by winning races and in career mode so do you. Win fast money and deck out your truck from over 125 upgrades like performance shocks, race tires, brush guards, roll bars, snorkels, working winches and performance exhaust to increase vehicle power and speed."},{"t":"90 Minutes","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"Smilebit","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","r":"October 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/90_Minutes"},{"t":"911 Fire Rescue","p":["Windows"],"d":"Sunstorm Interactive","u":"WizardWorks","g":"Simulator","r":"June 11, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/911_Fire_Rescue","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8i51.jpg","s":"911 Fire Rescue is a first-person \"Douser\" that pits you against raging fires. You run into burning buildings with your fire hose to put out fires and rescue trapped victims. The game is set in houses and buildings, simulating smoke that blurs your vision and burns your lungs. The missions require you to find the people trapped inside."},{"t":"Ace Combat 04: Shattered Skies","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Namco","r":"September 13, 2001"},{"t":"Action Man: Search for Base X","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"February 6"},{"t":"Advance Wars","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"September 10, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advance_Wars"},{"t":"Adventure at the Chateau d'Or","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Karma Labs","u":"Karma Labs","g":"Adventure","r":"April 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adventure_at_the_Chateau_d'Or","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3rt7.jpg","s":"The princess heir has chosen you as her hero and you lead the quest to unlock the secret of the fabulous Chateau d’Or. Call upon your intelligence, intuition and newfound knowledge to survive and bring the princess safely to her goal. Test your powers of observation and intellect in this thrilling game. The fate of the Princess of the Chateau d’Or lies in your hands."},{"t":"Adventure Pinball: Forgotten Island","p":["Windows"],"d":"Digital Extremes","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Pinball","r":"March 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adventure_Pinball:_Forgotten_Island","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co309h.jpg","s":"It starts when you receive a note and map from your long lost grandfather, along with the promise of the adventure of a lifetime. You'll encounter dinosaurs, volcanoes, and man-eating sharks, as well as a tribe of cavemen to save. Your mission is to save the Forgotten Island.\n\nThere are 9 interconnected tables included for you to conquer. They have to be unlocked during the game and offer 2 or 3 settings per table. In comparison to most other pinball simulations the goals on a table are mostly not to get a high score but to solve missions like chasing away sharks. Also unusual are the 3D graphics: instead of a static top-down view the camera follows the pinball ball."},{"t":"Aetolia","p":["macOS","Browser","Windows","Linux"],"g":"Role-playing (RPG)","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aetolia_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2pe9.jpg","s":"Aetolia is a multiplayer RPG (role-playing game) set in a gothic-fantasy world. Aetolia is played entirely in text with minimal graphics.\n\nCharacters in Aetolia talk, trade, fight, plot and even kill and eat each other. They roam the continents and explore dungeons, killing (or helping) foul creatures, gather gold and treasure while increasing in power and prestige.\n\nUnlike other fantasy settings, Aetolia enables you to play characters of all alignments and creeds, and it's entirely in the hands of the player whether to become a hated villain, vaunted hero, or anything in between."},{"t":"Age of Sail II","p":["Windows"],"d":"Akella","u":"TalonSoft, Global Star Software","g":"Computer wargame","r":"February 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_of_Sail_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/kim8e8yxpus2z2hftot2.jpg","s":"Age of Sail II simulates naval warfare from 1775 to 1820 with a real-time 3-D sailing and combat model. Over 1,200 historical ships and 100 historical American and British scenarios are available, including: Copenhagen, the Battle of the Nile, Trafalgar, Cape St. Vincent, and Camperdown. A full, floating 3-D camera allows complete control of your fleet, and the 3-D accelerated graphics reveal accurately rendered ships and beautiful landscapes"},{"t":"Aidyn Chronicles: The First Mage","p":["Nintendo 64"],"r":"March 14"},{"t":"Air Ranger: Rescue Helicopter","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"ASK","u":"ASK, Midas Interactive Entertainment","g":"Flight simulator","r":"March 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_Ranger:_Rescue_Helicopter"},{"t":"Airburst","p":["macOS"],"d":"Strange Flavour","u":"Freeverse","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airburst_(video_game)"},{"t":"AirForce Delta Storm","p":["Xbox"],"d":"Konami Computer Entertainment Studios, Mobile21","u":"Konami","g":"Flight simulator","r":"November 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AirForce_Delta_Storm","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co30uz.jpg","s":"Strap into your cockpit and blast through the gut-wrenching world of flight combat. Your adrenaline pumps as machine gun fire pelts off your tail, you execute back-breaking barrel rolls and experience dog-fighting action. Take your pick of over 70 planes, including military fighters rendered from actual aircraft. With a full range of extreme missions to numb your senses, AirForce Delta Storm pushes your Xbox to the sky!"},{"t":"Alcatraz: Prison Escape","p":["Windows"],"d":"Zombie Inc.","u":"Activision Value","g":"Stealth","r":"November 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcatraz:_Prison_Escape","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/wqbhux5ewstttmxdcmsd.jpg"},{"t":"Alchemy","p":["Windows"],"r":"August 29","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alchemy_(game)"},{"t":"Alfred Hitchcock Presents: The Final Cut","p":["Windows"],"d":"Arxel Tribe","u":"Wanadoo Edition, Ubi Soft","g":"Action-adventure","r":"November 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Hitchcock_Presents:_The_Final_Cut","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co25eh.jpg","s":"Robert Marvin-Jones is a billionaire recluse who decides to pay homage to Alfred Hitchcock by making a film in his backyard; however his film crew have gone missing and possibly been brutally murdered. Meanwhile, private detective Joseph Shamley has psychic power. Jones' niece, a mute named Alicia, asks Shamley to use his powers of deduction to solve the mystery"},{"t":"Alien Front Online","p":["Arcade","Dreamcast"],"d":"Wow Entertainment","u":"Sega","g":"Vehicular combat","r":"January 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_Front_Online","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coawt0.jpg","s":"Alien Front Online is a Dreamcast video game originally released for the console in 2001.\n\nIn the game, players use armored vehicles to fight tense battles staged in large, interactive arenas. Players could choose which side they wished to fight for, the aliens or the human defenders of Earth, and could also choose one of three different vehicles to combat with; each having its own advantages and disadvantages. Although Alien Front was fully playable for one person, it was primarily designed as an online game in which up to 8 players (up to 4 on each side) could battle it out. Players move around the arena attempting to blast each other with their weaponry, while also looking for power-ups that will enhance their ability to wreak massive destruction. Typically, the game was played for a set number of minutes, after which the team with the most kills would be declared the winners. Being killed while playing the game had no serious consequences (you simply respawned in a different part of the area you were in), but it did reflect on your win/loss score."},{"t":"Alienators: Evolution Continues","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Digital Eclipse","u":"Activision","r":"November 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alienators:_Evolution_Continues_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4z71.jpg","s":"You thought you'd rid the Earth of the aliens. But now they're back, and the evolution of hostile life-forms continues. Lucky for Earth, the Alienators are on the job. Use all the alien-butt-kicking science within your grasp to battle the Genus... before this evil evolves to a state of total supremacy and succeeds in global domination!"},{"t":"Aliens versus Predator 2","p":["Windows"],"r":"October 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aliens_versus_Predator_2"},{"t":"Aliens: Thanatos Encounter","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Crawfish Interactive","u":"THQ","g":"Shooter","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aliens:_Thanatos_Encounter","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3j7s.jpg","s":"Prepare for horror and fright beyond your wildest nightmares, as you step into the world of ALIENS: Thanatos Encounter. The terrifying creatures made famous by the popular ALIENS movies come to Game Boy Color in a totally new adventure. You play as one of five space marines who are sent to explore an abandoned freighter. Naturally, the situation is far worse than your squad expected the ship is completely infested with thousands of vicious aliens. In this single-player shooter, you'll make your way through the various sections of the ship in 12 missions. Discover the solution to the mystery of the aliens' reappearance through the cinematic cutscenes you'll see throughout the game. You can use a variety of alien-busting weapons like machine guns and flame-throwers to fend off the incoming horde and, if all else fails, turn tail and run as fast as you can! Battle a wide variety of different breeds of aliens, including some all-new hybrids."},{"t":"All-Star Baseball 2002","p":["PlayStation 2","GameCube"],"d":"Acclaim Studios Austin","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"March 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/All-Star_Baseball_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6dll.jpg","s":"All-Star Baseball 2002 has the complete 2001-2002 MLBPA rosters. You may choose to play as one of the current teams, or you can go back to the past and play as people like Nolan Ryan or Reggie Jackson. You can also create your own player and/or team.\n\nThe featured game modes are Quick Play, Exhibition, All-Star Game, Season, Series, Home Run Derby and Batting Practice. Batting is cursor based, and you can choose between a power or a contact swing - for the latter there are even more options available, e.g. trying to hit the opposite field. Batting is similar in as you choose your itching style (every athlete can have up to six in his repertoire) and then choose the location."},{"t":"Alone in the Dark: The New Nightmare","p":["PlayStation","Game Boy Color","Windows","Dreamcast","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Darkworks","u":"Infogrames","g":"Survival horror","r":"May 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alone_in_the_Dark:_The_New_Nightmare","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1wc4.jpg","s":"Alone in the Dark: The New Nightmare is the fourth installment in the survival horror video game series Alone in the Dark. The player can choose which of the two protagonists to play as. Carnby’s side of the plot focuses mainly on fighting monsters through physical means, while Aline’s is more centered on puzzle-solving. The two occasionally meet, and the main plot areas intersect."},{"t":"Amazing Learning Games with Rayman","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","PlayStation"],"d":"Ubi Soft","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Education","r":"August 9, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazing_Learning_Games_with_Rayman"},{"t":"American Bass Challenge","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"December 5","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Bass_Challenge"},{"t":"Amped: Freestyle Snowboarding","p":["Xbox"],"d":"Salt Lake Games Studio","u":"Microsoft Game Studios","g":"Snowboarding","r":"November 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amped:_Freestyle_Snowboarding","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3g92.jpg","s":"Rip the ultimate line on wide-open, real mountains packed with challenges. Whether you hit the cliffs, grind fallen trees, jib the rainbow tree rail, or take the hidden paths, you decide where and how you go down. While you pull off insane tricks on rails, kickers, and half pipes, remember to pose for roaming photographers and camera crews. With enough exposure, you'll find your face on magazine covers and videos and even become an international media superstar."},{"t":"Anachronox","p":["Windows"],"g":"RPG","r":"June 27","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anachronox","i":"images/steam-242940.jpg","s":"Join Sylvester &quot;Sly&quot; Boots and Stiletto Anyway as they trek across six bizarre planets investigating a galaxy-crushing mystery, searching for artefacts from an extinct alien race and unlocking the secrets of a powerful, advanced technology. It's your turn. PICK YOUR POISON."},{"t":"Anarchy Online","p":["Windows"],"r":"June 27","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anarchy_Online"},{"t":"Angel Present","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angel_Present","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co73p2.jpg","s":"Angel Present takes place in a 2.5D environment with adventure elements in 2 different worlds, with 3d battles. The game mixes these more action-based elements, with static screens and text and CG movies. The story centers around the main protagonist stumbling across a fairy and soon discovering they have magical powers."},{"t":"Antz Racing","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"RFX Interactive","u":"Light & Shadow Production, Electronic Arts, Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"March 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antz_Racing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co81my.jpg","s":"Antz Racing is a racing game for the Game Boy Color."},{"t":"Arabian Nights","p":["Windows"],"d":"Silmarils","u":"Visiware, Wanadoo Edition, The Adventure Company","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arabian_Nights_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7o27.jpg","s":"Arabian Nights is a 3-D action-adventure arcade video game published in 2001 for Microsoft Windows. It was published by Visiware and Wanadoo. It is based on One Thousand and One Nights."},{"t":"Arcanum: Of Steamworks and Magick Obscura","p":["Windows"],"g":"RPG","r":"August 21","l":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/500810","i":"images/steam-500810.jpg","s":"Imagine a place of wonder, where magick and technology coexist in an uneasy balance, and an adventurer might just as easily wield a flintlock pistol as a flaming sword. Welcome to the land of Arcanum."},{"t":"Arctic Thunder","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"September 17","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctic_Thunder"},{"t":"Armagetron Advanced","p":["Windows"],"g":"Racing, Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armagetron_Advanced","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2hqd.jpg","s":"Armagetron is a multiplayer game in 3d that attempts to emulate and expand on the lightcycle sequence from the movie Tron. It's an old school arcade game slung into the 21st century. Highlights include a customizable playing arena, HUD, unique graphics, and AI bots. For the more advanced player there are new game modes and a wide variety of physics settings to tweak as well. It is available for Linux, Mac OS X, Microsoft Windows, AmigaOS 4 and OpenBSD as free software released under version 2 of the GNU General Public License."},{"t":"Armored Core 2: Another Age","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"August 20"},{"t":"Army Men Advance","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"DC Studios","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Top-down shooter","r":"June 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Army_Men_Advance","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co50mn.jpg","s":"Plastro, the infamous leader of the Tan Army, has recruited alien forces from another world to aid in his ongoing war against the Green Nation. It's up to Sarge and Vikki G. to jump into action to save their troops from a variety of unknown enemies, and then track down Plastro himself for a final confrontation!"},{"t":"Army Men: Green Rogue","p":["PlayStation 2","PlayStation"],"d":"The 3DO Company","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"March 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Army_Men:_Green_Rogue","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2eub.jpg","s":"Created in a covert laboratory by splicing DNA from the strongest and most skilled soldiers, the \"Omega Soldier\" has been given the strength and weapon skills to become the Tan army's worst high-tech threat!\nArmy Men: Green Rogue incorporates a 3rd person shooter with coin-op shoot 'em style gameplay that will raise the heartbeat of anyone who dares to accept the challenge."},{"t":"Army Men: Operation Green","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Pocket Studios","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Top-down shooter","r":"December 3, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Army_Men:_Operation_Green","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6t2f.jpg","s":"The Tan Army is back?you must defeat each and every one of them, destroy the secret device and lead the Green Army to victory. The first Army Men title to bring realistic military action and combat to the Game Boy Advance!"},{"t":"Army Men: World War - Final Front","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"The 3DO Company","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Third-person shooter","r":"February 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Army_Men:_World_War_-_Final_Front","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6t2c.jpg","s":"Will the madness ever end? It doesn't look likely, with conflict on a global scale flaring up again for the increasingly weary Green Army, as they fight to keep liberty and justice alive by pushing back the nefarious pursuits of the Tan Army and it's wicked leaders. Hot on the heels of the first war comes this new battle, one that will take our faithful green soldiers into battle on land, sea, air, and even deep underground to rat out the Tan forces. The ultimate mission: seek and destroy the secret Tan weapons factory and perhaps put an end to this struggle for once and for all.\n\nArmy Men: World War - Final Front is another chapter in the popular Army Men series from 3DO, and the sequel to Army Men: World War - Land Sea and Air. It once again places the players in control of a Green Army soldier dedicated to the mission of eradicating the Tan forces from every corner of the globe. Players will guide the soldier via a third-person, behind-the-back perspective, marching him boldly forward to take on the enemy onslaught.\n\nThe single-player game takes players through five different campaign, each set in a different global location and each with their own specific objectives. The soldier has a host of weapons to fight off the enemy soldiers and vehicles, armaments ranging from rifles and bazookas to flame throwers. He'll also be able to procure vehicles himself. Players will pilot tanks, jeeps, cargo trucks, motorcycles, and submarines in their quest to destroy the enemy supply and weapon lines.\n\nIn Multiplayer mode, two players are able to compete in head-to-head action or team up against the computer. Capture the Flag is included, is Grudge Match, which simply lets them blow each other up until only one is left standing. There is also a mode called Steal the Bacon, which is reminiscent of Kill the Man with the Ball, only instead of a ball, you've got to keep your hands on the bacon while your enemies hunt you down."},{"t":"Army Men: World War - Team Assault","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"5000ft Inc.","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Third-person shooter, Strategy","r":"November 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Army_Men:_World_War_-_Team_Assault"},{"t":"Astonia III","p":["Windows"],"u":"Intent Software","g":"MMORPG","r":"December 31, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astonia_III","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/sw4emhvxauqc0oyy1kdp.jpg","s":"Astonia is a free online role-playing game (MMORPG). Join this vast fantasy world as either a Mage or Warrior and explore the game world with thousands of other players. Make friends, amass huge amounts of game gold, create a powerful character or simply have fun playing this"},{"t":"Atari Anniversary Edition","p":["Windows","Dreamcast","PlayStation"],"d":"Digital Eclipse","u":"Infogrames, Inc.","g":"Various","r":"June 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atari_Anniversary_Edition"},{"t":"Atlantis III: The New World","p":["Windows","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Cryo Interactive","u":"Cryo Interactive, DreamCatcher Interactive","g":"Adventure","r":"September 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantis_III:_The_New_World","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coa450.jpg","s":"Step back into the land of dreams and legends. Embark on an incredible adventure and become immersed in an extraordinary tale of intrigue and mystery. Assume the role of an archaeologist as you search for the existence of a metropolis built, according to myth and legend, by the ancient Egyptians. Your quest for the mysterious secrets that have eluded many will take you on a journey through worlds of unequalled beauty. Let wisdom be your guide as you encounter challenging puzzles along your path and travel through time and spirit.Discover and explore the stunning surroundings of the vast Sahara Desert, the wondrous icy plains of Siberia, and the fascinating lands of ancient Egypt. The greatest treasure of all time lies waiting to be discovered."},{"t":"Atlantis The Lost Empire: Trial by Fire","p":["Windows"],"r":"May 18"},{"t":"Atlantis: The Lost Empire","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"June 14"},{"t":"Atsumare! GuruGuru Onsen","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atsumare!_GuruGuru_Onsen"},{"t":"ATV Offroad Fury","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Rainbow Studios","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"February 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ATV_Offroad_Fury","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6dsp.jpg","s":"Off-road racing game where you can choose from among 12 ATV models from manufacturers like Honda, Yamaha, and Kawasaki. You can even customize your rider's apparel and your ATV's specs before you enter into 6 event types spanning more than 20 tracks"},{"t":"Auxiliary Power's Demolition Derby and Figure 8 Race","p":["Windows"],"d":"Auxiliary Power","u":"Auxiliary Power","g":"Racing","r":"October 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auxiliary_Power's_Demolition_Derby_and_Figure_8_Race"},{"t":"Azurik: Rise of Perathia","p":["Xbox"],"r":"November 25"},{"t":"B-17 Gunner: Air War Over Germany","p":["Windows"],"d":"Sunstorm Interactive","u":"Infogrames","g":"Simulation","r":"May 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B-17_Gunner:_Air_War_Over_Germany","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/vvi65c0idbebthioguck.jpg","s":"An arcade style rail shooter where you man the gunner position of a B-17 Bomber."},{"t":"Back Track","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Telegames, JV Games","u":"Telegames","g":"First-person shooter","r":"October 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_Track"},{"t":"Backyard Basketball","p":["Game Boy Advance","macOS","PlayStation 2","Windows"],"d":"Humongous Entertainment, SolWorks, Mistic Software, Game Brains","u":"Infogrames/Atari, Fingerprint Network, Playground Productions","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backyard_Basketball"},{"t":"Baldur's Gate II: Throne of Bhaal","p":["Windows"],"u":"Interplay Entertainment","r":"June 22, 2001"},{"t":"Baldur's Gate: Dark Alliance","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"December 3"},{"t":"Ballistics","p":["Arcade","Linux","Windows"],"d":"Grin","u":"Xicat Interactive, Triotech, Linux Game Publishing","g":"Racing","r":"October 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballistics_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/tkxtjn0engza8vy9nqgc.jpg","s":"Ballistics is a futuristic racing computer game developed by Grin and published by Xicat Interactive in 2001. Grin developed an arcade version of the game, released in 2002 featuring a unique reclined seating position cabinet by Triotech Amusement. Players race across seven different tracks in various leagues against other competitors on high-speed hoverbikes.\n\nThe game was Grin's first to be released, and featured the first version of their Diesel game engine. Grin worked closely with NVIDIA to incorporate then new technologies into the game, and was marketed as one of the flagship titles for the GeForce 3 Series of graphics cards.\n\nCritical reaction was generally favourable, with reviewers being impressed by the beauty of the graphics and the thrilling depiction of speed. They were however, slightly disappointed with the shallow nature of the gameplay. A newer version of the arcade game was released in 2003, incorporating motion simulator technology into the arcade cabinet."},{"t":"Barbie Beach Vacation","p":["Windows"],"d":"Krome Studios","u":"Vivendi Universal Interactive Publishing","g":"Action-adventure","r":"October 3, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbie_Beach_Vacation","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6t6d.jpg","s":"Barbie Beach Vacation takes her and her friends to the the sands and the waves of a remote island."},{"t":"Barbie: Explorer","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Runecraft","u":"Vivendi Universal Interactive Publishing","g":"Action-adventure, platform","r":"September 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbie:_Explorer","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co25bg.jpg","s":"Explore the world with Barbie. Travel to Tibet, Egypt, and the African jungle on a mission to find four ancient, jeweled treasures. Run, jump, swing, climb, and dive to avoid obstacles and deadly pitfalls in a race to unlock the secrets of the Mystic Mirror. Travel only with Barbie or bring a friend, as this game supports up to two players."},{"t":"Barbie: Pet Rescue","p":["Game Boy Color","Windows"],"d":"HotGen","g":"Action","r":"January 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbie:_Pet_Rescue"},{"t":"Bass Strike","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"September 26","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bass_Strike"},{"t":"Batman: Chaos in Gotham","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"April 16"},{"t":"Batman: Gotham City Racer","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Sinister Games","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Racing","r":"April 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batman:_Gotham_City_Racer","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3zcn.jpg","s":"Cruise as Batman, Nightwing, or Batgirl - or one of 23 criminals like The Joker, Mr. Freeze, Catwoman, and Killer Croc. Master 15 supercharged vehicles loaded with signature weapons like Batarangs, Laughing Gas, Cat Claws, Sewage Slicks, and tons more. Live Batman's world through 51 episode-based missions with real cut-scenes from the TV show.\n\nGo on a crook-bustin' free-for-all in Patrol Mode or start a wild crimewave as a Villain and leave Batman in the dust! Go head-to-head against a friend in Hero-vs.-Villain splitscreen.\n\nBlaze through the urban maze of Gotham in massive 3D-rooming environments and multiple angles."},{"t":"Batman: Vengeance","p":["Windows","PlayStation 2","GameCube","Xbox","Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 15"},{"t":"Battle Beaster","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Beaster","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coatii.jpg","s":"A simulation game released exclusively in Japan for Dreamcast."},{"t":"Battle Realms","p":["Windows"],"d":"Liquid Entertainment","u":"Crave Entertainment, Ubi Soft","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"November 8, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Realms","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2ei7.jpg","s":"Kenji, exiled heir to the Dragon throne, must stake his claim in a chaotic world wracked by the subversive intrigues of the Serpent, Wolf, and Lotus clans. Shall he vanquish the land under a campaign of malevolence and terror or restore the war-torn realm to its former glory? Enter Battle Realms and choose your destiny. Explore the history that led up to the events in Battle Realms in Battle Realms: Winter of the White Wolf. Scattered and unorganized, your enslaved Wolf brethren hunger for revolt against their corrupt Lotus Clan masters. You must unite them. You must lead them. You must free them.\n\nBattle Realms is fantasy real-time strategy game inspired by the stories told in kung-fu movies and oriental mythology. Develop your tiny peasant village into a highly trained and deadly efficient clan of warriors who are ready to die with your name on their lips. Experiment with Unit Alchemy and turn villagers into one-of-a-kind fighters like Samurai Cannoneers, Bandits, Geisha, and scores of other mythical warriors. Defend your people against the hordes of barbarians, evil magicians, and deadly ninjas."},{"t":"Beatmania IIDX 5th Style","p":["Arcade","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Music","r":"March 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beatmania_IIDX_5th_Style","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co964g.jpg","s":"The 5th release in the beatmania IIDX series. Marks the first of many appearances from Namco mainstays sampling masters."},{"t":"Beatmania IIDX 6th Style","p":["Arcade","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Konami, Konami Computer Entertainment Japan","u":"Konami, Konami Computer Entertainment Japan","g":"Music","r":"September 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beatmania_IIDX_6th_Style","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co964f.jpg","s":"The 6th game in the beatmania IIDX series. This version saw a major graphical upgrade in terms of interface and overall polish. Features many licensed tracks from Avex's Super Eurobeat and Cutting Edge sub labels."},{"t":"Bejeweled","p":["Windows"],"r":"May 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bejeweled_(video_game)"},{"t":"Beyblade","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Wavedge Co","u":"Crave Entertainment","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyblade_(2001_video_game)"},{"t":"Bioscopia","p":[],"g":"Educational, adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bioscopia"},{"t":"Bittersweet Fools","p":["Windows","Dreamcast","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Minori, HuneX","u":"Minori, D3 Publisher","g":"Eroge, Visual novel","r":"August 31, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bittersweet_Fools","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cocag3.jpg","s":"Bittersweet Fools is a visual novel developed by Minori. The game takes place in Florence, Italy. The character design is by Yu Aida, whose Gunslinger Girl series also takes place in Italy."},{"t":"Black & White","p":["Windows"],"u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Adventure, Indie, RPG, Strategy","r":"March 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_%26_White_(video_game)","i":"images/steam-1998450.jpg","s":"&quot;Black &amp; White&quot; is a dystopian visual novel + war chess game: black and white, sensibility and reason, freedom and order: follow your beliefs and join your camp."},{"t":"Blood Wake","p":["Xbox"],"d":"Stormfront Studios","u":"Microsoft Game Studios","g":"Vehicle combat","r":"December 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood_Wake","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co31xd.jpg","s":"Blood Wake is a naval combat video game that was released in 2001 on the Xbox as part of Microsoft's initial wave of Xbox launch titles. The game was developed by Stormfront Studios and published by Microsoft Game Studios.\n\nSet in an Asian-themed fictional world, Blood Wake is a story-driven, mission-based high seas action game in which the player controls a series of small fighting vessels. Some reviewers described its gameplay as \"Twisted Metal on water,\" although its mission-based story line differentiated Blood Wake from Twisted Metal's arena-based gameplay."},{"t":"Blue Wing Blitz","p":["WonderSwan Color","WonderSwan"],"d":"Square","u":"Square","g":"Tactical role-playing game","r":"July 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Wing_Blitz","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8ntu.jpg","s":"Blue Wing Blitz is a traditional turn-based tactical role-playing game, although it has the particularity of involving mostly aerial warfare units. These are moved on a grid map, and an encounter with an enemy unit opens a separate, close-up combat screen in which up to two ally units and two enemy units engage in a short dogfight; the player using menu commands to attack, change altitude or take evasive maneuvers."},{"t":"Boku no Tennis Jinsei","p":["Dreamcast"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boku_no_Tennis_Jinsei","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1wim.jpg","s":"A tennis game for the Sega Dreamcast."},{"t":"Boku, Doraemon","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boku,_Doraemon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/hv6kjtiyo3vwdgyl4lsb.jpg","s":"Boku, Doraemon is a Doraemon adventure game for Dreamcast."},{"t":"Bomberman 64","p":["Nintendo 64"],"d":"Racjin","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Action","r":"December 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bomberman_64_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co25y5.jpg","s":"Not to be confused with the 1997 game known in North America and Europe as \"Bomberman 64,\" this 2D Bomberman title was the last N64 game to be released in Japan."},{"t":"Bomberman Kart","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Racjin, Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Kart-racing","r":"December 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bomberman_Kart","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1otb.jpg","s":"Bomberman Kart is a racing game for PlayStation 2 starring the Bomberman characters. It is similar to Mario Kart. The game supports 4 player local multiplayer using the PlayStation 2 Multitap. The first version was released in 2001 in Japan and later 2003 in Europe. The second version, called Bomberman Kart DX (director's cut) was released in 2004 only in Japan. Both versions have never been released in the United States."},{"t":"Bomberman Online","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"h.a.n.d., Hudson Soft","u":"Sega","g":"Action, maze, party","r":"October 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bomberman_Online","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co30fo.jpg","s":"Just when you thought Bomberman had planted his last explosive, the prolific video game hero returns for a modern update on his classic series. With addictive battles, frantic new modes, and brand new stages, Bomberman Online offers explosive action for all. Fans of the older multiplayer versions of the franchise will rejoice at the inclusion of four online modes Battle Royal, Hyper Bomber, Panel Attack, and Submarine Attack each designed to accommodate up to 10 players. The game's lone single-player mode boasts five awesome levels, and the graphics are rendered in 3D this time around."},{"t":"Bomberman Tournament","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"June 29","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bomberman_Tournament"},{"t":"Boong-Ga Boong-Ga","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Taff System","u":"TaffSystem","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boong-Ga_Boong-Ga","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coa4f9.jpg","s":"Boong-Ga Boong-Ga, also known as Spank 'em, is an wacky arcade game."},{"t":"BotFighters","p":["Mobile phone","Sega Master System"],"d":"It's Alive Mobile Games AB!","r":"March 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BotFighters"},{"t":"Boxing Fever","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxing_Fever"},{"t":"Breath of Fire","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"December 1","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breath_of_Fire_(video_game)"},{"t":"Bridge Base Online","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridge_Base_Online"},{"t":"Bulánci","p":["Windows"],"g":"Arcade","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bul%C3%A1nci","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2ad6.jpg","s":"The game sees players battle other Bulanci called EAP (extremely aggressive pillows). It is a 2D game with a top down view. The history within the game follows the trajectory of human history."},{"t":"Burnout","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"November 1","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burnout_(video_game)"},{"t":"C-12: Final Resistance","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Sony Computer Entertainment Europe","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Third-person shooter","r":"April 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C-12:_Final_Resistance","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coagzt.jpg","s":"It is an action shooter set in a futuristic dystopian city on Earth overthrown by aliens. The game has two modes - third person and first person. Most of the time the player sees the character in third person, however the player can also see through the character´s eyes and shoot. However, the player cannot move the character while in the first person. The game is mission-based and it is strictly linear. The main enemies are aliens and cyborgs."},{"t":"Cabela's 4x4 Off-Road Adventure","p":["Windows"],"d":"Fun Labs","u":"Activision Value","g":"Racing","r":"February 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabela's_4x4_Off-Road_Adventure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9f9x.jpg","s":"In 2001, Empire Interactive Europe Ltd., Activision Value Publishing, Inc. publishes Cabela's 4x4 Off-Road Adventure on Windows. This racing / driving game is set in an off-road / monster truck, licensed title, vehicle simulator and automobile themes."},{"t":"Cabela's Big Game Hunter: Ultimate Challenge","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Coresoft","u":"Activision","g":"Sports, first-person shooter","r":"December 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabela's_Big_Game_Hunter:_Ultimate_Challenge","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/dctimti24wzzkilpvwti.jpg","s":"The Cabela line has made major waves on the PC for years, and now it's ready to bring its unique brand of hunting to the PlayStation. Realistic hunting simulation is the aim, and CABELA'S BIG GAME HUNTER: Ultimate Challenge delivers on many levels. Two different career-oriented modes allow you to hunt for elk, deer, and other tricky prey over the course of numerous detailed levels. You'll have all of the maps, vehicles, and weaponry you could ever need in order to get the job done, and you'll need all of the help you can get The wilderness never seems to end in this expansive title. If you need some justification to kill animals, you can join forces with the Department of Natural Resources and help to keep the wolf and bear populations in check (just as brutal a process as deer hunting, mind you). There are adjustable skill settings to ensure that gamers of all walks get their money's worth, and you can even select an x-ray option that aids you in lining up your shots by showing you your prey's internal organs."},{"t":"Cabela's Ultimate Deer Hunt","p":["Windows"],"u":"Activision Value","g":"Sports","r":"September 3, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabela's_Ultimate_Deer_Hunt","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2eql.jpg"},{"t":"Candy Stripe: Minarai Tenshi","p":["Dreamcast"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candy_Stripe:_Minarai_Tenshi","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7ne3.jpg","s":"Candy Stripe: Minarai Tenshi is an dating sim adventure game for the Sega Dreamcast. The game takes place in the fictional Holy Angels Hospital. The player is a medical intern, and the game's objective is to establish personal relationships with one of three incoming nurses over a one-year period."},{"t":"Capcom vs. SNK 2","p":["Arcade","Dreamcast","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom, Sega","g":"Fighting","r":"August 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capcom_vs._SNK_2"},{"t":"Capitalism II","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Enlight","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Business simulation","r":"December 17, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capitalism_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2l61.jpg","s":"Capitalism II is a business simulation where players build and manage a corporate empire. The game covers key aspects of commerce, including manufacturing, marketing, research, and stock trading. Players make strategic decisions, hire executives, develop technology, and expand into industries like media and real estate. With a realistic market system, it offers a detailed and immersive corporate experience."},{"t":"Carnivores: Ice Age","p":["Windows"],"d":"Action Forms","u":"WizardWorks, Infogrames","g":"Sport, First-person shooter","r":"January 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carnivores:_Ice_Age","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2zh3.jpg","s":"Carnivores: Ice Age is a first-person shooter video game developed by Action Forms, and later ported to iOS and Android devices by Tatem Games. It is the third game in the Carnivores series. Unlike the previous games, in which the player must hunt dinosaurs, Carnivores: Ice Age allows the player to stalk large Cenozoic mammals such as mammoths and saber-toothed cats."},{"t":"Casanova: The Duel of the Black Rose","p":["Windows"],"d":"Arxel Tribe","u":"Wanadoo Edition","g":"Adventure","r":"June 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casanova:_The_Duel_of_the_Black_Rose","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cobdaw.jpg","s":"As the name implies, this game puts you in the role of a lady-charming, swashbuckling, sword-wielding rogue during the Enlightenment era. In a mix of action and adventure, you'll play the good-looking Giacomo Casanova in Venice, Italy. It is a story of love, ambition, political conflict and vengeance in an 18th century Mediterranean locale.\n\nWhile at sea, his ship is attacked by cutthroat pirates and his mother is murdered. The gameplay begins while Giacomo, the only survivor, is recuperating in Venice in the home of the Duke of Grimani. He vows to seek out the murderous pirates to avenge his mother's death. Soon he discovers that her murder may be part of a much deeper plot linked to a conspiracy to take over the country's current leadership.\n\nPlenty of women wander the streets of Venice who can be charmed by the handsome Casanova's wit and audacity. Conversation choices effect how others respond to you, and the wrong answer could get your face slapped instead of kissed.\n\nMany spontaneous sword fights happen to keep you busy and help you practice for the latter parts of the game. Other weapons, such as a crossbow, will be needed in some fights.\n\nThe game is controlled using both the keyboard and mouse. Walk, run, jump, and fence using keyboard keys; aim and fire the crossbow with your mouse. There are adventure-type puzzles to solve, many of which rely on conversation or inventory objects."},{"t":"Casino Tycoon","p":["Windows"],"d":"Cat Daddy Games","u":"Monte Cristo and Davilex Games","g":"Business simulation","r":"December 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casino_Tycoon_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co94bh.jpg"},{"t":"Castlevania Chronicles","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"October 8","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castlevania_Chronicles"},{"t":"Castlevania: Circle of the Moon","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"u":"Konami","r":"March 21, 2001"},{"t":"Castrol Honda VTR","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Interactive Entertainment Ltd., Bubble Boy","u":"Midas Interactive Entertainment, Success","g":"Racing","r":"February 9, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castrol_Honda_VTR","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cob50k.jpg","s":"The only way to experience the latest in Superbike technology, Castrol Honda VTR gives you control of the Castrol Honda World Superbike teams VTR 1000. Almost 1000 ccs of power are yours to control over a range of tournaments spanning 22 international tracks. The game features the new VTR 1000 SP-1 and the new Castrol Honda World Superbike Team Livery. There are 23 computer opponents all with full animated riders so lets see if you can become the next Castrol Honda World Champion."},{"t":"Catch the Sperm","p":["Windows"],"d":"Phenomedia AG CH","u":"Phenomedia AG CH, Federal Office of Public Health","g":"Edutainment, sports video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catch_the_Sperm","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8y4t.jpg","s":"Catch the Sperm is a promotional game to raise awareness for HIV. The game was developed for the Stop AIDS campaign in 2001 (a joint prevention campaign by the Swiss Federal Ministry of Health and Swiss Aids Federation) in Switzerland. The player has to protect a couple having sex by shooting condoms at sperm cells and HIV viruses to catch them. The condom gun is located at the right side of the screen and can be moved up and down with the mouse, while sperm cells and HIV viruses are entering the screen on the left side. The goal is to catch as many sperm cells and HIV viruses as possible within 90 seconds. The game is immediately lost if a HIV virus reaches the other side of the screen. For each catch the player gets points, while each shot costs points."},{"t":"Cel Damage","p":["Xbox","PlayStation 2","GameCube"],"r":"November 15","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cel_Damage"},{"t":"Championship Manager Quiz","p":["PlayStation","Windows"],"d":"King of the Jungle","u":"Eidos Interactive","g":"Sports","r":"November 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Championship_Manager_Quiz","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cobdfx.jpg","s":"Championship Manager quiz is the game from the popular franchise \"championship manager\" which instead of playing the math you answer questions to progress around the pitch. It features over 4,000 soccer related questions for any young or old soccer fan"},{"t":"Championship Manager: Season 01/02","p":["Windows","macOS","Xbox"],"d":"Sports Interactive","u":"Eidos Interactive","g":"Sport, Management","r":"October 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Championship_Manager:_Season_01/02","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2sd3.jpg","s":"Season 2001/2002 in the Championship Manager series. Players can choose to play as three of 26 leagues and access detailed profiles and histories for over 100,000 players, managers, and coaches. The full international simulation features all the major tournaments, as well as an improved Data Editor, allowing you to change and modify the stats. You can keep your teams squads up to date or even add yourself into the game. Championship Manager Season 01/02 also features enhanced Media involvement, more messages, and feedback."},{"t":"Chee-Chai Alien","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Creatures","u":"Creatures","g":"Action","r":"February 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chee-Chai_Alien","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9s7s.jpg","s":"Chee-Chai Alien is a Japanese only Game Boy Color game."},{"t":"Chemicus: Journey to the Other Side","p":["Windows"],"g":"Point-and-click, Puzzle, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemicus:_Journey_to_the_Other_Side","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cwma3jlz7yipuxaz0uft.jpg","s":"Journey to the other side of reality, to a virtual city shrouded in secrecy. Welcome to Chemicus, a land where scientific knowledge from the beginning of time is protected and wields sacred powers. You play as an accidental intruder transported to Chemicus by an ancient amulet. Your unintended presence has threatened the tranquility of this virtual city, and it's up to you--if you dare--to restore harmony before it's too late.\n\nIn Chemicus: Journey to the Other Side players gain knowledge and use their intelligence to solve challenging riddles and unlock the city's darkest secrets. Take up the quest for ancient science truths and discover powerful tools; explore science facts and gather essential resources; experiment with chemical elements and discover the ultimate power. Chemicus awaits your return with stunning 3-D movies, riveting animation, more than 2,000 3-D images, exotic landscapes, and mysterious interiors."},{"t":"ChuChu Rocket!","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"June 11","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ChuChu_Rocket!"},{"t":"City Crisis","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"July 17","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Crisis"},{"t":"Civilization III","p":["Windows"],"u":"Infogrames","r":"October 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civilization_III"},{"t":"Clive Barker's Undying","p":["Windows"],"r":"February 7","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clive_Barker%27s_Undying"},{"t":"Codename: Outbreak","p":["Windows"],"d":"GSC Game World","u":"Virgin Interactive","g":"First-person shooter","r":"October 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Codename:_Outbreak","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1mrk.jpg","s":"Venom. Codename: Outbreak is a tactical 3D-shooter set in the near future. Your aim is to oppose an alien invasion on Earth and protect the human race from extermination while evil parasites continue turning people into mind-killing beasts. Together with a teammate, you play the role of an ex-Ukranian combat group member - and your primary goal is to progress through 14 action-packed single-player missions or play via multiplayer with your friends over a LAN connection."},{"t":"Colobot","p":["Windows"],"g":"Strategy, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colobot","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3c1l.jpg","s":"Original mix of adventure and educational game. While you have fun with this game, you will also learn C-BOT, program language that is similar to all C- languages. C-BOT wouldn't allow you to write programs, but it will ease learning of C- languages."},{"t":"Columns Crown","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Wow Entertainment","u":"Sega, THQ","g":"Puzzle","r":"December 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columns_Crown","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coat52.jpg","s":"Princess Dazzle has searched all over for the missing Magic Gems that fill in the Columns Crown. You must help her recover them from a band of Forest Thieves by beating every puzzle to unlock the hidden Gems! Can you collect them all and complete the crown? Frantic multi-player action full of devastating special moves. Collect Magic Gems to unleash spectacular elemental attacks. New Survival, Versus, and Flash Columns modes."},{"t":"Comanche 4","p":["Windows"],"d":"NovaLogic","u":"NovaLogic","g":"Combat flight simulation","r":"November 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comanche_4","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1yam.jpg","s":"The best-selling Comanche series returns with this fast-paced action game that places you at the controls of a Comanche RAH-66 helicopter. In 6 single player campaigns and multiplayer, the game combines serious firepower with effortless controls and stunning, interactive terrain."},{"t":"Command & Conquer: Yuri's Revenge","p":["Windows"],"d":"Westwood Pacific","u":"EA Games","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"October 9, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_&_Conquer:_Yuri's_Revenge"},{"t":"Commander Keen","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"David A. Palmer Productions","u":"Activision","g":"Platform","r":"May 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commander_Keen_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4tph.jpg","s":"To the kids at school, you're 8-year-old Billy Blaze. To alien enemies in the galaxy you're superhero Commander Keen and it's up to you to save the Earth from destruction. As you put on your brother's football helmet you'll blast away on your Megarocket to space-worlds far away from home. Your mission: Face off against intergalactic creatures, recover the lost plasma crystals and save humanity -- it's all in a days work. Created by the game masterminds at id SoftwareTM, Commander Keen in the ultimate outer space blast-a-thon!"},{"t":"Conflict Zone","p":["Dreamcast","Windows"],"d":"MASA Group","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"June 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict_Zone","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coausb.jpg","s":"Conflict Zone is a modern-day war game with 3D graphics. Your every move is being watched by the media so you'll have to act right to make a good impression (rescue civilians,..). The impression you make will also determine how much money you will get for your missions, so there is no resource management."},{"t":"Conker's Bad Fur Day","p":["Nintendo 64"],"u":"Rare","r":"March 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conker%27s_Bad_Fur_Day"},{"t":"Conquest: Frontier Wars","p":["Windows"],"d":"Digital Anvil, Fever Pitch Studios","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"August 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conquest:_Frontier_Wars","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1yb8.jpg","s":"Mankind has finally learned how to explore the outer regions of space using wormhole technology, but Man is not alone in the Void. Two other races are also exploring and exploiting the cosmos for its resources and living space: the Mantis, an insect-like race whose technology relies on large numbers of quick fleets, and the Celaerans, beings of energy cursed by their creators to spend their lives in strange, armored canisters. Expand your fleet, explore your universe, and crush your opponents!\n\nConquest: Frontier Wars features large-scale fleet battles, intelligent fleet admirals who serve under your command as hero units, and pulse-pounding deep space RTS Sci-Fi gameplay! Manage your supply lines while waging war in multiple maps simultaneously. Don’t let your fleets collapse because as the old maxim states: your fleet is your fate."},{"t":"Cool Boarders","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"May 21","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cool_Boarders"},{"t":"Cosmic Rift","p":["Windows"],"u":"Sony Online Entertainment","g":"Action","r":"April 17, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmic_Rift"},{"t":"Cosmic Smash","p":["Arcade","Dreamcast"],"d":"Sega Rosso","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","r":"January 11, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmic_Smash","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4e7i.jpg","s":"Cosmic Smash is a heavily stylised sports game based on the game squash, filled with fast arcade thrills and stylish visuals."},{"t":"Cosmos no Sora ni","p":["Windows"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmos_no_Sora_ni","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cocks3.jpg","s":"Time of school festival. While most students are eager to show their spirits, main character who lost his memory in the childhood pays all his attention to five girls with scars on their hearts."},{"t":"CR Hissatsu Shigotonin Pachitte Chonmage @ VPACHI","p":["Dreamcast"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CR_Hissatsu_Shigotonin_Pachitte_Chonmage_@_VPACHI","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7ne8.jpg","s":"CR Hissatsu Shigotonin Pachitte Chonmage @VPACHI (CR必殺仕事人 パチってちょんまげ@VPACHI) is a pachinko game for the Sega Dreamcast. It is based on the television series Hissatsu Shigotonin."},{"t":"Crack Attack!","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crack_Attack!"},{"t":"Crash Bandicoot: The Wrath of Cortex","p":["PlayStation 2","Xbox","GameCube"],"r":"October 29"},{"t":"Crazy Arcade","p":["Windows"],"d":"Nexon Corporation","u":"NEXON Korea, Nexon America, Shanda, DigiCell - formerly","g":"Maze, strategy","r":"October 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crazy_Arcade","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2pt2.jpg","s":"Crazy Arcade or Boom Online has a gameplay similar to Bomberman of Hudson Soft. The aim of each game is carefully planting the bubble bomb and try to kill the opponent when the bomb explode."},{"t":"Crazy Taxi","p":["PlayStation 2","GameCube"],"r":"May 14","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crazy_Taxi_(video_game)"},{"t":"Crazy Taxi 2","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"May 28","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crazy_Taxi_2"},{"t":"Croc 2","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Natsume Co., Ltd.","u":"THQ","g":"Platformer","r":"January 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croc_2_(2001_video_game)"},{"t":"Cross Gate","p":["Windows"],"d":"Dwango","u":"Enix, Square Enix","g":"MMORPG","r":"July 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross_Gate","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7vl3.jpg","s":"CrossGate is a MMO RPG featuring diverse missions, jobs and could catch own pets."},{"t":"Cruis'n Velocity","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 28","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cruis%27n_Velocity"},{"t":"Cube","p":["Windows"],"d":"Wouter van Oortmerssen","g":"First-person shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cube_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coag0c.jpg","s":"Cube is a freeware 3D first-person shooter game. It can be played both in multiplayer and singleplayer mode. It allows the player to edit maps in-engine in full 3D, this means it is possible to fly around the map, point and drag to raise/lower blocks, create slopes etc. Multiple online players can even edit a map simultaneously."},{"t":"Cubix - Robots for Everyone: Race 'N Robots","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"November 13"},{"t":"Culdcept","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"OmiyaSoft","u":"Media Factory, Sega, Interchannel","g":"Board game, collectible card game","r":"July 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culdcept_(2001_video_game)"},{"t":"Cyber Troopers Virtual-On Force","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Sega AM3","u":"Sega","g":"Action","r":"October 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyber_Troopers_Virtual-On_Force","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9bf9.jpg","s":"Cyber Troopers Virtual-On Force is a 3D mecha fighting game developed by Sega AM3 (formerly Hitmaker) and published by Sega. It was released in Japanese arcades only on the Sega Hikaru arcade system board in 2001. Following its initial release, there was initially no home console port of the game due to the Hikaru's superior graphical capabilities.\n\nA region free home port of the game was released for Xbox 360 on December 22, 2010. The Xbox 360 version retains the same gameplay as the original series, only with a few inclusions: 2 on 2 Leader Battles, co-op boss fight mode, a Mission mode and Xbox Live support for online play. Battles have local 2 player splitscreen or up to 4 player over System Link. The game also has a special Collector's edition which includes a booklet called Virtual On Chronicle 15, that looks back at 15 years of Virtual-On, and a six disc soundtrack called Virtual On Official Sound Data. All of the items come packaged in a box with artwork from Hajime Katoki. Jaguarandi is now available as a hidden character, along with Apharmd the Hatter based on Apharmd J. However, Ajim, which was a playable character in Oratorio Tangram, is not playable in this Force version. The split-screen gameplay, which was absent from Xbox Live Arcade version of Oratorio Tangram, makes a return in this version. Preordering of the games allows access and download a special \"Thorax\" item that adjusts the bust sizes of both female Virtualoids: Fei-Yen and Angelan."},{"t":"D.A. Pursuit of Justice","p":["Windows"],"d":"Legacy Software","u":"Legacy Software","g":"Adventure","r":"October 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D.A._Pursuit_of_Justice"},{"t":"Dai Senryaku VII: Modern Military Tactics","p":["Windows"],"d":"SystemSoft","u":"SystemSoft, Kemco, Valcon Games","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"December 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dai_Senryaku_VII:_Modern_Military_Tactics"},{"t":"Dance Dance Revolution","p":["PlayStation"],"u":"Konami","r":"March 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dance_Dance_Revolution_(1998_video_game)"},{"t":"Dark Age of Camelot","p":["Windows"],"u":"Vivendi Universal Games","r":"October 10, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Age_of_Camelot"},{"t":"Dark Angel: Vampire Apocalypse","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"July 8"},{"t":"Dark Rift","p":["Nintendo 64"],"r":"December 17","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Rift"},{"t":"Dark Summit","p":["Xbox","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Radical Entertainment","u":"THQ","g":"Snowboarding","r":"November 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Summit","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2dwr.jpg","s":"Snowboarders have never felt welcome on Mt. Garrick, a once quiet and peaceful ski resort. Chief O' Leary, the resident ranger, has led the charge against boarders and limited their access to certain sections of the mountain. But the situation has progressed. Chief O'Leary has mysteriously closed the summit to all boarders and is trying desperately to drive them off the mountain."},{"t":"DarkSpace","p":["Windows"],"d":"Palestar","u":"Palestar, Got Game Entertainment","g":"MMO, Real-time strategy","r":"December 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DarkSpace"},{"t":"Dave Mirra Freestyle BMX 2","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"August 27","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dave_Mirra_Freestyle_BMX_2"},{"t":"Daytona USA","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"March 21","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daytona_USA_(video_game)"},{"t":"DDRMAX Dance Dance Revolution 6thMix","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami, Bemani","u":"Konami","g":"Music, exercise","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DDRMAX_Dance_Dance_Revolution_6thMix","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2vre.jpg","s":"DDRMax: Dance Dance Revolution 6thMix is the 6th game in the Dance Dance Revolution series of music video games. It was released in the arcades by Konami on October 19, 2001. Although only officially released in Japan, units exist worldwide. 6thMix contains a total of 42 songs, 36 of which are new to Dance Dance Revolution. It was the first in the series to feature Freeze Arrows. In the proto version of this game, it has some songs from DDR 1st-5th Mix.\n\nThe interface used is a recoloring and smoothing of the song wheel interface first introduced in Dance Dance Revolution 5thMix, with the addition of changeable sort settings and a longer time limit."},{"t":"Dead or Alive 3","p":["Xbox"],"r":"November 15","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dead_or_Alive_3"},{"t":"Deadly Dozen","p":["Windows"],"d":"Interactive","u":"Infogrames, Ziggurat Interactive","g":"Tactical shooter","r":"November 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadly_Dozen","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7nlo.jpg","s":"Infiltrate German occupied territory with your squad of military misfits. Destroy, steal, and kidnap your way out."},{"t":"Denki Blocks!","p":["Game Boy Color","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Denki, Covert Operations, Tiger Games","u":"Rage Games, Majesco, Global A Entertainment, Jakyl","g":"Puzzle","r":"October 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denki_Blocks!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6iqy.jpg","s":"The people of Puzzle Island love to play puzzle games. Their favorite is Denki Blocks! A game of skill that is played by sliding blocks around a board, joining colored blocks together to build shapes and solve puzzles."},{"t":"Derby Stallion 64","p":["Nintendo 64"],"d":"Parity Bit","u":"Media Factory","g":"Simulation","r":"August 10, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derby_Stallion_64","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co54uq.jpg","s":"The Derby Stallion (also known in Japan by the portmanteau abbreviation DerbyStal) video games are a series of genre-merging horse-racing and business simulation games originally created by ASCII Entertainment, and released by Nintendo. The series comprises 21 games, spans more than 10 console platforms, and is the best-selling horse racing series of all time with total sales topping more than 4 million in Japan."},{"t":"Desperados: Wanted Dead or Alive","p":["Windows"],"d":"Spellbound Entertainment","u":"Infogrames","g":"Real-time tactics","r":"April 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desperados:_Wanted_Dead_or_Alive","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co22xz.jpg","s":"Play six Desperados, lead by the hero John Cooper in the hardest and wildest Wild West Game of all times. Guide Cooper and his fearless companions in the exciting pursuit of the mysterious bandit leader El Diabolo.\n\nBreath-taking landscapes and a thrilling story create an unmatched Wild West atmosphere resulting in “Desperados – Wanted Dead or Alive” being a challenging team based real-time-adventure-strategy-game. The Wild West has never been this entertaining!\n\n6 different game characters, each of them having special abilities\n25 real time-strategy missions full of action\nAward-winning full motion videos with thrilling plots\nUnique sceneries including ghost towns, gold mines, pueblos, swamps, camps saloons, prisons and steamers\nThrilling gun fights, bank robberies, ambush missions, traps and breakneck pursuits\nSpectacular effects in the landscapes including thunderstorms, sun sets, caves, fires and waterfalls\n6 practice missions to get used to controlling the characters\nAltogether more than 30 different characteristics to be used by you skillfully in order to survive the levels and to escape"},{"t":"Devil May Cry","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Capcom","r":"August 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devil_May_Cry_(video_game)"},{"t":"Dexter's Laboratory: Deesaster Strikes!","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"September 26"},{"t":"Diablo II: Lord of Destruction","p":["Windows"],"r":"June 29"},{"t":"Dig Dug Deeper","p":["Windows"],"d":"Creature Labs","u":"Infogrames","g":"Puzzle","r":"November 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dig_Dug_Deeper","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coc9lw.jpg","s":"Dig Dug Deeper is a 3D remake of the original 1982 arcade game Dig Dug."},{"t":"Diggles: The Myth of Fenris","p":["Windows"],"d":"SEK GmbH","u":"Innonics, Strategy First","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"September 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diggles:_The_Myth_of_Fenris","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co23ru.jpg","s":"Godfather Odin has chosen the Diggles to set out on a long and burdensome expedition to the center of the earth where the hound of hell, Fenris, spreads terror. Fenris was once Odin's watchdog and ran away. Ever since he became aware of his growing power and strength he has been terrifying the underground world. Fenris must be disarmed and put on a chain. This task requires a very special chain, a chain that can only be forged by the Diggles...\n\nAccomplishing this dangerous mission will take decades and involved many generations of dwarves. During their journey the dwarves have to pass through 4 different worlds and more than 100 different quests have to be solved. The player has to arrange a daily schedule for the dwarves by determining how much they work and what they do in their leisure time. The player has to teach them how to forge weapons, cook mushrooms, raise hamsters, produce tools and invent machines. Like people in real life, the dwarves also want to have some spare time in order to sleep, eat, play cards, go bowling or to a pub. Male and female Diggles can even fall in love with each other and thus conceive their offspring, which ensures the survival of their clan and can transmit their learned attributes to their dwarf children. The player's most important goal is to prepare the Diggles for the crucial fight with Fenris."},{"t":"Digimon Battle Spirit","p":["WonderSwan Color"],"d":"Dimps","u":"Bandai","g":"Fighting","r":"October 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digimon_Battle_Spirit"},{"t":"Digimon Jintrix","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digimon_Jintrix"},{"t":"Digimon Park","p":["PlayStation"],"g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digimon_Park","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4o9e.jpg","s":"Digimon Park is an interactive game for children part of the Kids Station series.\n\nThe player can choose a digimon and make it evolve increasing his stats. The game also features a collection of mini-games."},{"t":"Digimon Rumble Arena","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Bandai","u":"Bandai","g":"Fighting","r":"December 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digimon_Rumble_Arena","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2zfm.jpg","s":"Digimon Rumble Arena is a crossover fighting video game developed and published by Bandai in association with Hudson Soft for the PlayStation video game console.\n\nPlayers are able to choose from up to 24 characters (fifteen of which need to be unlocked) from the Digimon Adventure and Digimon Tamers seasons of the Digimon anime, such as Terriermon, Agumon, Gatomon, and Renamon."},{"t":"Digimon Tamers: Brave Tamers","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digimon_Tamers:_Brave_Tamers"},{"t":"Digimon Tamers: Digimon Medley","p":["WonderSwan Color"],"g":"Role-playing (RPG)","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digimon_Tamers:_Digimon_Medley","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co852j.jpg","s":"Digimon Medley is based on the popular manga and and anime series Digimon, in which ordinary Japanese children find a way into a digital world, where they help good digital monsters fight against evil ones. The game is not directly plot-related to Digimon Adventure or Digimon Tamers: Brave Tamer games. Instead, it follows more closely the actual episodes of the series."},{"t":"Digimon Tamers: Pocket Culumon","p":["PlayStation"],"g":"Role-playing (RPG)","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digimon_Tamers:_Pocket_Culumon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5ssm.jpg","s":"In this final entry of the Pocket Digimon sub-series, you can interact with Culumon, that little Digimon from Digimon Tamers anime series. Unlike in the previous Pocket Digimon games, you can only get Culumon to your PocketStation. Also, Culumon can not evole at all."},{"t":"Dinosaur'us","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"RFX Interactive","u":"Light & Shadow Production, Electronic Arts","g":"Platform, role-playing","r":"February 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dinosaur'us","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co30dl.jpg","s":"Dinosaur'us is a game developed by RFX Interactive and co-published by Electronic Arts and Light & Shadow Productions for the Game Boy Color platform. Dinosaur'us was never released outside of Europe."},{"t":"Disney's Extremely Goofy Skateboarding","p":["Windows"],"d":"Krome Studios","u":"Disney Interactive","g":"Sports","r":"September 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disney's_Extremely_Goofy_Skateboarding","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2pxh.jpg","s":"Disney's Extremely Goofy Skateboarding is a skateboarding game with mechanics similar to the Tony Hawk's Pro Skater series, but aimed at younger players. Two Disney characters, Goofy and Max, can compete in 4 worlds with 3 levels each."},{"t":"Diva Starz: Mall Mania","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Digital Illusions Canada","u":"Vivendi Universal Interactive Publishing","g":"Action","r":"November 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diva_Starz:_Mall_Mania","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8dz4.jpg","s":"Diva Starz: Mall Mania is an Action game, developed by Digital Illusions and published by VU Games, which was released in 2001."},{"t":"Divided Ground: Middle East Conflict 1948–1973","p":["Windows"],"d":"TalonSoft","u":"Take-Two Interactive","g":"Computer wargame","r":"July 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divided_Ground:_Middle_East_Conflict_1948%E2%80%931973"},{"t":"Do You Like Horny Bunnies?","p":["Windows"],"d":"ZyX","u":"ZyX, G-Collections","g":"Eroge","r":"March 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Do_You_Like_Horny_Bunnies%3F"},{"t":"DoDonPachi II","p":["Arcade"],"d":"IGS","u":"Capcom, IGS","g":"Vertical scrolling shooter, Bullet hell","r":"May 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DoDonPachi_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9611.jpg"},{"t":"Dokapon: Monster Hunter","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 30"},{"t":"Dominions: Priests, Prophets and Pretenders","p":["Linux","Windows"],"d":"Illwinter Game Design","u":"Illwinter Game Design","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"July 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominions:_Priests,_Prophets_and_Pretenders"},{"t":"Doom","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 28","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_(1993_video_game)"},{"t":"Dr. Mario 64","p":["Nintendo 64"],"r":"April 8","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dr._Mario_64"},{"t":"Dracula: Crazy Vampire","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Planet Interactive Development","u":"DreamCatcher Interactive","g":"Action","r":"October 9, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dracula:_Crazy_Vampire","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3z1k.jpg","s":"Dracula: Crazy Vampire is an Action game, developed by Planet Interactive and published by DreamCatcher Interactive, which was released in 2001."},{"t":"Dragon Rage","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"The 3DO Company","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Shooter","r":"November 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_Rage","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/dh2c2g4ms3bj86ylxp97.jpg","s":"In a world of myth and magic, only one being stands between the powers of darkness and the extinction of an entire race. Orcs plague his kind and destroy their land. Only Cael Cyndar dares to challenge their power, swooping and soaring high above the land.\n\nFight for the freedom of all magical creatures and save your race the dragons from extinction. Play as Cael Cyndar and with him control the elements of fire, water, wind and earth. This game was originally to be called \"Dragon Wars of Might and Magic\"."},{"t":"Dragon Riders: Chronicles of Pern","p":["Dreamcast","Windows"],"d":"Ubi Studios UK","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Adventure","r":"August 8, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_Riders:_Chronicles_of_Pern","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6irp.jpg","s":"The Dragon Riders of Pern have protected their world from the alien oppressor Thread, but now they have all gone extinct. Unless you can find a new rider to take up, the world of Pern is doomed. From this point the game takes the player through four chapters with a vast storyline.\n\nDragon Riders is a game in 3D with strong RPG and action elements. The world of Pern is based upon the novels by Anne McCaffrey. The game contains over 120 locations on three continents. The player can interact and speak with over 170 characters."},{"t":"Dragon Warrior Monsters 2","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Tose","u":"Enix","g":"Role-playing","r":"March 9, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_Warrior_Monsters_2"},{"t":"Dragon Warrior VII","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"November 1","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon_Warrior_VII"},{"t":"Dragon's Lair","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Digital Eclipse","u":"Capcom USA","g":"Action","r":"February 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon's_Lair_(GBC_video_game)"},{"t":"Driven","p":["PlayStation 2","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"BAM! Studios Europe, Crawfish Interactive","u":"BAM! Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"November 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Driven_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6tto.jpg","s":"Driven is an overhead-view F1 racing game that combines realistic aspects of the sport with some outrageous elements inspired by the film. In the game's Story Mode, you take on the alternating roles of Joe Tanto and Jimmy Bly with the goal of completing 12 objectives taken from the movie."},{"t":"Druuna: Morbus Gravis","p":["Windows"],"d":"Artematica","u":"Microids","g":"Adventure game, survival horror","r":"September 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druuna:_Morbus_Gravis","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9tbi.jpg","s":"Condemned to an eternal vegetative state, the very sexy Druuna is waiting for someone to free her from her sick, claustrophobic and dilapidated world, inhabited by people suffering from a terrible virus that transforms them into bloodthirsty beasts. A symbiotic computer directly linked to Druuna's brain is the only thing that can help you to capture and to relive the situations that have reduced her to a state of \"cold sleep\". You must try to avoid the unscrupulous individuals that live in her mind and face the traps hidden along this uncertain road leading finally to salvation."},{"t":"Ducati World Racing Challenge","p":["PlayStation","Windows","Dreamcast"],"d":"Attention to Detail","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"January 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ducati_World_Racing_Challenge"},{"t":"Dynasty Warriors 3","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Omega Force","u":"Koei","g":"Hack and slash","r":"September 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynasty_Warriors_3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co45v2.jpg","s":"Dynasty Warriors 3 is the third installment of the Dynasty Warriors series in North America and the second within the Shin Sangoku Musou series in Japan. It is a spin-off from the video game Romance of the Three Kingdoms and based around a series of books by the same name, written by Luo Guanzhong.\n\nThe game contains a number of fictional and non-fictional elements from the same era in Chinese history that Romance of the Three Kingdoms is set. However, it follows the main events of part of the story which is to unify China under the single rule of a kingdom."},{"t":"E-racer","p":["Windows"],"d":"Rage Software","u":"Rage Software","g":"Racing","r":"April 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E-racer"},{"t":"E.T.: Digital Companion","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"October 18"},{"t":"E.T.: Interplanetary Mission","p":["PlayStation","Windows"],"d":"Santa Cruz Games","u":"NewKidCo, Ubi Soft","g":"Puzzle, action, platformer","r":"December 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E.T.:_Interplanetary_Mission"},{"t":"EA Sports F1 2001","p":["Windows","PlayStation 2","Xbox"],"d":"Image Space Incorporated, EA UK","u":"EA Sports","g":"Racing","r":"October 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EA_Sports_F1_2001","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3ap7.jpg","s":"Battle for position at every sweeping bend, high-speed straightaway, and hairpin turn within the world's most glamorous sproting arena. Engage in high stakes racing from Monaco to Monza and take on the most intelligent and daring racers to hit the Formula One circuit."},{"t":"Earth 2150: Lost Souls","p":["Windows"],"d":"TopWare Interactive","u":"Blackstar Interactive, Strategy First, Oxygen Interactive","g":"Strategy","r":"December 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth_2150:_Lost_Souls","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2e2s.jpg","s":"Take the completely 3-dimensional landscape into account while planning your troop movements: you'll need to scale towering mountains, dig out protective bulwarks and create entire tunnel systems. Explosions and detonations can change entire regions in an instant. \n \n“Lost Souls” sees the end of our beloved planet Earth. Devastating wars between the Eurasian Dynasty and the United Civilized States have wreaked havoc and caused mortal wounds to our planet, glaciers melted, the sea rose to a threatening level, and meteorite showers buried whole parts of the world. The only hope left for the population was the escape to the red planet Mars."},{"t":"Earthworm Jim","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"June 11","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earthworm_Jim_(video_game)"},{"t":"Echelon","p":["Windows"],"d":"MADia Entertainment","u":"Buka Entertainment, Bethesda Softworks","g":"Action, simulation","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Echelon_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co22po.jpg","s":"It's the year 2351, and you're a pilot trying to rise through the ranks all the way to glory. As a member of the Galactic Federation, you'll be granted more than 20 weapons to use in each pass-fail mission. Your goal is to defend the planet from attacking alien forces, and you'll put your skills into play across more than 30 campaigns. There's a huge list of aircraft for you to tame ranging from helicopters and planes to high-tech hovercraft. With 100 vehicles in all, deadly enemies, and 3D graphics, ECHELON is one far out trip through the solar system."},{"t":"Ecks vs. Sever","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 21","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecks_vs._Sever"},{"t":"Egypt Kids","p":["Windows"],"d":"EMG","u":"Cryo Kids","g":"Educational, adventure","r":"December 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egypt_Kids"},{"t":"Elite Forces WWII: Normandy","p":["Windows"],"d":"Third Law Interactive, 3LV Games","u":"ValuSoft","g":"First Person Shooter","r":"February 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elite_Forces_WWII:_Normandy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co92vn.jpg"},{"t":"Emperor: Battle for Dune","p":["Windows"],"d":"Intelligent Games, Westwood Studios","u":"EA Games","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"June 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor:_Battle_for_Dune","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co20ic.jpg","s":"Emperor: Battle for Dune represents the seamless combination of cutting-edge 3D graphics and perfected real-time strategy gameplay. In your conquest for the Imperial Throne, choose from 3 unique sides and 5 powerful subgroups to plot a perilous course through up to 5 treacherous gameworlds. In both singleplay and multiplay, lead your forces in both large-scale planetary strategies and cunning ground-level tactics. Westwood Studios is proud to have chosen the gripping universe of Dune for its first 3D real-time strategy title - Emperor:Battle for Dune."},{"t":"Empire Earth","p":["Windows"],"r":"November 12","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empire_Earth"},{"t":"Endonesia","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Vanpool","u":"Enix","g":"Adventure","r":"May 31, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endonesia","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2ykl.jpg","s":"In Endonesia, the player takes the role of a boy who went into the park and suddenly found himself in another world. A mysterious voice contacts him and says that this world is called Endonesia, and if he wants to return home, he has to call upon its fifty gods. In order to find the gods, the hero has to communicate with the inhabitants of Endnesia by transferring his own emotions to others.\n\nCreated by Taro Kudo and Kazuyuki Kurashima of Love-de-lic fame, Endnesia is one of the spiritual descendants (along with Giftpia and Chulip) of their seminal game Moon: Remix RPG Adventure. Like those other games, Endnesia is a puzzle-solving adventure with RPG elements such as statistics, item-gathering, and character growth.\n\nThe theme of the game is sharing emotions; gameplay-wise, it translates into puzzle-solving which involves using the right emotion on the right characters at the right time. For example, using the Hunger emotion on an animal will make it hungry, urging it to find food, which will clear the path for the hero.\n\nSimilarly to Moon and its descendants, Endnesia has a day-night cycle. The hero's health meter is depleted due to exhaustion; though the game's world is quite large, the player always has a time limit for his daily activities. The player should also feed the hero; hunger makes his vision blurry and slows down his pace."},{"t":"Erotica Island","p":["Windows"],"d":"Redfire Software","u":"Flare Media Limited","r":"March 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erotica_Island"},{"t":"Es","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7wg1.jpg","s":"Es is a realistic Japanese psycho-drama. The government is working on a project, attempting to extract a serial killer's memory and inject it into another person's mind. The player control such ambiguous mind made of two persons. The game is based on a Japanese TV show and has very little interactivity."},{"t":"ESPN Final Round Golf 2002","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"August 28","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ESPN_Final_Round_Golf_2002"},{"t":"ESPN National Hockey Night","p":["PlayStation 2","Game Boy Color"],"d":"Konami Computer Entertainment Osaka, Sandbox Studios","u":"Konami","g":"Sports","r":"March 8, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ESPN_National_Hockey_Night_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/fgtniu91ejplncsr5dtx.jpg","s":"Released in 2000, ESPN National Hockey Night was the last hockey game published by Konami and was released to generally negative reviews. 2000 Calder Trophy winner and Stanley Cup Champion Scott Gomez graced the cover."},{"t":"ESPN NFL PrimeTime 2002","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Farsight Technologies","u":"Konami of America","g":"Sports video game","r":"October 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ESPN_NFL_PrimeTime_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co965n.jpg","s":"Manage your team for up to 15 seasons as Chris Berman and Tom Jackson go \"Inside the Numbers\" to breakdown your games. Create free agents or edit existing players with an in-depth Player Editor. The game includes updated 2001 season rosters for all 31 NFL teams."},{"t":"ESPN X Games Skateboarding","p":["PlayStation 2","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Konami Computer Entertainment Osaka, Konami Honolulu Studio","u":"Konami","g":"Sports","r":"August 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ESPN_X_Games_Skateboarding","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3g93.jpg","s":"he X Games could be considered the Olympics for Generation X, and now one of the premier events skateboarding has made its way to the Game Boy Advance with ESPN X GAMES SKATEBOARDING. Choose from eight pro skaters Bob Burnquist, Carlos DeAndrade, Chad Fernandez, Kerry Getz, Rick McCrank, Colin McKay, Chris Senn, and Lincoln Ueda and 64 licensed decks as you get ready to shred on the streets, sidewalks, handrails, and more. There will be three cities to skate in San Francisco, New York, and Los Angeles each with location-specific elements and landmarks. The game consists of nine levels, broken into three categories: X Games competition, street skating, and fantasy. The 12 big-name bands including Linkin Park, Voodoo Glow Skulls, and New Found Glory will provide the adrenaline-pumping tunes to keep the action fast and furious. Get ready to ollie, grind, and kickflip your way to victory with ESPN X GAMES SKATEBOARDING."},{"t":"Eurofighter Typhoon","p":["Windows"],"d":"Digital Image Design","u":"Rage Software, Take-Two Interactive","g":"Flight simulation","r":"May 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurofighter_Typhoon_(video_game)"},{"t":"Europa Universalis II","p":["Windows"],"d":"Paradox Development Studio","u":"Strategy First, Vision Park, Ubi Soft","g":"Grand strategy","r":"November 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europa_Universalis_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2jr6.jpg","s":"Europa Universalis II invites you to a global struggle for supremacy from the dark times of Jeanne D'Arc to the flaming wars of Napoleon. As the leader of a country you have to guide domestic and foreign policies. Engage in religious struggles, set up expeditions to claim the New World, lead your country to prosperity and victory. Send your Privateers to roam the seven seas, muster mercenaries to bolster your defences, and send missionaries to convert infidels to your State Religion. Interact with true historical events and persons to determine which path your nation will take in the game. The new game includes an elaborate and expanded system for diplomacy, trade, military might and development. It also includes new graphics, music, sound, manual and a new tutorial system to lower the learning curve."},{"t":"European Super League","p":["Dreamcast","PlayStation","Windows"],"d":"Crimson, Coyote Developments","u":"Virgin Interactive Entertainment","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Super_League_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co40g5.jpg","s":"European Super League brings together the talent from the very finest teams in Europe to create a \"European Super League Championship\". Ronaldo, Zidane and Edgar Davids are names to excite the most fanatical football fan and they, along with countless other world class footballers, are the foundation of this game."},{"t":"Everblue","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Arika","u":"Capcom","g":"Action-adventure","r":"August 9, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Everblue","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1ouq.jpg","s":"Everblue is an adventure game based on scuba diving. The player takes the role of a scuba diver salvaging items from sunken ships and photographing underwater wildlife while either avoiding or confronting predators like sharks. The game also features an inventory system for collected objects, healing items, weapons, and tools, as well as an above water town with shops and NPCs."},{"t":"Evil Twin: Cyprien's Chronicles","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"December 7"},{"t":"Exhibition of Speed","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"Player 1","u":"Titus Interactive","g":"Racing","r":"May 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exhibition_of_Speed","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co40gx.jpg","s":"Looking for high-speed authentic arcade action? Strap yourself firmly in for this electrifying new racing game. Extensive game features include over twenty world-class racing vehicles including hidden cars, fifteen challenging tracks with varying weather conditions in five different universes, spectacular car jumps with additional boosters on the track, stunning visual SFX on the landscapes and multiplayer gaming for up to four players."},{"t":"Extermination","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"July 23","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extermination_(video_game)"},{"t":"Extreme-G 3","p":["GameCube"],"r":"November 27","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extreme-G_3"},{"t":"F-14 Tomcat","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Virtucraft","u":"Majesco","g":"Action game","r":"September 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-14_Tomcat_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6v6e.jpg","s":"F-14 Tomcat is a port of Absolute Entertainment's 1994 SNES game Turn and Burn: No-Fly Zone with some GBA exclusive content.\n\nIt features a multiplayer mode between other systems and a brand new storyline: the player controls a F-14 pilot chosen by the US government to be assigned in the theater of operations of a war between the republics of China and Taiwan to protect America's \"interests\" in the region."},{"t":"F-Zero: Maximum Velocity","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"June 11"},{"t":"F1 World Grand Prix 2000","p":["Windows","PlayStation"],"d":"Eutechnyx","u":"Video System","g":"Racing","r":"March 8, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_World_Grand_Prix_2000","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coa67t.jpg","s":"F1 World Grand Prix 2000 is a Formula 1 racing sim designed for true fans. Considerable attention has been devoted to realism and historical accuracy. The game features 22 real-life drivers from 11 F1 teams and the action takes place on detailed simulations of real tracks from around the world.\n\nThe game features a Challenge Mode that puts the player in special situations based on actual race events that have occurred to F1 drivers. The challenge may be for the player to catch up to the leader with only a few laps to go, or to block out opponents and help a teammate pull ahead. The two-player mode in F1 World Grand Prix 2000 allows head-to-head racing against a friend on a vertical or horizontal split screen."},{"t":"Fade","p":["Windows Mobile"],"d":"Fade Team","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fade_(video_game)"},{"t":"Fallout Tactics: Brotherhood of Steel","p":["Windows"],"d":"Micro Forté","u":"14 Degrees East","g":"Tactical role-playing","r":"March 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fallout_Tactics:_Brotherhood_of_Steel","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1ybr.jpg","s":"This is a Tactical Squad-Based Combat comes to the Fallout Universe. You are the wretched refuse. You may be born from dirt, but we will forge you into steel. You will learn to bend; if not you, will you break. In these dark times, the Brotherhood - your Brotherhood - is all that stands between the rekindled flame of civilization and the howling, radiated wasteland.\n\nYour weapons will become more than your tools, they will become your friends. You will use your skills to inspire the lowly and protect the weak... whether they like it or not. Your squadmates will be more dear to you than your kin and for those that survive there will be honor, respect and the spoils of war."},{"t":"Fantasy Westward Journey","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantasy_Westward_Journey"},{"t":"Fast Food Tycoon 2","p":["Windows"],"d":"Software 2000","u":"NA Activision Value Publishing, EU Software 2000, WW Assemble Entertainment","g":"Business simulation game","r":"February 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fast_Food_Tycoon_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/oktq1weie4losmxzfwrn.jpg","s":"Fast Food Tycoon 2 uses the concepts of its predecessor of managing a small pizza business and growing it into an empire, this time as an international chain."},{"t":"Fear Effect 2: Retro Helix","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Kronos Digital Entertainment","u":"Eidos Interactive","g":"Action-adventure","r":"February 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fear_Effect_2:_Retro_Helix","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2ppv.jpg","s":"Set in a crazed, alternate future of Hong Kong, Fear Effect 2: Retro Helix is the prequel to the survival-horror hit Fear Effect. You play as one of four deadly mercenaries on a team that's been commissioned to bring back the kidnapped daughter of a powerful Chinese executive. The action begins with Hana, Glas, and Deke the three main characters from the original game meeting each other for the first time. Soon afterward they meet Rain, a close friend of Hana's. Hana, Glas, and Deke are on separate missions to retrieve three objects, but before long they realize that they're involved in the same deadly plot. The action takes the foursome through Hong Kong, Hell's Kitchen in New York City, the walled city of Xi'an, the lost tomb of the first emperor of China, and in the end, to the mountain island of the immortals, Penglai Shan. Then, after lunch, things really get crazy!"},{"t":"FIFA Football 2002","p":["PlayStation","PlayStation 2"],"r":"November 1","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_Football_2002"},{"t":"Fighting Legends Online","p":["Windows"],"d":"Maximum Charisma Studios","u":"Maximum Charisma Studios","g":"Massively multiplayer online role-playing game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fighting_Legends_Online"},{"t":"Final Fantasy Chronicles","p":["PlayStation"],"u":"Square","r":"June 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_Chronicles"},{"t":"Final Fantasy IX","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"February 16","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_IX"},{"t":"Final Fantasy X","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Square","r":"July 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_X"},{"t":"Final Fight One","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"September 26","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fight_One"},{"t":"Fire Pro Wrestling","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Spike","u":"Spike, BAM! Entertainment","g":"Fighting, wrestling","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_Pro_Wrestling_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coatix.jpg","s":"Step into the Fire Pro Wrestling ring and experience blistering hot wrestling action that will blow you away! Extreme Wrestlers. Masked Luchadores. Martial Artists. Ultimate Fighters. They're all here and they all have one mission--win at any cost. Chose Slams. Flying Backbreakers. Pile Drivers. Skull Crushers. No move is too extreme and nothing is illegal unless you get caught!"},{"t":"Fire Pro Wrestling D","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Fighting, Simulator, Sport","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_Pro_Wrestling_D","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8iot.jpg","s":"The legend lives on in 2001 with a new member in the Fire Pro family. Arguably the most respected wrestling franchise in Japan, Fire Pro revives itself after Human's (the original publisher) demise. The new features include the availability of objects to use as weapons inside and outside of the ring, an online network option, and support for up to eight players simultaneously over the net."},{"t":"Floigan Bros.","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"Visual Concepts","u":"Sega","g":"Action-adventure","r":"July 31, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floigan_Bros.","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co40hu.jpg","s":"Large and lovable Moigle has a Secret Project that will keep the greedy Baron Malodorous and his gang of Mercenary Cats from taking over the Floigan Junkyard. Unfortunately, Moigle has lost the parts he needs to complete his project so he must enlist the help of his quick-witted older brother, Hoigle. Together, the Floigan Brothers play games and solve puzzles, all while defending their family turf!"},{"t":"Flower, Sun, and Rain","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Grasshopper Manufacture, h.a.n.d.","u":"Victor Interactive Software, Marvelous Entertainment, Rising Star Games, Marvelous Entertainment USA","g":"Adventure","r":"May 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower,_Sun,_and_Rain","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3hcu.jpg","s":"Flower, Sun, and Rain is set on the Micronesian island resort of Lospass, and is loosely connected to the events of The Silver Case. The story follows Sumio Mondo, a \"searcher\" who makes a living looking for things people have lost. Mondo is initially contracted to defuse a bomb planted on a plane, but is so distracted with helping the islanders that the bomb goes off. He ends up caught in a timeloop, where he relives the same day running up to the explosion. Gameplay focuses on Mondo exploring the island, solving numerical puzzles with the help of his computer \"Catherine\" to solve people's problems on Lospass."},{"t":"Fly! II","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fly!_II"},{"t":"Formula One 2001","p":["PlayStation","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Studio 33, Studio Liverpool","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"May 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Formula_One_2001_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9rlk.jpg","s":"Formula One 2001 is a racing video game for the PlayStation and PlayStation 2 developed by Studio 33 (PS1) and Sony Studio Liverpool (PS2) and published by Sony Computer Entertainment. It was released on 20 April 2001 in Europe, 24 September in North America and 11 October in Japan. It was the first game to support the Logitech racing wheel (a.k.a. GT Force) (PS2 only).\n\nFormula One 2001 was the last simulation Formula One game to appear on the PSone (as the PlayStation had become known by that point, to differentiate it from the PlayStation 2). It was also the last game in the series released in North America before the 2005 release of F1 Grand Prix for the PlayStation Portable."},{"t":"Fortress","p":[],"d":"CCR Inc","u":"Netsgo","r":"October 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortress_(2001_online_video_game)"},{"t":"Fortress","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Pipe Dream Interactive","u":"Majesco","g":"Tower defense","r":"August 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortress_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/ritxzbshkr1usyxd0uoi.jpg","s":"Fortress is a FAST-ACTION strategy game where players build an endless variety of unique structures and compete against each other in an all out war to the end! Hurl Strone Age rocks during the days of the dinosaurs, engage in cannon fire from the Golden Age of Piracy to Medieval Times, and energize plasma projectiles in the world of tomorrow. Populate your fortress with a variety of humorous Twerp characters as you learn to unlock the secrets of the Wizard's Tower! Play alone or in head-to-head mode using the Game Boy Advance Game Link cable feature."},{"t":"Frank Herbert's Dune","p":["Windows","PlayStation 2"],"r":"November 14","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Herbert%27s_Dune_(video_game)"},{"t":"Freddi Fish 5: The Case of the Creature of Coral Cove","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Humongous Entertainment","u":"Infogrames","g":"Adventure","r":"June 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freddi_Fish_5:_The_Case_of_the_Creature_of_Coral_Cove","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3zxo.jpg","s":"There's more to this case than meets the eye!"},{"t":"Frequency","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"November 20","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frequency"},{"t":"Frogger's Adventures: Temple of the Frog","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 24"},{"t":"Frogger: The Great Quest","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"November 20"},{"t":"From Dusk Till Dawn","p":["Windows"],"d":"GameSquad","u":"Cryo Interactive, DreamCatcher Interactive","g":"Third-person shooter","r":"October 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/From_Dusk_Till_Dawn_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2g3z.jpg","s":"A quasi sequel based on the 1996 horror action film 'From Dusk Till Dawn' by Robert Rodriguez and that takes place directly after the events of the first film. Made by French Developer Cryo."},{"t":"From TV Animation - One Piece: Grand Battle!","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Ganbarion","u":"Bandai","g":"Fighting","r":"March 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/From_TV_Animation_-_One_Piece:_Grand_Battle!"},{"t":"From TV Animation - One Piece: Set Sail Pirate Crew!","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Aim At Entertainment","u":"Bandai","g":"Role-Playing Game","r":"August 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/From_TV_Animation_-_One_Piece:_Set_Sail_Pirate_Crew!"},{"t":"Fuzion Frenzy","p":["Xbox"],"d":"Blitz Games","u":"Microsoft Game Studios","g":"Party","r":"November 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuzion_Frenzy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2a2f.jpg","s":"Fuzion Frenzy is a launch title for the Microsoft Xbox. At its core, Fuzion Frenzy is a four-player party game featuring 45 different mini-games (not including the titular Fuzion Frenzy). A demo of the game was included with some other launch titles in the US, including Halo, Munch's Oddysee, the first Project Gotham Racing game and Amped. The title was among the first to be released as a part of the Xbox Originals program.\n\nA sequel was later released for the Xbox 360, entitled Fuzion Frenzy 2."},{"t":"Gallop Racer 2001","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Tecmo","u":"Tecmo","g":"Alternative sports","r":"March 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gallop_Racer_2001","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4hmg.jpg","s":"Gallop Racer 2001 is a horse racing video game focused on simulating the jockey racing experience through a season mode. The game bases its challenges on knowing how your horse handles each race rather than pure driving. It contains breeding mechanics, a myriad of race types to try and jockey point management."},{"t":"Gals!","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gals!"},{"t":"Ganbare Goemon: Ōedo Daikaiten","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Konami Computer Entertainment Kobe, Now Production","u":"Konami","g":"Action-adventure","r":"March 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganbare_Goemon:_%C5%8Cedo_Daikaiten"},{"t":"Gangsters 2","p":["Windows"],"d":"Hothouse Creations","u":"Eidos Interactive, Trymedia Systems Inc.","g":"Strategy","r":"June 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gangsters_2"},{"t":"Geneforge","p":["macOS"],"d":"Spiderweb Software","u":"Spiderweb Software","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geneforge_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2174.jpg","s":"Geneforge is an Indie fantasy role-playing game, the first game in the five-part Geneforge Saga. It features an enormous world, the ability to create your own army of lethal, totally obedient creatures, and a cunning enemy AI with foes who can go on patrols, stalk you, and run for help.\n\nGeneforge has a huge and open storyline. You have the freedom to choose your own path and decide how the story will turn out. You can fight the evil overlord or you can join him. You can help the peasants or you can torment them. You can be the bold hero or you can just try to get away. No matter what you choose, Geneforge offers an enormous adventure with plenty of replay value."},{"t":"Gensō Suikoden Card Stories","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Will","u":"Konami","g":"Strategy","r":"September 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gens%C5%8D_Suikoden_Card_Stories"},{"t":"Giants: Citizen Kabuto","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"December 20"},{"t":"Giga Wing 2","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"May 16","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giga_Wing_2"},{"t":"Gilbert Goodmate and the Mushroom of Phungoria","p":["Windows"],"d":"Prelusion Games Inc.","u":"FastTrak Software Publishing Ltd.","g":"Adventure","r":"June 8, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilbert_Goodmate_and_the_Mushroom_of_Phungoria","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co25e0.jpg","s":"A 2001 point-and-click adventure game, re-released on Steam on June 23, 2015."},{"t":"Globetrotter 2","p":["Windows"],"d":"Deadline Games","u":"Vision Park, Mindscape","g":"Educational, quiz","r":"October 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Globetrotter_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4o8u.jpg","s":"In Globetrotter 2, the player returns as a backpacker, who travels all around the globe all the while answering questions about a variety of subjects."},{"t":"Goemon: Shin Sedai Shūmei!","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"KCET, Now Production","u":"Konami","g":"Action-adventure","r":"December 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goemon:_Shin_Sedai_Sh%C5%ABmei!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co56rz.jpg","s":"Goemon: Shin Sedai Shūmei! is a game in the Ganbare Goemon series released for the PlayStation on December 20, 2001 in Japan. It is a spin-off of the original Ganbare Goemon series.\nIn February 2002, a conversion of this game was released for the Game Boy Advance titled Goemon: New Age Shutsudō!, with various modifications and cuts made from the original PlayStation version.\n\nNeither version was released outside of Japan."},{"t":"Golden Sun","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"August 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Sun_(video_game)"},{"t":"Golf Resort Tycoon","p":["Windows"],"d":"Cat Daddy Games","u":"Activision Value","g":"Business simulation","r":"June 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golf_Resort_Tycoon"},{"t":"Golf Shiyouyo 2: Aratanaru Chousen","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golf_Shiyouyo_2:_Aratanaru_Chousen"},{"t":"Goofy's Fun House","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"The Code Monkeys","u":"NewKidCo, Ubi Soft","g":"Platform","r":"June 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goofy's_Fun_House","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8rgd.jpg","s":"Goofy must explore each a part of his home which consists of 15 distinct rooms. Each room includes elements such as a blender in the kitchen or table saw in the garage that the player can interact with. The main focus of play centers around collecting the 50 film canisters and certain objects Goofy has misplaced throughout his home. In addition to this, there are several mini-games that may be accessed via the paintings in the rec room of Goofy's house if the player finds all the objects that are needed. There's mini-games that range from golf and fishing to skiing and driving (based on How to play...) . After Goofy completes each mini-game and finds a few special items found somewhere in the house, a corresponding cartoon like: How to Play Golf will be unlocked. The game is considered \"Completed\" when the player completes all the mini-games, finds all of the cartoon-specific items, and collects at least 50 film canisters, unlocking the final cartoon."},{"t":"Gorasul: The Legacy of the Dragon","p":["Windows"],"d":"Silver Style","u":"JoWooD Productions","g":"Role-playing video game","r":"November 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gorasul:_The_Legacy_of_the_Dragon"},{"t":"Gothic","p":["Windows"],"g":"Action, RPG","r":"November 23","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gothic_(video_game)","i":"images/steam-65540.jpg","s":"War has been waged across the kingdom of Myrtana. Orcish hordes invaded human territory and the king of the land needed a lot of ore to forge enough weapons, should his army stand against this threat. Whoever breaks the law in these darkest of times is sentenced to serve in the giant penal colony of Khorinis, mining the so much needed..."},{"t":"Gradius Galaxies","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 12","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gradius_Galaxies"},{"t":"Gran Turismo 3: A-Spec","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","r":"April 28, 2001"},{"t":"Grand Theft Auto III","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Rockstar Games","r":"October 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Theft_Auto_III"},{"t":"Green Green","p":["Windows"],"d":"Groover","u":"Groover, WellMADE","g":"Eroge, Visual novel","r":"October 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Green_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co802q.jpg","s":"Green Green is an erotic visual novel developed and published by Groover that takes place in an isolated and nondescript boarding school known as Kanenone Gakuen that prides itself on the tradition of men growing amongst other men without any external influences to disturb them. Until one day, when the current administration decided to consider making the academy co-ed, and in order to see how the change would affect the student body, first and second-year girls were brought to live on school grounds for one month. While you, Yuusuke Takasaki, take the change in stride, your three hormone-driven friends see this as the true beginning of their youth."},{"t":"Growlanser II: The Sense of Justice","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Career Soft","u":"Atlus, Working Designs","g":"Tactical RPG","r":"July 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Growlanser_II:_The_Sense_of_Justice","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2wir.jpg","s":"Growlanser II: The Sense of Justice is a sequel to the first Growlanser; it is set in the same universe and timeline, featuring characters from the previous game. The story once again focuses on the war between the kingdoms of Rolandia and Burnstein; as opposed to the first game, the spotlight here is on the latter, as the player takes control of a young knight named Wein Cruz. Dreaming of becoming an Imperial Knight, the highest rank in his country, Wein gradually begins to see that not everything is the way it seems, and has to recount events from his own past, face injustice, and solve moral dilemmas concerning duty and friendship."},{"t":"Growlanser III: The Dual Darkness","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Career Soft","u":"Atlus, Working Designs","g":"Tactical RPG","r":"December 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Growlanser_III:_The_Dual_Darkness","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2wis.jpg","s":"Growlanser III: The Dual Darkness is the third installment to the popular series Growlanser created by Career Soft and published by Atlus, as well as one of the two games released in the United States in Growlanser Generations."},{"t":"GT Advance 2: Rally Racing","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"MTO","u":"MTO, THQ","g":"Racing","r":"December 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GT_Advance_2:_Rally_Racing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9bnn.jpg","s":"A rally racing game released for Game Boy Advance."},{"t":"GT Advance Championship Racing","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"MTO","u":"MTO, THQ","g":"Racing","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GT_Advance_Championship_Racing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9bnm.jpg","s":"Install your car kits, head to the track, start your engines and get ready to challenge the most competitive racers on the circuit! Choose from over 45 authentic cars and race for championship gold on an amazing 32 different tracks. Upgrade your car's performance using 9 categories of upgradeable parts including the engine, suspension, aerodynamics and more."},{"t":"Gundam Battle Online","p":["Dreamcast"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gundam_Battle_Online","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9dcv.jpg","s":"Gundam Battle Online is an simulation game based on the Gundam series."},{"t":"Gunfighter: The Legend of Jesse James","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Rebellion Developments","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Shooter game","r":"November 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunfighter:_The_Legend_of_Jesse_James","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/xuw2oxworntaqelwinbt.jpg","s":"Step into the boots of a legend of the Old West for a sharp-shooting adventure of true romance and frontier justice. Jesse James' partner has been shot dead and his best girl Zee has been kidnapped by a band of no-good outlaw varmints. Guide the rogue hero as he blasts away at the bad guys to avenge his lost companion and save the girl. The game is compatible with PlayStation light guns for a true first-person experience. Gunfighter: The Legend of Jesse James also features an assortment of mini-games for variety and replay value."},{"t":"Half-Life: Blue Shift","p":["Windows"],"d":"Gearbox Software","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"First-person shooter","r":"June 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Half-Life:_Blue_Shift","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co802z.jpg","s":"Half-Life: Blue Shift is an expansion pack for Valve Software's science fiction first-person shooter video game Half-Life. The game was developed by Gearbox Software with Valve Corporation and published by Sierra Entertainment on June 12, 2001. (It was originally set for release in Spring.) Blue Shift is the second expansion for Half-Life, originally intended as part of a Dreamcast version of the original game. Although the Dreamcast port was later cancelled, the PC version continued development and was released as a standalone product. The game was released on Steam on August 24, 2005."},{"t":"Half-Life: Decay","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Gearbox Software","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"First-person shooter","r":"November 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Half-Life:_Decay","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7ixz.jpg","s":"Half-Life: Decay is an expansion pack for Valve Software's science fiction, first-person shooter video game Half-Life. Developed by Gearbox Software and published by Sierra Entertainment, Decay was released as part of the PlayStation 2 version of Half-Life. It is the third expansion pack for Half-Life, and like its predecessors, Decay returns to the setting and timeline of the original story, albeit portraying the story from the viewpoint of a different set of protagonists: two scientists working in the Black Mesa Research Facility. Decay is a cooperative multiplayer game, designed to be played by two people working together to pass through the game's levels."},{"t":"Halo: Combat Evolved","p":["Xbox"],"u":"Microsoft Game Studios","r":"November 15, 2001"},{"t":"Hamster Monogatari 64","p":["Nintendo 64"],"d":"Culture Brain","u":"Culture Brain","g":"Life simulation game","r":"April 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamster_Monogatari_64","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6w3a.jpg","s":"Hamster Monogatari 64 is a life simulation game which the player raise and take care of a hamster which the hamster are placed in a cage. The player can give the hamster attention but if the player give the hamster lots of attention, the hamster will be determine what they become either a loveable one or a savage and maniac one. As the hamster grow up and evolve, they gain new abilities for the hamster competitions. Players can raise up to four hamsters which players can take care of them in a variety of ways."},{"t":"Hands of Time","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Mirage Media S.C.","u":"Titus Interactive SA","g":"Action","r":"October 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hands_of_Time_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cob4m6.jpg","s":"Hands of Time is an Action game, developed by Mirage and published by Titus Software, which was released in 2001."},{"t":"Happy Lesson","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happy_Lesson_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7nw7.jpg","s":"Game based on the popular anime Happy lesson."},{"t":"Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone","p":["Windows","PlayStation","Game Boy Color","Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 16","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harry_Potter_and_the_Philosopher%27s_Stone_(PC_video_game)"},{"t":"Harukanaru Toki no Naka de 2","p":["Windows"],"g":"Role-playing (RPG), Simulator, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harukanaru_Toki_no_Naka_de_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2fa8.jpg","s":"An otome dating sim."},{"t":"Harusame Youbi","p":["Dreamcast"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harusame_Youbi","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/c7obtu3jewycvfzh3itg.jpg","s":"Anime Dating RPG released in Japan"},{"t":"Harvest Moon 3 GBC","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"November 14","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvest_Moon_3_GBC"},{"t":"Harvest Moon: Save the Homeland","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Victor Interactive Software","u":"Victor Interactive Software, Natsume Inc.","g":"Life simulation, role-playing","r":"July 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvest_Moon:_Save_the_Homeland","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co26e3.jpg","s":"The first Harvest Moon game for the PS2 takes a little deviance from all the other games in the series. The goal this time around is to save your town from being turned into a resort next year. The game focuses more on relationships than making money - which is the goal in all other Harvest Moon games to date. You still are running a farm and must succeed in the life of a farmer, but the actual gameplay rewards those who interact with the townspeople over those who just work the fields."},{"t":"Hatena Satena","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Puzzle","r":"October 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hatena_Satena","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/gurgijsti4y1iy8y7juh.jpg","s":"Hatena Satena is a Puzzle game, developed by Hudson Soft and published by Hudson, which was released in Japan in 2001."},{"t":"Headhunter","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"November 16","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Headhunter_(video_game)"},{"t":"Heavy Metal: Geomatrix","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"September 13"},{"t":"Heroes of Might and Magic: Quest for the Dragon Bone Staff","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"New World Computing","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"April 17, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heroes_of_Might_and_Magic:_Quest_for_the_Dragon_Bone_Staff","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7lco.jpg","s":"Heroes of Might and Magic: Quest for the Dragon Bone Staff is a 2001 video game released on the PlayStation 2. Though 3DO did not advertise it as such, the game is an enhanced remake of King's Bounty."},{"t":"Hidden Invasion","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Toka","u":"Swing! Entertainment, Conspiracy Entertainment","g":"Action","r":"December 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hidden_Invasion","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9ec4.jpg","s":"Hidden Invasion is a action/adventure game for the PS2 and GameCube."},{"t":"High Heat Major League Baseball 2002","p":["Windows","PlayStation","PlayStation 2","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Team .366, Möbius Entertainment","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Sports","r":"March 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Heat_Major_League_Baseball_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6ppx.jpg","s":"High Heat Major League Baseball was a series of baseball computer games, released on PlayStation, Xbox, PlayStation 2 and PC. There were six annual versions of the game released, started with High Heat Baseball 1999, and ending with High Heat Major League Baseball 2004.\n\nThe game, featuring the official licensed team and player names from all 30 MLB teams, was created by games company 3DO which subsequently filed for bankruptcy in 2003 soon after the release of the final version of the series, High Heat Major League Baseball 2004. In August 2003, Microsoft purchased the rights to the High Heat franchise from 3DO, however, Microsoft has yet to develop a new title in the series.\n\nHigh Heat was traditionally known for possessing more simulation-style qualities than competitors World Series Baseball, All Star Baseball, or Triple Play Baseball, but frequently lagged behind in graphical quality. At the core of High Heat′s gameplay was its batter-pitcher interface, which was often hailed as the most realistic of its era."},{"t":"Himitsu: Yui ga Ita Natsu","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Himitsu:_Yui_ga_Ita_Natsu"},{"t":"Hornby software","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornby_software"},{"t":"Hoshigami: Ruining Blue Earth","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"MaxFive","u":"Atlus USA, MaxFive","g":"Tactical role-playing","r":"December 10, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hoshigami:_Ruining_Blue_Earth","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4eqb.jpg","s":"Utilizing a host of new gameplay elements, Hoshigami: Ruining Blue Earth expands upon the formula made popular by the cult classic Final Fantasy Tactics. The continent of Mardias serves as the game's primary world, and is divided into three kingdoms: Nightweld, the Valaimian Empire and Gerauld. The story begins as Valaimian troops advance into Nightweld, a peace-loving kingdom. Lacking the military might to repel the invaders from multiple fronts, Fazz - a mercenary and the game's protagonist - is hired to defend the Tower of Wind. Needless to say, this is just the beginning of a dire and perilous journey for the unwitting Fazz.\n\nAs you and your party trek across the world map, you'll take part in various battles. Ready-for-Action Points (RAP) determine which, as well as the number of actions that can be performed by a particular character. The more actions performed (and thus, more RAPs expended) during a turn, the longer you'll have to wait for your next chance to act. When attacking, an interactive gauge appears that does damage based upon where the slider is stopped. Stopping it at the very end will result in greater damage dealt to the enemy. Wait too long however and the slider will return to the beginning."},{"t":"Hostile Waters: Antaeus Rising","p":["Windows"],"g":"Strategy","r":"March 23","l":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/267980","i":"images/steam-267980.jpg","s":"In 2032 an Earth that knows only peace is forced to relearn the art of war. Twenty years have passed since the population of Earth did a fine job of destroying nearly everything in one final struggle for power by the politicians and military leaders."},{"t":"Hot Potato","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Pukka Games","u":"BAM! Entertainment","g":"Puzzle","r":"June 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Potato_(video_game)"},{"t":"Hot Wheels Extreme Racing","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Atod, HotGen","u":"THQ","g":"Racing","r":"September 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Wheels_Extreme_Racing"},{"t":"Hot Wheels: Burnin' Rubber","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Altron","u":"THQ, Altron","g":"Racing","r":"November 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Wheels:_Burnin'_Rubber","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co72mt.jpg","s":"Start your engines and race for the championship in your favorite Hot Wheels cars as they fly over jumps, careen around hairpin turns and speed through radical environments!"},{"t":"Hover Ace","p":["Windows"],"d":"GSC Game World","u":"Russobit-M","g":"Racing, Arcade","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hover_Ace","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4l41.jpg","s":"Hover Ace is a futuristic arcade racer with guns.\n\nPlayer takes in control one of five types of hovercrafts (Bike, Scout, Sport, Police and military) and participate in events on different planets (Earth, Venus, Mars and Planet of Glaciers). After the events, he receives money award, which could be spent on new weapons, hovercrafts, items or used to upgrade already installed ones."},{"t":"Hugo: Black Diamond Fever","p":["Windows","PlayStation","Game Boy Color","Mobile phone"],"d":"ITE Media, Kiloo ApS","u":"ITE Media, Kiloo ApS","g":"Platform game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugo:_Black_Diamond_Fever","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7r7c.jpg","s":"Hugo: Black Diamond Fever is a 3D platform game in the Hugo series that was developed by ITE Media and published by Electronic Arts in 2001 for the PC and PlayStation.\n\nThe evil Scylla is back and this time has found a way to make herself the most powerful witch of all time and take over the world. For this, she requires very rare black diamonds, which are to be found on the jungle island where the Kikurians live. She takes them all prisoner and forces them to work day and night in search of black diamonds. The elder King Kikurian asks Hugo the troll to liberate the slaves and stop Scylla's plot before it is too late. When Fernando arrives a letter from King Kikurian, Hugo reads it, but finds poor King Kikurian imprisoned in a cell and unable to help his people, and has absolutely no time to waste. Can Hugo free all the slaves and stop Scylla's plot?"},{"t":"Hundred Swords","p":["Windows","Dreamcast"],"d":"Smilebit","u":"Sega, Activision Value, Empire Interactive","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"February 8, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hundred_Swords","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2w79.jpg","s":"A Hundred Swords is a fantasy based strategy game with a medieval tone, developed by Smilebit initially for the Sega Dreamcast, and later released for the PC."},{"t":"Hunting Unlimited","p":["Windows"],"g":"Simulator, Sport","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunting_Unlimited","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3vs2.jpg","s":"Hunting Unlimited is an animal hunting game with a focus on action and hunting skill. It is the first in SCS Software's hunting series."},{"t":"Hyperbowl Plus! Edition","p":["Windows"],"d":"HyperEntertainment","u":"Microsoft","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperbowl_Plus!_Edition","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6r40.jpg","s":"HyperBowl Plus! Edition is a 3D bowling-style video game made in 2001 by HyperEntertainment. It was published by Microsoft, and could only be accessed by use of a Microsoft Plus! CD-ROM.\n\nThe version of HyperBowl included in Microsoft Plus! for Windows XP contains two of the six lanes initially available (Classic and Pins of Rome) and is designed for a single player. When the upgrade has been purchased and installed, four additional lanes are installed. Multiplayer support is also enabled, allowing up to four players to bowl and keep track of their scores during a game.\n\nHyperBowl is similar to a basic game of ten-pin bowling: the goal is to knock down as many of the ten pins as possible within thirty seconds. Unlike in normal bowling, the lane also contains obstacles, like moving vehicles in the Tokyo and San Francisco lanes and trees in the Yosemite lane. The player can use a mouse or trackball to guide the ball while it's moving in order to avoid obstacles and aim for the pins. A clock at the upper-right corner of the lane window displays the time left to knock down pins. The game displays players' scores in a similar way to the displays found in traditional bowling alleys. The game ends when all players have completed the ten frames."},{"t":"Hyperspace Delivery Boy!","p":["Windows Mobile","Windows","Linux"],"d":"Monkeystone Games","u":"Monkeystone Games, Linux Game Publishing","g":"Puzzle, action","r":"December 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperspace_Delivery_Boy!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co28p1.jpg","s":"You are Guy Carrington, lowly Courier-in-Training on the HDS Colby Jack. Your job: delivery mail and packages to the most dangerous parts of the galaxy! Can you make Courier 1st Class? Can you win the heart of your secret love, Chief Engineer Dolly? Can you face THIRTY levels of dangerous puzzles and insane inhabitants?"},{"t":"Icewind Dale: Heart of Winter","p":["Windows"],"d":"Black Isle Studios","u":"Interplay Entertainment","g":"Role-playing","r":"February 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icewind_Dale:_Heart_of_Winter"},{"t":"Ico","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","r":"September 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ico"},{"t":"Icy Tower","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Free Lunch Design","g":"Platform","r":"December 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icy_Tower","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co542w.jpg","s":"A combination of a platform and action game, the goal of the game character is to climb a tower by jumping onto steps of a staircase inside it. The game brings you custom characters, unlockable floors types, replays to share with your friends, lots of combo rewards and more!"},{"t":"Ikaruga","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Treasure","u":"Treasure, Entertainment Software Publishing, Infogrames, Nicalis","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"December 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ikaruga","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1qjb.jpg","s":"In this vertical scrolling spaceship shooter, you control a ship that can change its polarity at will. As blue/white, the ship can easily destroy red/black enemies, and vice versa. Bullets of your polarity can be collected to fuel your energy gauge, but those opposite will kill you. Destroying an enemy ship causes them to fire revenge bullets which can be absorbed and fired back by the player. Different difficulties exist to change exactly how you interact with polarized points, or how they appear.\n\nIt is the spiritual successor to Radiant Silvergun, featuring similar level and boss design, as well as it's buddhism inspired themes and aesthetics. Both are directed by Hiroshi Iuchi, who also composed Ikaruga's soundtrack."},{"t":"IL-2 Sturmovik","p":["Windows"],"u":"1C Company","r":"November 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IL-2_Sturmovik_(video_game)"},{"t":"Illbleed","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"April 16","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illbleed"},{"t":"Independence War 2: Edge of Chaos","p":["Windows"],"d":"Particle Systems","u":"Infogrames","g":"Space combat simulator","r":"June 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independence_War_2:_Edge_of_Chaos","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co24nz.jpg","s":"Command four space vessels and a band of revolutionary pirates into an all-consuming battle against impossible odds, because there just isn't enough Space for everyone!"},{"t":"Indiana Jones and the Infernal Machine","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"March 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indiana_Jones_and_the_Infernal_Machine"},{"t":"Inoue Ryouko: Last Scene","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inoue_Ryouko:_Last_Scene"},{"t":"Insaniquarium","p":[],"d":"Flying Bear Entertainment","u":"PopCap Games, MumboJumbo","g":"Puzzle, virtual pets","r":"August 31, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insaniquarium"},{"t":"Inspector Gadget: Advance Mission","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Magic Pockets","u":"Light & Shadow Production, DreamCatcher Interactive","g":"Action","r":"November 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inspector_Gadget:_Advance_Mission","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2d3w.jpg","s":"Inspector Gadget: Advance Mission is an Action game, developed by Magic Pockets and published by DreamCatcher Interactive, which was released in 2001."},{"t":"Inspector Gadget: Gadget's Crazy Maze","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Vision Media Engineering","u":"Ubi Soft, Light & Shadow Production","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inspector_Gadget:_Gadget's_Crazy_Maze","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8gj4.jpg","s":"Inspector Gadget: Gadget's Crazy Maze isn't the type of game one would expect when taking on the role of the bumbling, multi-functional detective of the 1980's cartoon. Rather than an action title like its Game Boy Color counterpart, this incarnation of Inspector Gadget is best described as a puzzle game with limited action elements.\n\nAccording to the story of Gadget's Crazy Maze, Inspector Gadget's mysterious arch-nemesis is up to no good once again. This time, the MADD ringleader Dr. Claw has been creating mind-control crystals on his secret moon base, then tossing them down to Earth. If the crystals lay dormant for a short amount of time, they will activate, taking control of the consciousness of the whole world. To make sure no one disturbs them, Dr. Claw has spread them all around four different areas and inserted his vile henchmen to protect them."},{"t":"Inu no Osanpo","p":["Arcade"],"d":"WOW Entertainment, Cave","u":"Sega","g":"Simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inu_no_Osanpo","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3t6g.jpg","s":"Players \"walk\" a virtual dog by walking on a treadmill and control the pet with a leash attached to a plastic dog in front of the player. Inu no Osanpo simulates the experience of walking a dog. Players first choose one of six breeds of dog to walk: Poodle, Pug, Pembroke Welsh Corgi, Shiba Inu, Labrador Retriever, or Siberian Husky. The difficulty of the game changes depending on the breed of dog chosen. After selecting a dog, the player selects one of three possible walking routes, each with a unique scenario. The goal of Inu no Osanpo is to maintain the dog's happiness long enough to complete the entire route."},{"t":"Inuyasha","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Bandai","u":"Bandai","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inuyasha_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co79pt.jpg","s":"The purpose is to defeat \"Naraku\" and collect the scattered \"Soul of the Four Souls\", but you can continue to collect \"Four Soul Fragments\" after clearing. There are also original movies only for games produced by Sunrise. A special \"Inuyasha notebook\" was added as a first-time benefit. Inuyasha becomes a human and weakens on the day of the new day (new moon) due to the age setting. After clearing the Maitreya, the air holes cannot be used."},{"t":"Invincible King Tri-Zenon","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invincible_King_Tri-Zenon"},{"t":"Iridion 3D","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Shin'en Multimedia","u":"Majesco, Interplay Entertainment","g":"Rail shooter","r":"June 11, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iridion_3D","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3geg.jpg","s":"In the distant regions of our galaxy, far beyond the darkest limits of the Orion Nebula, a deadly force has bee unleashed upon the universe and is heading straight for Earth. Get ready to travel forward to a time, when the fate of the universe rests in the palm of your hands! - See more at:"},{"t":"J.League Jikkyō Winning Eleven 2001","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Traditional soccer simulation","r":"June 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J.League_Jikky%C5%8D_Winning_Eleven_2001"},{"t":"Jackie Chan Adventures: Legend of the Dark Hand","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 7","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jackie_Chan_Adventures_(video_game)"},{"t":"Jade Cocoon 2","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Genki","u":"Genki, Ubi Soft","g":"Role-playing","r":"August 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jade_Cocoon_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2ni9.jpg","s":"Presenting a combination of monster breeding and RPG elements, Jade Cocoon 2 expands upon Genki's original title in a number of significant ways. Gameplay comprises a familiar mix of storytelling and monster raising, with significant tweaks implemented for good measure. The town acts as your base of operations and it's here you can stock up on supplies, or take jobs for which you can earn money and build a reputation. The latter proves vital as it's directly related to the number of monsters you can use in battle. The successful completion of certain tests rewards you with the ability to command more beasts -- up to a maximum of 12 at once, however, you can only take these tests when your reputation is high enough.\n\nThese trials take place in the Arena, which is also home to Beast Hunter battles, where you can test your mettle against fellow Beast Hunters. Monster eggs are found on your travels and can be hatched in the Room of Life; an all-encompassing monster management room that provides detailed information on the beasts and allows you to merge them, creating hybrid creatures. The monsters now also evolve over time. Your amulet consists of 12 slots, three for each of the four elements. Your creatures are placed in positions on the amulet corresponding to their position in battle, and during combat they'll attack the enemy and defend you from harm. Their amulet position also dictates the effectiveness of their elemental powers.\n\nThe storyline starts 100 years after the original adventure, with the Cocoon Masters having become the stuff of myth and legend. Beast Hunters are the modern equivalent, and players take on the role of Kahu; an aspirant Beast Hunter. A chance encounter results in Kahu being cursed, and he, along with a fairy named Nico, must rid his body of the curse or die. Levant (the original title's protagonist) is now a guardian and acts as your guide."},{"t":"Jak and Daxter: The Precursor Legacy","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","r":"December 3, 2001"},{"t":"James Bond 007: Agent Under Fire","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"EA Redwood Shores","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"First-person shooter","r":"November 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Bond_007:_Agent_Under_Fire","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co26dr.jpg","s":"James Bond 007: Agent Under Fire is the first James Bond game to appear on the PlayStation 2, GameCube, and Xbox. The game casts the player as the legendary James Bond who must accomplish missions throughout different areas of the world. Bond is sent on a mission rescue Zoe Nightshade, a CIA agent in the hands of Nigel Bloch. Nigel Bloch is the CEO of Identicon, a section of a highly successful industry company known as Malprave industries. Bond is also tasked to retrieve the set of DNA samples that are in Identicon's hands. So James Bond sets off on another adventure...\n\nThe game combines three game modes into one package: first-person-shooter, which is what most of the missions are, a rail-shooter, where the player only controls firing, and a driving game, where the player drives one of the many Bond cars. Numerous weapons have been added to the series, along with some fictional weapons like the Photon cannon and the 5-way grenade launcher. The player can utilize a wide array of gadgets and weapons, like the Q-Claw, Q-Decoder, and Bond's trusty Walther handgun. There is a large variety of missions, and Bond can go through most missions with weapons, or take a stealthy approach. The player is also rated in each level, based on score and difficulty level, and can unlock new features for both single and multiplayer."},{"t":"Jazz and Faust","p":["Windows"],"d":"Saturn Plus","u":"1C Company","g":"Adventure","r":"September 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz_and_Faust","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2d64.jpg","s":"Jazz and Faust is an epic adventure game where you can immerse yourself in the role of either seam captain Faust or a smuggler Jazz and embark on a quest for lost treasure and riches in a beautifully rendered 3D environment. Traveling through three ancient worlds, you will communicate with the various inhabitants, thus revealing the game's story objectives, quests, and puzzles."},{"t":"Jekyll and Hyde","p":["Windows"],"d":"In Utero","u":"DreamCatcher Interactive, Cryo Interactive","g":"Adventure","r":"August 31, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jekyll_and_Hyde_(video_game)"},{"t":"Jeremy McGrath Supercross World","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Acclaim Studios Salt Lake City","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"November 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeremy_McGrath_Supercross_World","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coatic.jpg","s":"Jeremy McGrath Supercross World is a Racing video game released in 2001 by Acclaim Entertainment. The game is endorsed by supercross star Jeremy McGrath and is the sequel to Jeremy McGrath Supercross 2000."},{"t":"Jimmy Neutron: Boy Genius","p":["Windows","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"AWE Games, BigSky Interactive, Human Soft","u":"THQ","g":"Adventure, platform","r":"November 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jimmy_Neutron:_Boy_Genius_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co49vy.jpg","s":"Initiate Contact with another galaxy and launch a makeshift satellite into space before breakfast. What in the world will the rest of the day bring Jimmy Neutron Boy Genius? It's up to you to find out!"},{"t":"Jimmy White's Cueball World","p":["Windows"],"d":"Awesome Developments","u":"Virgin Interactive","g":"Sports","r":"December 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jimmy_White's_Cueball_World","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4ywm.jpg"},{"t":"Jonny Moseley Mad Trix","p":["PlayStation 2","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"The 3DO Company","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Snowboarding","r":"December 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonny_Moseley_Mad_Trix"},{"t":"Judgement Silversword","p":["WonderSwan Color","Windows"],"d":"Qute","u":"Qute","g":"Vertical-scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judgement_Silversword","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8haj.jpg","s":"Judgement Silversword is a vertically scrolling shooter in which wave upon wave of multifarious of opposing craft will try to take you down with bullets, bombs, lasers, or even their own fuselages. Using the Y-Buttons as a directional pad and the X-Buttons for your weaponry you'll try your hand at completing the S-AIXAKs destiny.\n\nM-KAI released Judgement Silversword as a freeware demo on his personal website in 2001. The retail version was released by Qute on February 2, 2004 with the subtitle Rebirth Edition"},{"t":"Jumpgate: The Reconstruction Initiative","p":["Windows"],"d":"NetDevil","u":"3DO","g":"MMOG/Space Simulation","r":"September 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jumpgate:_The_Reconstruction_Initiative","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/xm7eztolxdsp4ixpx7fx.jpg","s":"Jumpgate: The Reconstruction Initiative is a space combat MMO in which players pilot spacecraft. Players accumulate wealth through mining, trade, and combat. Funds are used to purchase upgrades for their craft. The game divides space into sectors containing asteroids and space stations. Travel between sectors takes place via jumpgates. \n \n  PvP Combat \n  PvE Combat \n  Mining \n  Manufacturing \n  Trading \n  Persistant Universe encompassing over 150 sectors \n  3 playable factions \n  2 non-playable factions \n  Joystick support"},{"t":"JumpStart SpyMasters: Unmask the Prankster","p":["Windows"],"d":"Knowledge Adventure","u":"Knowledge Adventure","g":"Educational/adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JumpStart_SpyMasters:_Unmask_the_Prankster"},{"t":"Jurassic Park III: Danger Zone!","p":["Windows"],"d":"Knowledge Adventure","u":"Knowledge Adventure","g":"Action, Adventure","r":"July 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurassic_Park_III:_Danger_Zone!"},{"t":"Jurassic Park III: Dino Defender","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Knowledge Adventure","u":"Knowledge Adventure","g":"Action","r":"July 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurassic_Park_III:_Dino_Defender","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2ze8.jpg","s":"Jurassic Park III: Dino Defender is a game based on the Jurassic Park 3 franchise. You play a Dino Defender who must travel to Isla Sorna to capture the dinosaurs after a freak storm has knocked out the power supply and freed them. Your role is to use whatever is available to capture the dinosaurs, and discover what has happened. The game plays as a side scrolling platform action game, with the player fighting a range of dinosaurs."},{"t":"Jurassic Park III: Island Attack","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 26"},{"t":"Jurassic Park III: Park Builder","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"September 10"},{"t":"Jurassic Park III: The DNA Factor","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"July 16"},{"t":"Kabuki Warriors","p":["Xbox"],"d":"Genki, Lightweight","u":"Genki, Crave Entertainment","g":"Fighting","r":"November 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kabuki_Warriors","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5ua4.jpg","s":"As a fight promoter for a troop of Kabuki warriors trying to travel from Edo to Kyoto, your actor/samurai must possess both strength and style. Along the way you will have to fight other Kabuki troops, garner the crowds favor to receive tips, and trade warriors with your defeated counterparts. You will need a very accomplished group of samurai when you arrive in Kyoto to defeat the city's star troop."},{"t":"Kaen Seibo: The Virgin on Megiddo","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Adventure, Visual Novel","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaen_Seibo:_The_Virgin_on_Megiddo","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8hb2.jpg","s":"Kaen Seibo is a Japanese-style adventure which relies largely on conversations and the choices you make during them. However, the game also contains elements of Western-style adventuring. There are items that must be found, picked up and used at appropriate moments. There are areas where you can move through a 3D-like environment, viewing it from first-person perspective. During the dialogues, you also have commands that are accessible through a menu. There are also segments with a time limit."},{"t":"Kango Shicyauzo","p":["Windows"],"d":"Trabulance","u":"Trabulance, G-Collections","g":"Visual novel, eroge","r":"March 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kango_Shicyauzo"},{"t":"Kessen II","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"September 26","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kessen_II"},{"t":"Kinetica","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Santa Monica Studio","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"October 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinetica","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2z42.jpg","s":"They're called kinetic suits. They represent a total merging of man and machine with one goal in mind: speed. High speed. The \"drivers\" do more than operate their cycles -- they become cycles for insane velocity and maneuverability in this ultimate futuristic race. Besides acceleration and braking, the kinetic suits allow various antigravity tricks that can be performed for speed boosts, not unlike the system seen in SSX. At breakneck speed, there's no room for error; quick decisions and lightning reflexes are needed to weave your way through the tight tracks. Strap yourself in for one of the fastest rides you'll ever experience with your PS2!"},{"t":"King's Field IV","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"FromSoftware","u":"FromSoftware, Agetec, Metro3D","g":"Action role-playing","r":"October 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King's_Field_IV","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cob3up.jpg","s":"King's Field: The Ancient City, known in Japan as King's Field IV, is a medieval-themed first-person role playing video game produced by From Software for the PlayStation 2 in 2001. It is the fourth game in the King's Field series and the last one released for home consoles."},{"t":"Kingdom Under Fire: A War of Heroes","p":["Windows"],"d":"Phantagram","u":"Gathering of Developers","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"January 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_Under_Fire:_A_War_of_Heroes","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2c3s.jpg","s":"Kingdom Under Fire included single-player and multiplayer online modes through Phantagram's \"Wargate\" server. The game is the first release in the Kingdom Under Fire series which later received critical acclaim through the Xbox release Kingdom Under Fire: The Crusaders, a game which, like others in the series, incorporated both role-playing and real-time strategy elements. A \"Gold Patch\" was released for Kingdom Under Fire which introduced a map editor, extra missions, and in-game save option; the version was also re-released as Kingdom Under Fire Gold.\n\nTwo factions, light and dark, are playable in the game, with each side having units fulfilling the typical roles of warrior, archer, flying unit and wizards – as well as more powerful individual hero characters. The campaigns are split into 13 missions of which 10 are battles and the other three being a dungeon crawl for a hero character. Skirmish and multiplayer modes were also supported, with online games with human opponents organized via the Wargate.Net server."},{"t":"Kirby Tilt 'n' Tumble","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"April 11","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirby_Tilt_%27n%27_Tumble"},{"t":"Kirikou","p":["PlayStation","Game Boy Color","Windows"],"d":"Étranges Libellules, Planet Interactive, Krysalide","u":"Wanadoo Edition","g":"Platform","r":"November 9, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirikou_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5fsf.jpg","s":"Kirikou is a 2001 platform video game based upon the 1998 French film Kirikou and the Sorceress."},{"t":"Klonoa 2: Lunatea's Veil","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Namco","r":"March 22, 2001"},{"t":"Klonoa: Empire of Dreams","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"August 30"},{"t":"Klustar","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Rebellion Developments","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klustar"},{"t":"Kohan: Immortal Sovereigns","p":["Windows","Linux"],"d":"TimeGate Studios","u":"Strategy First, Ubi Soft","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"March 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kohan:_Immortal_Sovereigns","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co70cr.jpg","s":"A real-time strategy game set in a fantasy world where you take on the role of an immortal known as the Kohan. Your race once ruled the world but was devastated in a series of massive cataclysms. As an immortal, you have reawakened to a new world unlike the one you once knew. Now you must come to grips with your past glory, your present hardship, and your future destiny. If you are to return your race to their rightful glory, you must solve the mystery of their destruction."},{"t":"Konami Krazy Racers","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"June 11","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konami_Krazy_Racers"},{"t":"Koto Battle: Tengai no Moribito","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"AlphaDream","u":"AlphaDream","g":"Role-playing video game/Digital collectible card game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koto_Battle:_Tengai_no_Moribito","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co503z.jpg","s":"The first game developed by AlphaDream, it was released on the Game Boy Color in Japan only."},{"t":"Kuru Kuru Kururin","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Eighting","u":"Nintendo","g":"Puzzle","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuru_Kuru_Kururin","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co65dy.jpg","s":"The story begins when Kururin’s brothers and sisters go missing, and it is up to him to find them. Kururin is initially unsure that he is up to the task because he has never left his home world before. Being the adventurous and helpful fellow he is, Kururin agrees to rescue his lost family. Teacher Hare trains Kururin in the art of controlling the Helirin, a stick-shaped helicopter that has a slow-spinning propeller. Piloting the Helirin through the different worlds will be a difficult task, but using Teacher Hare's valuable lessons, Kururin bravely sets out on his adventure to rescue his lost family."},{"t":"Lady Sia","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 15","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Sia"},{"t":"Larry Ragland's 4x4 Challenge","p":["Windows"],"d":"Xpiral","u":"Xicat Interactive","g":"Racing","r":"November 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larry_Ragland's_4x4_Challenge","s":"Are you ready for high speed offroad competition in the only game endorsed by Larry Ragland, winner of 5 Baja 1000 races? Race on 8 different short tracks designed by offroad pros in 15 different high-powered 4x4's, for 3 different championships. The races are long, the competition is fast, and the outlook is grim. All you got is your skills and unerring faith in your machine. Are you ready for Larry Ragland's 4x4 Challenge?\n\nLarry Ragland's 4x4 Challenge (LRC from now on) is basically an arcade racer in a short-track offroad setting. Usually set inside a stadium or such, it features some unknown-make vehicles (no adjustments allowed) each with slightly different ratings in handlings, acceleration, and more. The courses are very reminiscent of the ones in LEADFOOT (by Ratbag Interactive).\n\nThe tracks are your typical supercross type-tracks... Lots of curves, jumps, and a few straights with switchbacks and more switchbacks. The tracks are different enough as some are longer with higher speed whereas some are low-speed with lots of switchbacks.\n\nYou get an arcade mode where you're supposed to keep up with the clock, or you can play the championship, where you race a field of 5 other AI drivers across a series of events on the various tracks. When you win one championship, you unlock the next one, which is longer and features a couple new tracks. There are a total of 3 championships across 8 different tracks, up to 6 laps each.\n\nThe vehicles in LRC are pretty tough, but they are NOT indestructible. This game actually models damage to your vehicle. There are four components you can damage: front tires/suspension, engine, transmission, and rear tires/suspension. The usual cause for damage is smacking into walls too hard, though hard collisions with another vehicle will do damage. Damaged cars don't run quite as fast and generates black smoke. The parts go from green to yellow to red, and if you're really bad... black.\n\nMultiplayer is only supported as 2-player split-screen, or 6-player LAN. Internet play is not supported."},{"t":"Leadfoot","p":["Windows"],"d":"Ratbag Games","u":"WizardWorks, Take-Two Interactive","g":"Racing","r":"May 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leadfoot_(video_game)"},{"t":"Legends of Might and Magic","p":["Windows"],"d":"New World Computing","u":"3DO","g":"Action, First-person shooter","r":"June 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legends_of_Might_and_Magic","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2gy8.jpg"},{"t":"Legends of Wrestling","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Acclaim Studios Salt Lake City","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"December 3, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legends_of_Wrestling","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4yv7.jpg","s":"Legends of Wrestling throws the greatest wrestlers into one game to find out who is the true king of kings. Wrestling superstars, including Hulk Hogan, Bret \"Hitman\" Hart, and Jerry \"The King\" Lawler, step back into the ring to deliver more body slams, piledrivers, and powerbombs to the competition. Play as authentic professional wrestlers or create your own legendary fighter, and then travel around the world to battle for wrestling's top titles. Legends of Wrestling also features customizable ring attire and new wrestler entrances."},{"t":"Lego Bionicle","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 3","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lego_Bionicle_(video_game)"},{"t":"Lego Bionicle: Quest for the Toa","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Saffire","u":"Lego Software","g":"Action-adventure","r":"October 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lego_Bionicle:_Quest_for_the_Toa","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3den.jpg","s":"LEGO Bionicle is an action-adventure platformer in an isometric perspective. The player controls Takua, a villager on the island of Mata Nui through a series of linear levels that take place in the 6 different biomes across the island. This is the only canon handheld Bionicle game, and seems to serve as a prelude to the Mata Nui Online Game and Mata Nui Online Game II."},{"t":"Lego Creator: Harry Potter","p":["Windows"],"g":"Simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lego_Creator:_Harry_Potter","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3iwu.jpg","s":"A LEGO Creator game based around the world of Harry Potter. It is tied into the Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone Lego sets."},{"t":"Lego Island 2 The Brickster's Revenge","p":["Windows","PlayStation","Game Boy Color","Game Boy Advance"],"r":"March 1","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lego_Island_2_The_Brickster%27s_Revenge"},{"t":"Lego Racers 2","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 27","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lego_Racers_2"},{"t":"Lotus Challenge","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Kuju Entertainment","u":"Virgin Interactive, Xicat Interactive, Sold-Out Software, Ignition Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"November 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lotus_Challenge","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9doz.jpg","s":"Racing game, featuring 37 cars licensed by England's prestigious manufacturer, from vintage roadsters to high-tech concept cars designed by Lotus Engineers exclusively for the game. Players compete on 15 race tracks, ranging from highly detailed real-world city locations to exhilarating custom circuits and speedways. The game features five modes. In addition to standard racing challenges, Lotus Challenge there is also an innovative Story mode, requiring the player to perform a variety of stunts, missions and challenges -- weaving through oncoming traffic to escape the paparazzi, hill-climbs on treacherous, snowy alpine tracks, being filmed jumping lines of school buses for adverts and many others. The GameCube version, previously referred to as Lotus Extreme, is based on the Motor Trend-licensed game of the same name on Xbox."},{"t":"Love Hina: Smile Again","p":["Dreamcast"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love_Hina:_Smile_Again","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/qspkhq00wiqlipsi3ouq.jpg","s":"Love Hina: Smile Again is an adventure game based on the Love Hina franchise for the Sega Dreamcast. The gameplay is very dialogue heavy, but it has a few mini-games and the storyline can be changed based on a luck system. You take control over Keitaro and talk with the girls in Hinata House."},{"t":"Lufia: The Legend Returns","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Neverland","u":"Taito, Natsume Inc., Ubi Soft","g":"Role-playing","r":"September 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lufia:_The_Legend_Returns","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2mkb.jpg","s":"The island of the Sinistrals, Doom Island, sank deep into the sea after the second Doom Island War. It was believed to be in an eternal sleep, but has once again begun silently beating, preparing to make its appearance in the sky for the third time. The evil Sinistrals have again been resurrected and released an unforseen terror on the land. A force has awaken witin you... a spiritual flow that draws you to a conflict that will save the world! The power of Maxim's legendary bloodline lives in you... The thread of destiny guides you through an adventurous journey looking for an unknown destination!"},{"t":"Luigi's Mansion","p":["GameCube"],"r":"November 18","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luigi%27s_Mansion"},{"t":"M&M's Blast!","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"December 1"},{"t":"Macross M3","p":["Dreamcast"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macross_M3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cocjbv.jpg","s":"Macross M3 is a Sega Dreamcast simulation game based on the Macross anime series."},{"t":"Mad Dash Racing","p":["Xbox"],"d":"Crystal Dynamics","u":"Eidos Interactive","g":"Racing","r":"November 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mad_Dash_Racing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4qm0.jpg","s":"Mad Dash Racing combines high-speed combat and 3D adventure action with a crew of motley characters for an insane multiplayer competition for up to four. Players select from nine different characters, each with their own unique personality and abilities, and compete in a race for survival against the evil Hex, his dirty minions and your unforgiving friends. Glide across rivers of hot, steaming lava, slip through underwater pipes, bash through mammoth boulders, and slide down icy glaciers to beat the other racers and escape Hex's wicked wrath.\n\nMusic by Moby, Fatboy Slim, Propellorheads & More!"},{"t":"Madden NFL 2002","p":["PlayStation 2","GameCube","Xbox","PlayStation","Game Boy Color"],"u":"EA Sports","r":"August 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madden_NFL_2002"},{"t":"Magi Nation","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Interactive Imagination","u":"Epoch Co.","g":"Role-playing","r":"March 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magi_Nation_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5ns4.jpg","s":"Based on the Magi Nation collectible trading card game.\n\nMagi Nation is a collectible card game developed and published by Interactive Imagination for the Gameboy Color.\n\nWhat would you do if you found yourself in a strange land, face-to-face with a mysterious presence and catapulted into an adventure to save an entire civilization? Well, find out! Explore a world of color and imagination that will challenge your every notion of reality. Experience the rush of dueling a Shadow Magi, with everything on the line. Find yourself going toe-to-toe with the awesome Timber Hyren, or the gargantuan Ormagon. Be the savior of an entire civilization. And find uses for jellybeans that you never thought possible."},{"t":"Magic & Mayhem: The Art of Magic","p":["Windows"],"d":"Charybdis Limited, Climax Studios","u":"Virgin Interactive, Bethesda Softworks","g":"Real-time strategy, role-playing","r":"October 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magic_&_Mayhem:_The_Art_of_Magic","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co53p3.jpg","s":"Magic & Mayhem: The Art of Magic is a 3D role-playing / strategy hybrid. You'll battle enemy creatures and wizards by conjuring forces and developing spells from the magic laws of Chaos, Law and Order. This unique magic system allows you to develop an individual strategy for each mission, altering the spells and creatures you use in battle each time. Summon fantastic creatures from mythology-giants, archers, undead minions, dragons, knights, and much more. Your character's skills and abilities develop as the game progresses through items found in scenarios and experience gained from successfully completing tasks. Robust multiplayer support allows gamers to battle head-to-head with up to eight friends or computer opponents over LAN or the Internet, including head-to-head and team play."},{"t":"Magic: The Gathering","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Strategy, Card & Board Game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magic:_The_Gathering_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2erv.jpg","s":"Magic: The Gathering is a Dreamcast game published and released by Sega in June 2001, though in Japan only. It takes place in the town of Magic Heart, the surrounding areas of Murg, Camat Island, Lydar Forest, Yeluk, Tornell, and The Balance Tower. It includes cards from 6th edition, Alliances, and Tempest. The game included 10 cards unique to it, generally utilizing random mechanics that would be difficult to implement in real-life card play."},{"t":"Magical Vacation","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"December 7","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magical_Vacation"},{"t":"Magna Carta: The Phantom of Avalanche","p":["Windows"],"d":"Softmax","u":"Softmax","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magna_Carta:_The_Phantom_of_Avalanche","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9jfv.jpg","s":"While it was never localized for western audiences, Magna Carta: The Phantom of Avalanche is notable for being the first entry in the Magna Carta series, developed and published by Softmax in Korea for Windows in 2001."},{"t":"Majestic","p":["Windows"],"d":"Anim-X","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Alternate reality game","r":"July 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Majestic_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1oob.jpg","s":"A subscription-based ARG that utilized AOL Instant Messenger. The servers were shut down on April 30, 2002."},{"t":"Mango Plumo","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Quebec Amerique","u":"QA International","g":"Edutainment","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mango_Plumo"},{"t":"Mario Family","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Natsume","u":"Jaguar International","g":"Utility","r":"August 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario_Family","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co21qz.jpg","s":"Mario Family is a piece of software for the Game Boy Color released only in Japan to be compatible with Jaguar's JN-100 sewing machine. The JN-100 came with a cable that would link it to a Game Boy Color, and in the game the player can choose one of several designs that the machine can copy onto fabric. This game is the second Mario sewing game after I Am a Teacher: Super Mario Sweater. It is also the last Mario game on the GBC."},{"t":"Mario Kart: Super Circuit","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"July 21, 2001"},{"t":"Mario Party 3","p":["Nintendo 64"],"r":"May 7","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario_Party_3"},{"t":"Mario Tennis","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"January 16","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario_Tennis"},{"t":"Mars Matrix: Hyper Solid Shooting","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"April 30"},{"t":"Mary-Kate and Ashley: Winners Circle","p":["Game Boy Color","PlayStation"],"d":"M4 Ltd, Tantalus Media","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Simulation","r":"February 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary-Kate_and_Ashley:_Winners_Circle","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co81sj.jpg","s":"MARY-KATE AND ASHLEY: Winners Circle for the Game Boy Color is a unique racing title that pits players against a plethora of obstacles while riding horseback. There are four different horses to choose from, each with a distinct look and style. Each horse has varying statistics for speed, jumping, and handling, requiring the player to make a careful choice to find the horse best-fitted to their racing style. Before riding you can also alter the clothing of the athlete to something of your choosing, then watch the changed sprite during gameplay. There are two modes of play: the first is a Training mode that lets you practice the controls and the course for the real competitions, and the second is the Steeplechase mode, where the real challenges lie. In this mode you must race through a course without being stopped by any of the many obstacles strewn about. MARY-KATE AND ASHLEY: Winners Circle is meant for young gamers, as the theme and graphics are sure to be loved by those looking for a light and exciting adventure."},{"t":"Mat Hoffman's Pro BMX","p":["PlayStation","Game Boy Color","Dreamcast","Windows","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Shaba Games","u":"Activision","g":"Sports","r":"May 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mat_Hoffman's_Pro_BMX","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3gfr.jpg","s":"Ride like 10-time World Vert Chamption Mat Hoffman or 7 other BMX pros. Pull off over 200 tricks and combo moves, including pro-signature moves. Shred through Street, Vert and Dirt courses with 4 different game modes to choose from."},{"t":"Mata Nui Online Game","p":["Browser"],"d":"Templar Studios","u":"The Lego Group","g":"Point-and-click adventure game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mata_Nui_Online_Game","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3krq.jpg","s":"A modular Flash point-and-click adventure game developed and updated throughout 2001 by Templar Studios LLC. for the LEGO Group, Mata Nui Online Game (or MNOG) is a game that brought the world of Bionicle to life."},{"t":"Matchbox: Emergency Patrol","p":["Windows","Game Boy Color"],"d":"Sandbox Studios, Lucky Chicken Games","u":"THQ","g":"Racing","r":"June 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matchbox:_Emergency_Patrol","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8t8f.jpg","s":"Who do you call when there's an emergency in Matchbox County? The Matchbox Emergency Patrol, that's who! Play as a firefighter, police officer, or paramedic in this high-energy challenge based on real-life crises. Whichever hero you choose, you'll receive word of a situation over your radio, hop in your designated vehicle, and navigate your way to the scene of the potential disaster. Of course, there's really no way to know exactly what the scene is going to be when you arrive, so you'd better be on your toes. You might find a robbery in progress, but it might just be two people having an argument, so use your best judgment as to how to handle the situation. Modeled after the Matchbox Emergency Patrol series of die-cast toy vehicles, MATCHBOX EMERGENCY PATROL (the game) is an in-depth adventure revolving around crimes, accidents, and medical emergencies. There are nine different missions for each of the three emergency workers, making it a very diverse game."},{"t":"Max Payne","p":["Windows","Xbox"],"u":"Gathering of Developers","r":"July 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Payne_(video_game)"},{"t":"Max Steel: Covert Missions","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"May 30"},{"t":"Mech Platoon","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Kemco","u":"Kemco","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"November 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mech_Platoon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3c5o.jpg","s":"The wise men say \"there is no end to the truth.\" In this war-town place, there are two things without end - the universe and greed.\nGlittering stars give the impression of eternal serenity, but the reality is that they hide a lie.\nAmong them blazes an endless war over limited resources; skirmishes that grow as the universe expands."},{"t":"MechCommander 2","p":["Windows"],"d":"FASA Interactive","u":"Microsoft","g":"Real-time tactics","r":"July 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MechCommander_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3hhk.jpg","s":"MechCommander Gold is the 1999 update to FASA Interactive's MechCommander, which contains both the original title's campaign and an entirely new 12-mission scenario called Desperate Measures, which incorporates several new 'Mech, weapons, and vehicles."},{"t":"Mega Man 64","p":["Nintendo 64"],"r":"January 10","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_Legends_(video_game)"},{"t":"Mega Man Battle Network","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 31","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_Battle_Network_(video_game)"},{"t":"Mega Man Legends","p":["Windows"],"r":"July 14","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_Legends_(video_game)"},{"t":"Mega Man X5","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"January 31","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_X5"},{"t":"Mega Man X6","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"December 4","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_X6"},{"t":"Mega Man Xtreme 2","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"October 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_Xtreme_2"},{"t":"Meine Liebe","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meine_Liebe"},{"t":"Memories Off 2nd","p":["Dreamcast","Windows","PlayStation"],"g":"Adventure, Visual Novel","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memories_Off_2nd","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cobv3l.jpg","s":"The 2001 sequel to Memories Off continues the interactive romance visual novel saga."},{"t":"Men in Black: The Series","p":["Game Boy Color","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Tiertex Design Studios, David A. Palmer Productions","u":"Crave Entertainment, Ubi Soft","g":"Action, platform","r":"September 11, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Men_in_Black:_The_Series_(video_game)"},{"t":"Men in Black: The Series – Crashdown","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Runecraft","u":"Infogrames","g":"First-person shooter, puzzle","r":"November 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Men_in_Black:_The_Series_%E2%80%93_Crashdown"},{"t":"Merchant Prince II","p":["Windows"],"d":"Holistic Design","u":"TalonSoft","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"April 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merchant_Prince_II"},{"t":"Metal Gear Solid 2: Sons of Liberty","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Konami","r":"November 13, 2001"},{"t":"Metal Walker","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"February 6","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Walker"},{"t":"Metropolismania","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Indi","u":"Media Factory, Natsume","g":"City-building","r":"September 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolismania","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2qos.jpg","s":"The best cities are built on friendship, not concrete! Everybody wants to live in the best city in the world! That's why you're a Metropolismaker, a well-connected urban planner who creates the most popular places on the planet. Of course com of your citizens have other ideas, so you'll have to work extra hard to keep everyone happy."},{"t":"Mia's Math Adventure: Just in Time!","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Kutoka Interactive","u":"Kutoka Interactive","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mia's_Math_Adventure:_Just_in_Time!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2umd.jpg","s":"Mia's Math Adventure: Just in Time! is the third title of the Mia's Big Adventure Collection software series created by Kutoka Interactive. Released in 2001 in Canada and the United States, the game teaches mathematics to children between 6 and 10 years old."},{"t":"Microsoft Flight Simulator 2002","p":["Windows"],"u":"Microsoft","r":"October 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Flight_Simulator_2002"},{"t":"Microsoft Train Simulator","p":["Windows"],"d":"Kuju Entertainment","u":"Microsoft Games","g":"Vehicle simulation","r":"June 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Train_Simulator","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/fyp96hwrjfe4j0o3yg32.jpg","s":"Microsoft Train Simulator (abbreviated to MSTS) is a train simulator for Microsoft Windows, released in July 2001 and developed by UK-based Kuju Entertainment."},{"t":"Miss Moonlight","p":["Dreamcast"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miss_Moonlight_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8e9d.jpg"},{"t":"Mission Control","p":["Acorn Archimedes","Windows","macOS"],"d":"Sherston Software","u":"Sherston Software","g":"platformer","r":"June 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mission_Control_(video_game)"},{"t":"Mizuiro","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mizuiro"},{"t":"MLB 2002","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"989 Sports","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment America","g":"Sports","r":"May 8, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MLB_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/ungdhd2mazswvcotdoeo.jpg","s":"Realistic baseball simulation is the name of the game in MLB 2002. Choose a team and lead your squad through a grueling, 162-game regular season, into the Playoffs, and straight to the World Series. All of the Major League teams, stadiums, and players are packed in here, with more than 300 motion-capture animations providing for extremely realistic play. They consulted over a dozen popular big leaguers in the construction of the game, yielding more than 250 signature moves and accurate player personalities. Take your pick from eight different styles of play, including Full Season, Playoffs, and a Franchise mode that casts you as the team's GM and lets you control all of the front office maneuvering. Hall of Fame announcer Vin Scully provides all of the play-by-play action, and Dave Campbell tags along doing color commentary. From the first pitch to the World Series-saving catch made in deep right field to close out the season, MLB 2002 is a complete baseball package."},{"t":"Mobile Golf","p":["Game Boy Color"],"g":"Role-playing (RPG), Sport","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_Golf","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3e8y.jpg","s":"A spin-off of the Mario Golf series. Much like the Game Boy Color version of Mario Golf, the player started out with four human characters."},{"t":"Mobile Suit Gundam: Federation vs. Zeon","p":["Arcade","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Banpresto, Bandai","g":"Action, Mecha simulation, Third-person shooter, Hack and slash, Air combat simulation and Naval combat simulation","r":"March 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_Suit_Gundam:_Federation_vs._Zeon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2ki6.jpg","s":"When the imperiled Earth Federation battles the notorious Zeon Forces, which side will you be on? Mobile Suit Gundam: Federation vs. Zeon forces you to choose which army to lead in furious outer-space combat. Play more than 200 missions solo or share the screen with a friend for head-to-head or team battle action. Actual actors from the TV series provide full voice-overs in the game, immersing you in Mobile Suit Gundam world."},{"t":"Mobility","p":["Windows","Linux"],"d":"GLAMUS GmbH","u":"GLAMUS GmbH","g":"City-building game","r":"May 9, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobility_(video_game)"},{"t":"Monkey Brains","p":["Windows"],"d":"Yobro productions","u":"Arush Entertainment","g":"Puzzle","r":"January 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkey_Brains"},{"t":"Monopoly Tycoon","p":["Windows"],"d":"Deep Red Games","u":"Infogrames","g":"Business simulation","r":"September 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monopoly_Tycoon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/vo9n47ibs3scb7ll52dq.jpg","s":"Build your fortune in a living, breathing Monopoly City(TM). Create a top property business and battle against your opponents to become the richest and biggest property tycoon around! Create multiple businesses in Monopoly City, from a humble bakers shop to an empire of superstores! Deal your way to the top by buying & selling, building & demolishing, starting businesses and selling them at their peak!"},{"t":"Monster Rancher 3","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"September 24","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monster_Rancher_3"},{"t":"Monster Rancher Advance","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Tecmo","u":"Tecmo","g":"Life simulation","r":"November 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monster_Rancher_Advance","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4dut.jpg","s":"Monster Rancher Advance is a monster-raising simulation that allows players to raise and customize 440 monster types. These monsters can then be tested in in-game tournaments, or made battle against other players's ones in two-player mode (by the GBA link cable).\n\nMonsters are created in the game's shrine through the entry of letters, numbers and punctuation symbols. Players begin with the ability to enter up to 4 characters to make monsters, and can unlock the ability to use up to 8 characters through gaining higher trainer ranks. Different words or combinations of characters can not only make different types of monsters, but also each monster can have varying attributes and characteristics, making each one unique.\n\nPlayers then train the monsters by choosing which foods to feed them with, which drills to do to raise attributes, what training to send them to for learning new attacks, and what items to give them. The game starts with a modest amount of money with which food and items can be purchased; more money is earned in tournaments. Gameplay revolves around planning training sessions, adding new attacks and juggling the bank account, along with attempting to win tournaments. As monsters age, they can be added to a coaching staff to help raise new monsters to better attributes, or they can be combined with other monsters to make stronger babies. Monsters do not die in this game, however they do face an enforced retirement age.\n\nThere is a storyline, where some characters from older Monster Rancher games are back, and new characters are introduced. The gameplay itself, like most other Monster Rancher games, is open-ended.\n\nThe two-player mode, requiring the link cable and two copies of the game, allows players to test the monsters they have raised against each other. It also allows players to trade items and cash money, to wager items or cash on tournaments, and trade raised monsters bringing them into each others' game files."},{"t":"Monsters, Inc. Scream Team","p":["Windows","PlayStation","PlayStation 2"],"r":"October 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monsters,_Inc._Scream_Team"},{"t":"Moon Tycoon","p":["Windows"],"d":"Legacy Interactive","u":"Anarchy Enterprises","g":"city-building game","r":"October 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon_Tycoon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/g3hdhb6sar68cfm5mraw.jpg","s":"Moon Tycoon is a city-building computer game released in 2001 by Anarchy Enterprises. It's based on the creation of a lunar colony. Moon Tycoon has three campaigns to play through, each with its own story-line, starting around the year 2021."},{"t":"Mortal Kombat Advance","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"December 12","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultimate_Mortal_Kombat_3"},{"t":"MOTAS","p":[],"d":"Jan Albartus","u":"Jan Albartus","g":"Escape the room, Puzzle","r":"November 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MOTAS"},{"t":"Moto Racer 3","p":["Windows"],"d":"Delphine Software International","u":"Electronic Arts, Infogrames","g":"Racing","r":"December 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moto_Racer_3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2whs.jpg","s":"Moto Racer 3 expands on the offerings found in Moto Racer 2, having more types of races including multiplayer online play."},{"t":"Motor City Online","p":["Windows"],"d":"EA Seattle","u":"EA Games","g":"MMOG/Racing","r":"October 31, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motor_City_Online","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7u2d.jpg","s":"In this online-only game, you buy classic cars, customize their performance to your liking, and race them against opponents around the world. The game was plagued with technical issues and met its end in August 29th 2003."},{"t":"Motor Mayhem","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Beyond Games","u":"Infogrames","g":"Vehicular combat","r":"July 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motor_Mayhem","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co85y5.jpg","s":"Motor Mayhem is a vehicle combat video game developed by Beyond Games"},{"t":"Mr. Driller G","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"November 22","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mr._Driller_G"},{"t":"Ms. Pac-Man: Quest for the Golden Maze","p":["Windows"],"d":"Creature Labs","u":"Infogrames","g":"Maze","r":"October 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ms._Pac-Man:_Quest_for_the_Golden_Maze","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3dnl.jpg","s":"A polygonal Ms. Pac-Man game for PC."},{"t":"MTV Music Generator 2","p":["PlayStation 2"],"g":"Music","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MTV_Music_Generator_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/pni8amhgu6dfn7wlw3mt.jpg","s":"Prepare for sound. Prepare for music. Prepare to generate. Whether you're a superstar DJ or just starting out, MTV Music Generator 2 is all you need to create killer tunes in minutes. From the makers of MTV Music Generator, it's music creation of PlayStation 2. It's easy. It's awesome."},{"t":"Mu Online","p":["Windows"],"d":"Webzen","u":"Webzen, ZhaoUC, Webzen Japan, Pinyou Technology","g":"Massively multiplayer online role-playing","r":"November 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mu_Online","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2mrn.jpg","s":"MU Online is a highly involved fantasy RPG based on the legendary Continent of MU.\nMU established a basic frame of various online games and other following games and regarded as a pioneer of 3D MMORPG."},{"t":"Multiplayer BattleTech 3025","p":[],"d":"Kesmai","g":"MMORPG","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplayer_BattleTech_3025"},{"t":"MX 2002 featuring Ricky Carmichael","p":["PlayStation 2","Xbox","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Pacific Coast Power & Light, Tiertex Design Studios","u":"THQ","g":"Sports, racing","r":"June 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MX_2002_featuring_Ricky_Carmichael","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6ti1.jpg","s":"Get your moto kicks on the newest, most advanced hand held system. Prepare for new levels of immersion, authenticity and compelling gameplay with MX 2002 featuring Ricky Carmichael. Featuring more than 20 of the most death defying tricks, 30 pro riders and fast intense competition, MX 2002 featuring Ricky Carmichael is a perfect blend of traditional racing and free ride madness. This isn't just a game - it's a complete motocross experience!"},{"t":"MX Rider","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Paradigm Entertainment","u":"Infogrames Europe","g":"Racing","r":"October 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MX_Rider","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/ptfqqoeainbtbsshxikx.jpg","s":"The complete motocross experience! Endless gameplay with 23 officially licensed motocross and supercross tracks and 6 fantasy freestyle arenas. Pull over 30 unreal tricks, some the pros are too sane to try. Race as one of 60 real life pro riders, each with their own unique attributes and racing styles. Get a team sponsorship and upgrade to a mind-blowing speed bike or the ultimate trick machine. Pull a quad can can over the shark tank (watch out, he bits) in one of 6 fantasy freestyle arenas."},{"t":"Myst III: Exile","p":["Windows","PlayStation 2"],"g":"Adventure, Casual","r":"May 8","l":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/925930","i":"images/steam-925930.jpg","s":"The story of Myst continues in this third entry of the series, originally released in 2001. Return to this strange and wondrous universe, where books become portals into worlds, and where the lessons of the past may yet hold meaning for today..."},{"t":"Myth III: The Wolf Age","p":["Windows"],"d":"MumboJumbo","u":"Take-Two Interactive, Gathering of Developers, MacSoft","g":"Real-time tactics","r":"October 31, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myth_III:_The_Wolf_Age","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co29od.jpg","s":"The third entry in the RTT-series, Myth: The Wolf Age is a prequel to the two previous games. Unlike the first two games, The Wolf Age was developed by Mumbo Jumbo."},{"t":"Nacah","p":["Windows"],"d":"Virtue Creations","u":"Bible Games Company","g":"Point-and-click adventure game, edutainment","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nacah","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co30y3.jpg"},{"t":"Namco Museum","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Mass Media","u":"Namco, Infogrames","g":"Various","r":"June 11, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Namco_Museum_(GBA_video_game)"},{"t":"Nancy Drew: Message in a Haunted Mansion","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 15"},{"t":"Nancy Drew: The Final Scene","p":["Windows"],"g":"Adventure","r":"November 1","l":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/615730","i":"images/steam-615730.jpg","s":"Search a Darkened Movie Theater to Free a Hostage from her Captor’s Dangerous Plot!"},{"t":"Nancy Drew: Treasure in the Royal Tower","p":["Windows"],"g":"Adventure","r":"August 1","l":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/615780","i":"images/steam-615780.jpg","s":"Follow Ancient Clues to Find Marie Antoinette’s Legendary Secret!"},{"t":"Napoleon","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Genki","u":"Nintendo","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleon_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6uy5.jpg","s":"Napoleon is real-time strategy game for the GameBoy Advance starring the historically famous commander. It was released in two countries: France and Japan.\n\nNapoleon is a Japanese Game Boy Advance launch title developed by Genki and published Nintendo. The game is a real-time strategy, a genre which rarely appeared on consoles at the time, let alone portable platforms. Instead of being an omnipotent being, however, Napoleon lets one play as the historical French leader himself as he rides on horseback to command his troops in battle. Contrary to what the name might imply, the game itself is not historically accurate, as its missions sometimes incorporate things such as monster summons which the player much take care of.\n\nNapoleon's gameplay simplifies a number of real-time strategy game conventions while adding some of its own unique traits. Resource gathering, for example, is not a part of the gameplay directly, although there are still limitations imposed on unit building. Troop morale is another factor which the player must take into consideration to ensure that Napoleon's lackeys are fighting optimally. Not only is this a factor in the actual battles themselves, where Napoleon can sometimes utter a war cry, but it's also a component of the game before and after battles, as the lower-ranked commanders Napoleon also brings into battle with him must be monitored for sufficient morale.\n\nAdditionally, Napoleon can only command his troops by going to each one individually, with the exception of the units which are grouped with another commander. In that case, simply telling the commander what to do is enough to get all of his attached units moving. He can also heal some units and also be healed by sub-commanders as well. Perhaps most importantly of all, though, is the fact that Napoleon's mobility is limited to how fast his horse can travel; any major parts of the battle he needs to attend to must be commuted to manually, instead of more instantaneous methods employed by most other real-time strategy games.\n\nThe title is romanized as \"Napoleon\" in Super Smash Bros. Ultimate."},{"t":"NASCAR Heat 2002","p":["PlayStation 2","Xbox"],"d":"Monster Games, Crawfish Interactive","u":"Infogrames","g":"Racing","r":"June 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASCAR_Heat_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7ydn.jpg","s":"NASCAR Heat Advance lets players strap themselves into a 750 hp stock car and enjoy all the thrills and spills of NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing anywhere. Whether it's the innovative Beat The Heat challenges or the all-new Career Mode, NASCAR Heat Advance promises to put the pedal to the metal and dust its competition. In fact, NASCAR Heat 2002 is the first NASCAR game for the hand-held system to provide race fans with the ability to see the entire car on the racetrack, not just the back of the car view that has been the only view available until now."},{"t":"NASCAR Racing 4","p":["Windows"],"u":"Sierra Entertainment","r":"February 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASCAR_Racing_4"},{"t":"NASCAR Thunder 2002","p":["PlayStation","PlayStation 2","Xbox"],"r":"October 1","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASCAR_Thunder_2002"},{"t":"NBA 2K2","p":["Dreamcast"],"u":"Sega","r":"October 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBA_2K2"},{"t":"NBA Hoopz","p":["PlayStation","PlayStation 2","Dreamcast","Game Boy Color"],"d":"Eurocom Entertainment Software, Torus Games","u":"Midway Home Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"February 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBA_Hoopz","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/gs5wpud4vvdv7pv1grwb.jpg","s":"Get ready for some seriously hot hoops action in the three-on-three court competition of NBA HOOPZ from Midway. In the grand tradition of NBA JAM and NFL BLITZ, arcade-style gameplay is the order of the day. Select your team of three NBA stars and try to outscore the computer or up to three of your friends in frantic basketball madness. If you sink three baskets in a row you even catch on fire! Or make three consecutive double-dunks or alley-oops and watch as your whole team ignites. A simplified control scheme (with only four buttons to learn) assists you as you shake up the court, performing death-defying skyhooks and stylish underhanded swooshes. When you tire of the pros, simply create your own player or team: start with an NBA player template and alter height, weight, and other physical features until you've got the ultimate arena warrior. There is also a slew of mini-games to master like 21, Three-Point Shootout, Around the World, and 2-ball, and you can catch all the most exciting game moments in slow-motion replays. The court is always ablaze with action in NBA HOOPZ for the Dreamcast."},{"t":"NBA Live 2002","p":["PlayStation 2","PlayStation","Xbox"],"d":": NuFX, : EA Canada","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Sports","r":"October 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBA_Live_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co397x.jpg","s":"NBA Live 2002 is the 2002 installment of the NBA Live video games series. The cover features Steve Francis as a member of the Houston Rockets. The game was developed by EA Sports and released on October 29, 2001 for the PlayStation 2, October 30, 2001 for the PlayStation and November 18, 2001 for the Xbox. This game was commentated by Don Poier and former NBA player Bob Elliot.[citation needed]"},{"t":"NBA ShootOut 2002","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Killer Game","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"September 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBA_ShootOut_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/trkfzv8lmawwjm0t5bwk.jpg","s":"If you love basketball (and you know you do), get ready for best thing since sliced bread. NBA SHOOTOUT 2002 takes the popular SHOOTOUT franchise to new heights, featuring the most realistic-looking action to date. The player models are created from a combination of photographs, motion-capture animation, and texture mapping which means the superstars and benchwarmers alike will appear just as they do in real-life. An updated game engine gives the play the feel of the NBA, and all of the new rules changes are in effect, too. The courts look like their real-life counterparts, with updates included for new floors like Seattle's. You choose your team, execute any desired trades, set your starting lineup, and pick through the playbook to create the most dominant squad in the league. High-flying dunks, touch shooting, and a sophisticated foul system make the NBA SHOOTOUT 2002 even more incredible than the beloved series installments that paved the way."},{"t":"NBA Street","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"EA Sports","r":"June 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBA_Street"},{"t":"NCAA College Football 2K2: Road to the Rose Bowl","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"Visual Concepts, Avalanche Software","u":"Sega of America","g":"Sports","r":"August 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA_College_Football_2K2:_Road_to_the_Rose_Bowl"},{"t":"NCAA Final Four 2002","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Killer Game","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"November 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA_Final_Four_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/knlme7je7bvkgx1yigwf.jpg","s":"The follow-up to Final Four 2001 features the obligatory roster updates with over 300 real NCAA teams and 31 conferences with authentic fight songs and 70 new player models to give the game a feel of realism. The standard modes apply such as Exhibition that supports both one-player or two-player and a Dynasty mode that lets the player compete in a full-season in order to secure a place in the 64-team tournament known as \"March Madness\". It also features the voice talents of Eddie Doucette and Billy Packer who provide play-by-play commentary."},{"t":"NCAA Football 2002","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"EA Sports","r":"July 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA_Football_(video_game_series)#NCAA_Football_2002"},{"t":"NCAA March Madness 2002","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"NuFX","u":"EA Sports","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NCAA_March_Madness_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/o5wbibwlbgp867xo5spz.jpg","s":"The 2002 entry in the college basketball franchise."},{"t":"Neon Genesis Evangelion: Ayanami Raising Project","p":["Windows","Dreamcast","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Gainax, Westone Bit Entertainment","u":"BROCCOLI, Gainax","g":"Life simulation, Raising simulation","r":"May 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neon_Genesis_Evangelion:_Ayanami_Raising_Project","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2wkn.jpg","s":"In Ayanami Raising Project, the player takes on the role of a NERV officer who has just been assigned to Tokyo-3. Upon meeting with Commander Gendo Ikari, he is charged with taking care of the First Child, the mysterious Evangelion pilot Rei Ayanami. It is up to the player to decide Ayanami's weekly schedule, balancing between education, duties at NERV, and leisure. The game spans approximately one year, and includes the events of the entire anime series, as well as the movie The End of Evangelion."},{"t":"Neppachi VI @VPACHI","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neppachi_VI_@VPACHI"},{"t":"Net Versus Chess","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_Versus_Chess"},{"t":"Net Versus Gomoku Narabe to Renju","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_Versus_Gomoku_Narabe_to_Renju"},{"t":"Net Versus Hanafuda","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Simulator, Turn-based strategy (TBS), Card & Board Game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_Versus_Hanafuda","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/flymn8xtjafungsj2ldv.jpg","s":"Net Versus Hanafuda is a version of the hanafuda card game for Dreamcast, with netplay."},{"t":"Net Versus Igo","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_Versus_Igo"},{"t":"Net Versus Mahjong","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_Versus_Mahjong"},{"t":"Net Versus Reversi","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_Versus_Reversi"},{"t":"Net Versus Shogi","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Simulator, Quiz/Trivia","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_Versus_Shogi","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7nvm.jpg","s":"Net Versus Shogi is a game for the Dreamcast."},{"t":"NFL 2K 2","p":["Dreamcast"],"u":"Sega","r":"September 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NFL_2K"},{"t":"NFL Fever 2002","p":["Xbox"],"d":"Microsoft Game Studios","u":"Microsoft Game Studios","g":"Sports","r":"November 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NFL_Fever_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/zoi218ugxkaxixbkkrks.jpg","s":"NFL Fever 2002 is an American Football video game published and developed by Microsoft Game Studios. It was originally released on November 15, 2001, as a launch title for the Xbox video game console. The game was followed by NFL Fever 2003"},{"t":"NFL GameDay 2002","p":["PlayStation","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Red Zone Interactive, 989 Sports","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"August 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NFL_GameDay_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8shs.jpg","s":"NFL GameDay 2002 is the seventh video game in the NFL GameDay series. It was developed by 989 Sports and Red Zone Interactive and published Sony Computer Entertainment America for the PlayStation and the PlayStation 2 in 2001. On the cover is Donovan McNabb."},{"t":"NFL QB Club 2002","p":["PlayStation 2","GameCube"],"d":"Acclaim Studios Austin","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"September 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NFL_QB_Club_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6ue6.jpg","s":"NFL QB Club 2002 is a football video game developed and published by Acclaim Entertainment under their Acclaim Sports banner. It is the final game in Acclaim's NFL Quarterback Club series. Some of the game's key features are:\n\nNFL Quarterback Challenge mode, featuring four head-to-head events: Speed and Mobility, Accuracy, Long Distance Throw and Read & Recognition.\nUnlock retired players like John Elway, Dan Marino, Steve Young, Jim Kelly and Phil Simms\n31 NFL clubs and over 1500 NFL players.\nPlay-by-play from Kevin Harlan and color commentary from Bill Maas.\nPlayer models feature blinking eyes, jaw motions, facial expressions and removable helmets.\nPlayer injuries, penalties and weather conditions affect season play and individual player performance.\n5 modes of play: Season, Exhibition, Challenge Mode, Playoffs, and Pro Bowl"},{"t":"NHL 2002","p":["PlayStation 2","Xbox"],"u":"EA Sports","r":"September 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NHL_2002"},{"t":"NHL Hitz 2002","p":["GameCube"],"r":"November 17","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NHL_Hitz_2002"},{"t":"Night of the Hermit","p":["Windows"],"g":"Point-and-click, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_of_the_Hermit","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co94ip.jpg"},{"t":"No Rules: Get Phat","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Flying Tiger Development","u":"TDK Mediactive","g":"Action, platform","r":"November 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No_Rules:_Get_Phat","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cobwlw.jpg","s":"One Eye Jack, a badass little skate fiend and leader of the No Rules posse, must stop an alien invasion of his local hangouts. To achieve his goal, he must board like a pro, be a dead-eye shot with his slingshot and alien slime gun, and grind the rails of social irresponsibility-all in the name of preserving his lifestyle."},{"t":"NYR: New York Race","p":["Game Boy Color","Windows","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Kalisto Entertainment","u":"Wanadoo Edition","g":"Racing game","r":"November 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NYR:_New_York_Race","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1oxu.jpg","s":"This is a science fiction racing game based on the film The Fifth Element directed by Luc Besson. Many of the characters from the film are playable, including characters that did not have a significant role in the film, and lacked names."},{"t":"O-Chan no Oekaki Logic","p":["PlayStation","Sega Saturn","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","WonderSwan"],"d":"Sunsoft","u":"Sunsoft","g":"Puzzle, Nonogram","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O-Chan_no_Oekaki_Logic","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4t68.jpg","s":"O-chan no Oekaki Logic is a Puzzle game, developed by Santaclaus and published by SunSoft, which was released in Japan in 2000."},{"t":"Oddworld: Munch's Oddysee","p":["Xbox"],"r":"November 15"},{"t":"Off-Road Redneck Racing","p":["Windows"],"d":"Rage Software","u":"Rage Software, Interplay Entertainment","g":"Racing video game","r":"March 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Off-Road_Redneck_Racing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7zck.jpg","s":"Yeehah! Stop yer grinnin' and drop yer linen, cause the slam bangin'est, mud-flappin'est off-road driving game ever created is comin' to Steam! Motor trucks 'n Buggies over the most brutal Southland terrain ever seen in a racing game."},{"t":"Okage: Shadow King","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Zener Works","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Role-playing","r":"March 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okage:_Shadow_King","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1oy0.jpg","s":"Conquer your demons and you may conquer the world in this distinctly styled role-playing adventure for the PlayStation 2. Some have compared the look of this game to Tim Burton's The Nightmare Before Christmas and the fluid, stop-motion appearance of the three-dimensional characters is not completely unlike that seen in seasonal television specials. OKAGE: Shadow King is not a holiday story, however, but a dark tale of polar opposites that questions basic notions of identity and essence.\n\nPlayers take the role of Ari, a young man with a remarkable affliction. A powerful demon -- calling himself \"Stan\" -- has taken refuge in Ari's shadow and is becoming infused in the boy's life. To free both his demon and himself, Ari embarks on a dangerous and involved quest to rid the world of seven other rival demons. Though he suffers under the burden of playing host to Stan, Ari is also aided from time to time by his demon's powers and the two begin to forge a symbiotic relationship."},{"t":"Ominous Horizons: A Paladin's Calling","p":["Windows"],"d":"N'Lightning Software Development","u":"N'Lightning Software Development","g":"First-person shooter","r":"September 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ominous_Horizons:_A_Paladin's_Calling","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9kwe.jpg","s":"It falls to you, a noble Paladin, to undertake the quest to retrieve the Gutenberg Bible, that was stolen, and to keep the darkness at bay."},{"t":"One Piece Mansion","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Puzzle, strategy","r":"June 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_Piece_Mansion","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2z1r.jpg","s":"A strategy game for the PlayStation unrelated to the popular anime and manga series One Piece."},{"t":"Oni","p":["Windows","PlayStation 2"],"r":"January 29","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oni_(video_game)"},{"t":"Onimusha: Warlords","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"March 13"},{"t":"Ooga Booga","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"September 13","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ooga_Booga"},{"t":"Open Kart","p":["Windows"],"d":"Microids","u":"Microids","g":"Racing, Simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_Kart","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cocd8k.jpg","s":"A serious simulation of real world go-kart racing"},{"t":"Operation Flashpoint: Cold War Crisis","p":["Windows"],"r":"June 22"},{"t":"Oregon Trail II","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"MECC","u":"SoftKey Multimedia","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_Trail_II"},{"t":"Original War","p":["Windows"],"d":"Altar Interactive","u":"Virgin Interactive","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"June 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Original_War","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co20k6.jpg","s":"An American geological expedition, under a UN mandate, assesses the natural resources in a distant part of Siberia, deep in the trackless steppes of Eastern Russia. But instead of oil, they discover a deposit of a hitherto unknown mineral.\n\nAmerican scientists working for Department of Defense find out that this mineral is in fact identical to the substance used to power an extraterrestrial artifact in their possession. This object is called the EON (Extraterrestrial Object Nexus).\n\nIt was found in 1919 by Colonel Emerson near the site of the Tunguska explosion. Emerson was the commander of a US military unit in charge of transporting US war supplies from the city of Vladivostok to the Eastern front during World War I.\n\nDoD Experiments have shown that items placed in the capsule are transported hundreds of thousands of years back in time. The American scientists were not able to understand its construction, nor could they manage to synthesize its fuel. Eventually, the machine - classified Top Secret - was stored, pending further advances in human science."},{"t":"Otto Matic","p":["macOS","Linux"],"d":"Pangea Software, Ideas From the Deep","u":"Aspyr Media, Ideas From the Deep, Pangea Software","g":"Action-adventure","r":"December 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_Matic","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2qwm.jpg","s":"Players take on the role of Otto Matic, a friendly robot, who must save humanity from the evil Brain Aliens of Planet X. Aliens have taken the humans to faraway planets, which Otto must travel to in order to rescue them."},{"t":"Outtrigger","p":["Arcade","Dreamcast"],"d":"Sega AM2","u":"Sega","g":"First-person shooter, Third-person shooter","r":"July 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outtrigger_(video_game)"},{"t":"P-O-X","p":["Handheld Electronic LCD"],"g":"Role-playing (RPG), Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P-O-X","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7vb2.jpg","s":"Create custom POX Infectors by combining heads, bodies and tails, program combat sequences and personalize them with their own names.\n\nExplore 17 levels of mazes, find out what your Infector can do and capture over 60 body parts.\n\nBattle wirelessly against other players."},{"t":"P.T.O. IV","p":["Windows"],"d":"Koei","u":"Koei","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P.T.O._IV"},{"t":"Pac-Man Collection","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Mass Media","u":"Namco","g":"Various","r":"July 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pac-Man_Collection"},{"t":"Pachi-Slot Teiou Dream Slot: Heiwa SP","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachi-Slot_Teiou_Dream_Slot:_Heiwa_SP"},{"t":"Pachi-Slot Teiou Dream Slot: Olympia SP","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachi-Slot_Teiou_Dream_Slot:_Olympia_SP"},{"t":"Pachinko no Dendou: CR Nanashi","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachinko_no_Dendou:_CR_Nanashi"},{"t":"PainStation","p":["Arcade"],"g":"Arcade","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PainStation","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3z5m.jpg","s":"Painstation is an art object and arcade game based on Pong developed by the artists' group, \"/////////fur//// art entertainment interfaces\", with pain feedback."},{"t":"Palette","p":["Windows"],"d":"Nishida Yoshitaka \"Yubiningyō\"","g":"Adventure game","r":"April 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palette_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3068.jpg","s":"Palette is a Japanese language freeware adventure game that was made with RPG Maker 95 by Nishida Yoshitaka. The game was highly acclaimed in the Fourth ASCII Entertainment Software Contest, awarded a Grand Prix of 10,000,000 yen, which resulted in remaking the game for PlayStation by Enterbrain. That version, entitled Forget me not -Palette-, saw the release on April 26, 2001 exclusively in Japan."},{"t":"Paper Mario","p":["Nintendo 64"],"r":"February 5","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paper_Mario"},{"t":"Paris-Dakar Rally","p":["Windows","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Broadsword Interactive","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"September 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris-Dakar_Rally_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2jfe.jpg","s":"Paris-Dakar Rally is a racing video game developed by Broadsword Interactive and published by Acclaim Entertainment for Microsoft Windows and PlayStation 2. It is based on the real-life Paris Dakar Rally – one of the world's most difficult and dangerous sporting events."},{"t":"Parkan: Iron Strategy","p":["Windows"],"g":"Shooter, Real Time Strategy (RTS), Simulator, Strategy, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parkan:_Iron_Strategy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3qud.jpg","s":"Parkan: Iron Strategy is a FPS-RTS spin-off to the Russian space sim series Parkan by Nikita."},{"t":"Party Time with Winnie the Pooh","p":["PlayStation","Windows"],"d":"Doki Denki","u":"Electronic Arts, Sony Computer Entertainment, Disney Interactive","g":"Party","r":"November 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Party_Time_with_Winnie_the_Pooh","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coa4ub.jpg","s":"Winnie the Pooh's Party Games: In Search of the Treasure is an adventure board game mixed with a compilation of exciting mini challenges that make it fun for a gathering of up to four friends. The game centers around a virtual board game that is simple fun and addicting. Each round starts a different mini-game and the results determine how each player advances on the main board."},{"t":"Passage: Path of Betrayal","p":["Windows"],"d":"DragonWorks Interactive","g":"Adventure","r":"July 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passage:_Path_of_Betrayal"},{"t":"Payback","p":["Amiga","macOS"],"d":"Apex Designs","u":"Apex Designs, DSI Games/Zoo Digital, Freeverse","g":"Action, Grand Theft Auto clone","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Payback_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2d0u.jpg","s":"If you'd always wondered what it would be like to be a master criminal, then Payback is the game for you. It's entirely non-linear - to complete a level you simply have to obtain a certain number of points - how you get those points is entirely up to you; you can do what the local kingpin tells you, uncover secret missions or just go on a mad killing spree.\n\nThis GBA action game takes inspirations from Rockstar/DMA's classic GTA series, but features unique gameplay elements, plus a visually stunning 3D GBA engine full of special effects and a smooth framerate."},{"t":"Pearl Harbor: Defend the Fleet","p":["Windows"],"d":"WayForward","u":"WizardWorks","g":"Shooter game","r":"May 11, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl_Harbor:_Defend_the_Fleet","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/nkrxrwlvwilxa4kkv5dh.jpg"},{"t":"Phantasy Star Online","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"January 29","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phantasy_Star_Online"},{"t":"Phantasy Star Online Version 2","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"September 24","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phantasy_Star_Online"},{"t":"Phoenix Wright: Ace Attorney","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 12"},{"t":"Pikmin","p":["GameCube"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"October 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikmin"},{"t":"Pillars of Garendall","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Beenox Studios","u":"Ambrosia Software","g":"Role-playing","r":"August 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pillars_of_Garendall","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cocpty.jpg","s":"A fantasy role-playing game set in the kingdom of Garendall, where the capital city, Gidolan Keep, is under attack by monsters.\n\nBattle monsters, train your character, earn gold, discover hidden items, and meet the kingdom’s inhabitants as you grow strong enough to defend Garendall. The game features 3D environments and 2D character sprites, giving its fantasy world a distinctive visual style."},{"t":"Pinobee: Wings of Adventure","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Artoon","u":"Hudson Soft, Activision","g":"Platform","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinobee:_Wings_of_Adventure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co48s1.jpg","s":"Pinobee: Wings of Adventure takes full advantage of Game Boy Advance's 32-bit color graphics. From Pinobee's fluid animation to the detailed environments he explores, it's obvious that a lot of spit and polish has been applied to this Pak. Pinobee screenshots may lead you to believe that this is a standard action platform game, but that is far from an accurate description.\nPinobee can walk along the ground or shimmy up walls, but his main means of locomotion is dashing through the air. As he makes it deeper in his quest, Pinobee gains the ability to dash farther and farther through the air. Soon enough, he's spending more time hovering and dashing through the sky than on the ground. This unique play control system gives Pinobee a fresh feel."},{"t":"Pipo Saru 2001","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Sony Computer Entertainment Japan","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Action","r":"July 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pipo_Saru_2001","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3tpk.jpg","s":"Pipo Saru 2001 (ピポサル2001 Pipo Saru Ni-sen Ichi) is an action video game in the Ape Escape series developed and published by SCEI for the PlayStation 2 video game console in Japan.\n\nA North American version was planned with the working title Ape Escape 2001, but it was canceled due to unknown reasons."},{"t":"Planet Hot Wheels","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Mattel, Hot Wheels","u":"Mattel","g":"Massively multiplayer online racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planet_Hot_Wheels"},{"t":"Planet Monsters","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Planet Interactive","u":"Titus","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planet_Monsters","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/fj23ct7g5wpxtyawb7qz.jpg","s":"There is only one rule with this game - Be the last one standing no matter what! Planet Monsters must confront themselves in eight unique countries each of 5 levels. Four monsters can play at once, so arm yourself with traps, blocks, jelly guns, grenades...and a great sense of humor!"},{"t":"Planet of the Apes","p":["Windows","Game Boy Color","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Visiware, Torus Games","u":"Ubi Soft, Fox Interactive","g":"Action-adventure","r":"September 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planet_of_the_Apes_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coajbj.jpg","s":"Fight for humanity as Ulysses, a deep-space astronaut shipwrecked on Earth nearly 2,000 years into the future. To his horror, a deadly breed of intelligent apes has evolved from mankind and now dominate the Earth. Hunted to near-extinction, the rag-tag human survivors huddle in fear of the Ape Empire.\n\nAgainst impossible odds, Ulysses must infiltrate the Simian society and halt the genocidal plan of Ape General Ursus to exterminate mankind forever. Familiar faces like Cornelius and Dr. Zaius - even new terrors like mandrill assassins and renegade baboons - await in this pulse-pounding tribute to the sc-fi cinema classic The future of the human race lies with one. It lies with you."},{"t":"Play Novel Silent Hill","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"KCE Tokyo, Will","u":"Konami","g":"Visual novel","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Play_Novel_Silent_Hill"},{"t":"Pocket Digimon World: Cool & Nature Battle Disc","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocket_Digimon_World:_Cool_&_Nature_Battle_Disc"},{"t":"Pocket Digimon World: Wind Battle Disc","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocket_Digimon_World:_Wind_Battle_Disc"},{"t":"Pocket Tanks","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Blitwise Productions","u":"Blitwise Productions","g":"Artillery","r":"October 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocket_Tanks","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coah6p.jpg","s":"The fastest game of artillery you'll ever play. Pocket Tanks is designed to be easy to learn, and fun to master. All the excitement of lobbing projectiles over a mound of dirt without all the complicated details found in most artillery games. Select your angle, power, and fire over 30 distinct weapons at your opponent. There is an innovative Weapon Shop to keep the game moving fast and a Target Practice mode for experimenting with all the weapons \"no holds barred!\""},{"t":"Pocky & Rocky with Becky","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Altron","u":"Altron, Natsume Inc.","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"October 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocky_&_Rocky_with_Becky","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6izu.jpg","s":"An evil Hydra Dragon has been awakened from a long sleep and has unleashed the world's most fearded mythical creatures on the land. It is up to you to stop them and eventually put an end to the evil Hydra's madness! You can choose one of the three characters, Pocky, Rocky the Raccoon or Becky to embark on this fantastic and dangerous adventure. Collect magical items and a variety of power-ups to help battle the mythical bosses as you journey to the final showdown with the Hydra!"},{"t":"Poke646","p":["Windows"],"d":"Poke646 team","g":"First-person shooter","r":"December 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poke646","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5iud.jpg","s":"Thirteen months after the Black Mesa incident, the research facility has been shut down. The nation never got to know anything about the experiments there, and the governement created a organisation specificly to handle \"Alien Related Topics\". The name of that organisation is Poke646.\n\nUnfortunately aliens are invading Nation City with portals, and Damien Reeves is the only Poke646 member who didn´t evacuate, due to an accident. Guided by a man named Dr. Sebastian Fuller through Poke646's radio transmitter, he must turn on 4 generators in the city to stop the portals from coming."},{"t":"Pokémon Card GB2","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pok%C3%A9mon_Card_GB2"},{"t":"Pokémon Crystal","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"July 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pok%C3%A9mon_Crystal"},{"t":"Pokémon Stadium 2","p":["Nintendo 64"],"r":"March 26","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pok%C3%A9mon_Stadium_2"},{"t":"Pool of Radiance: Ruins of Myth Drannor","p":["Windows"],"g":"RPG","r":"September 24","l":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/4834760","i":"images/steam-4834760.jpg","s":"Pool of Radiance: Ruins of Myth Drannor is a turn-based party RPG set in the Forgotten Realms. Build a band of heroes, descend into the haunted elven ruins of Myth Drannor, and use tactical positioning, spellcraft and steel to stop a dracolich from corrupting the realm."},{"t":"Portal Runner","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"September 10","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portal_Runner"},{"t":"Power Rangers Time Force","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"September 15","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Rangers_Time_Force"},{"t":"Primal Prey","p":["Windows"],"d":"Sunstorm Interactive","u":"ValuSoft, ARUSH Entertainment","g":"First-person shooter, Hunting simulation","r":"May 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primal_Prey","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co303b.jpg","s":"Primal Prey is a First Person hunting game similar to Deer Hunter that allows you to travel back to prehistoric times to hunt the ultimate trophies... dinosaurs."},{"t":"Priston Tale","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Priston_Tale"},{"t":"Pro Evolution Soccer","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Konami","r":"March 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pro_Evolution_Soccer_(video_game)"},{"t":"Progear","p":["Arcade","Mobile phone"],"d":"Cave","u":"Capcom","g":"Bullet hell","r":"April 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progear","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2oj8.jpg","s":"Progear is an arcade video game developed by CAVE and published by Capcom on the CPS-2 arcade system board in 2001. This was Cave's only horizontal scrolling shooter until Deathsmiles in 2007. The game features a steampunk-like style and art design. This game is the second of two CAVE shoot 'em ups to be released in America with the third CAVE NTSC-U/C release being Princess Debut, and in limited print; and as a result, the US version is somewhat rare.\n\nSometime in the past, the people of the country of Parts had found a way to become immortal, but only with elderly nobles. Five of these elders—Ballossum Pench, Gabriel Hammer, Jimchuck Spanner, Olsorro Slasher, and Leonard Drill—obtained immortality, and became known as the Motoruin sages. Eventually, they attempted to take over the Parts kingdom and start a new world order, collapsing the government and destroying parts of the country in the process. As their plans unfold, five children of one of these villages decide to battle the Motoruin using another new invention: the semi-automatic propelling engine, known as \"Progear\".\n\nAt the start of the game, the player is given a choice between two pilots/planes and three gunners, which determines the effectiveness of their respective modes. Each character's costume can be changed by holding the start button at the selection screen."},{"t":"Project Eden","p":["Windows","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Core Design","u":"Eidos Interactive","g":"Action-adventure","r":"October 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Eden_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1q73.jpg","s":"Due to severe overpopulation, the planet Earth’s diameter is increasing with the growth of towering mega-cities, each inhabited by millions of humans. Buildings are growing ever-upwards, interconnected with a thick web of roads, walkways and service channels. In these giant human hives, only the affluent may feel the rays of the sun and breathe the cleaner air. Your team has been selected to go down and investigate problems at the Real Meat factory, in which all of the equipment has started to simultaneously malfunction. Technicians sent to repair the machinery have gone missing without explanation. The UPA's (Urban Protection Agency) orders are clear: prepare for the descent.\n\nProject Eden is a 3D shooter with an emphasis on puzzle-solving. You'll lead a squad consisting of four UPA agents: Carter Dorlan, Andre Herderman, Minoko Molensky, and Amber Torrelson. Each operative has his or her own special ability that you'll need to master in order to progress. As Carter, use your knack for interrogation and nimble fingers to gain information and unlock doors. When you need something fixed and in a hurry, Andre is your man. Computer terminals are no match for Minoko's hacking talents. Last but not least, Amber can traverse hazardous environments with nary a scratch and she'll be your weapons expert. Brave the lowest strata and prepare to go through hell in Project Eden! If you're looking for some gritty cyberpunk-ish storytelling, challenging puzzles, and original gameplay (with optional drop-in, drop-out co-op), this is your game!"},{"t":"Project Gotham Racing","p":["Xbox"],"r":"November 9","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Gotham_Racing_(video_game)"},{"t":"Project Justice","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"May 16","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Justice"},{"t":"Project S-11","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Paragon 5","u":"Sunsoft USA","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"January 3, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_S-11","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4eqy.jpg","s":"When a vicious crew of aliens decides to hone in on Earth and do some damage, you're the planet's only hope for salvation. You'd better be in top form as you command a killer spacecraft out into orbit in hopes of rescuing humanity from unthinkable misery. Each of the eight stages in PROJECT S-11 poses unique challenges, and each setting is more harrowing than the last. Although you're going it alone in this one, there are four different types of power-ups hidden throughout the game to give the boost you'll need to survive and wipe out the terrifying extra-terrestrials. You'll benefit from a complete statistical readout that adorns the right side of your screen, keeping you abreast of all necessary information: your score, your ship's armor level, and the number of bombs you have stored up for combat. The game's fast-paced action looks excellent in full color, and the intensity of it will have you breaking a sweat before you're through the first level. Those aliens had better look out with you at the controls, Earth stands a fighting chance."},{"t":"Putter Golf","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Amedio Co.","u":"D3 Publisher, Agetec","g":"Golfing, Simulation","r":"August 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Putter_Golf","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coaur2.jpg","s":"Putter Golf is a 3D miniature golf simulation. It features both a \"normal\" 9-hole course, with realistic courses, as well as a \"trick\" 9-hole course, with somewhat more unusual features, such as warp zones and conveyor belts. Rounding out the package is a mix mode, with randomly selected holes from both courses, and a practice mode that both allows you to practice hitting on various slopes, and try out the features from the trick mode."},{"t":"Puyo Pop","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Sonic Team, Caret House","u":"Sega, THQ","g":"Puzzle","r":"October 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puyo_Pop_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6i1x.jpg","s":"Puyo Pop for the Game Boy Advance was the first game in the series to be developed by Sonic Team. It combines Puyo Puyo 2's mechanics with an all-new story mode."},{"t":"Rails Across America","p":["Windows"],"d":"Flying Lab Software","u":"Strategy First","g":"Business simulation","r":"September 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rails_Across_America","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7h1m.jpg","s":"Rails Across America is similar to the Railroad Tycoon games, but more streamlined and it adds some features from table top games such as cards to play against your opponents."},{"t":"Rally Championship Xtreme","p":["Windows"],"d":"Warthog Games","u":"Actualize","g":"Racing","r":"November 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rally_Championship_Xtreme","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7yjx.jpg","s":"Rally Championship Xtreme is a Racing game, developed by Warthog and published by Actualize, which was released in Europe in 2001."},{"t":"Rally Trophy","p":["Windows"],"d":"Bugbear Entertainment","u":"JoWooD Productions","g":"Racing","r":"November 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rally_Trophy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6y08.jpg","s":"In the game, the player is taken through 42 highly detailed stages in five countries - Russia, Sweden, Finland, Switzerland and Kenya - which cover several road surfaces including gravel, mud and tarmac. Stages take place in all weather conditions such as rain in both the day and night. Damage is accurately re-created, even down to broken headlights which the player must of course preserve to see during night stages. Rally Trophy was considered by many people to be the Grand Prix Legends of rally driving, due to its focus on historic cars, and the physics models of the cars (with no traction control or driver aids) being relatively realistic and challenging for its time."},{"t":"Rampage Puzzle Attack","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 29","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rampage_Puzzle_Attack"},{"t":"Rayman 2: The Great Escape","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"January 30"},{"t":"Rayman Advance","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"June 11","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rayman_Advance"},{"t":"Rayman M","p":["PlayStation 2","Windows"],"d":"Ubi Pictures","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Party, racing","r":"November 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rayman_M","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3qig.jpg","s":"Rayman Arena is a party game featuring Rayman. Up to four players can run through race tracks or compete in an arena."},{"t":"Reader Rabbit Preschool: Sparkle Star Rescue","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"The Learning Company","g":"Educational","r":"June 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reader_Rabbit_Preschool:_Sparkle_Star_Rescue","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2htx.jpg"},{"t":"Real Lives","p":["Windows"],"d":"Educational Simulations / Neeti Solutions","u":"Neeti Solutions","g":"Education, simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_Lives_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8v7h.jpg","s":"Real Lives is an educational game in which you take the role of a person in just about any country in the world. You are given a random country, gender, name, and family and must go through life. You can choose your education, leisure activities, job, and also make many moral choices along the way. Lives can be lived in about an hour or two, so you can experience life from someone else's point of view just about any time you want."},{"t":"Real War","p":["Windows"],"d":"Rival Interactive, Semi Logic Entertainments","u":"Simon & Schuster Interactive","g":"Real Time Strategy (RTS), Strategy, Tactical","r":"August 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_War","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cobrlj.jpg","s":"Based on the official Joint Chiefs of Staff training game developed for the U.S. military, Real War uses real-world scenarios, allowing you to play as the current U.S. military or as a terrorist army extrapolated from actual terrorist forces.\n\nReal War is a real-time strategy game in which you command today's armed forces. All jets, ships, and ground vehicles are taken directly from today's military. You can also get your hands on a few special items right off the Pentagon's drawing boards. Real War features 3-D vehicles with realistic movement against the backdrop of a richly detailed 2-D world, with jungle, desert, and arctic environments."},{"t":"Red Ace Squadron","p":["Windows"],"d":"Small Rockets","u":"Small Rockets","g":"Vehicle simulation game","r":"December 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Ace_Squadron"},{"t":"Red Faction","p":["PlayStation 2","Windows"],"u":"THQ","r":"May 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Faction_(video_game)"},{"t":"Reel Fishing: Wild","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"Westone Bit Entertainment","u":"Victor Interactive Software, Natsume Inc.","g":"Sports","r":"February 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reel_Fishing:_Wild","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4383.jpg","s":"Reel Fishing is a series of fishing video games by Natsume. The first game, Reel Fishing, was released for the PlayStation in 1996. Originally a localization of Pack-In-Video's (now Marvelous) Fish Eyes series from Japan, Natsume has since diverged from that series to create their own games."},{"t":"Ren'ai CHU!","p":["Dreamcast","Windows"],"d":"Saga Planets","u":"Saga Planets, GN Software","g":"Erotic game","r":"March 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ren'ai_CHU!"},{"t":"Resident Evil Gaiden","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"M4","u":"Capcom, Virgin Interactive","g":"Action-adventure","r":"December 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resident_Evil_Gaiden","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2xix.jpg","s":"Resident Evil Gaiden is a Resident Evil game for the Game Boy Color. It is non-canon, as its plot is contradicted by the main series in key parts and its events are ignored. The game features three playable characters and consists of exploring areas, collecting items and combating enemies in the process. Unlike previous entries in the series, Resident Evil Gaiden assumes a top-down perspective for the environment portions that, upon approaching an enemy, changes to a first-person combat mode with a reticle constantly moving to the left and right. To attack, the player has to press the action button while the reticle is in range of the enemy."},{"t":"Resident Evil Survivor 2 – Code: Veronica","p":["Arcade","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Namco, Nextech, SIMS Co., Ltd.","u":"Capcom, Namco","g":"Light gun shooter","r":"July 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resident_Evil_Survivor_2_%E2%80%93_Code:_Veronica","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2w3y.jpg","s":"Resident Evil Survivor 2 Code: Veronica is a first-person shooter video game and the second installment in the Gun Survivor series. As in most lightgun games, the objective is to make it through an environment while shooting enemies. It is somewhat less typical in that you have full roaming control over your character rather than being stuck on rails. More unusually, a timer begins as you enter each area. If the timer runs out before you leave, Nemesis will appear and chase you."},{"t":"Return of the Ninja","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Act Japan","u":"Natsume Inc.","g":"Platform, stealth","r":"March 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Return_of_the_Ninja","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7wh9.jpg","s":"What your enemy fears the most is something it cannot see: You!\n\nA mysterious army has attacked the land of Koga and stolen an ancient and sacred scroll. As a highly trained ninja, it's up to you to find out who is behind the trouble and recover Koga's precious treasure. To accomplish this, you'll need to recover six secret ninja tools hidden from the invaders. The enemy army may outnumber you a thousand to one, but you are a master of stealth-- and every military leader knows that a blind enemy is a vanquished foe."},{"t":"Return to Castle Wolfenstein","p":["Windows"],"u":"Activision","g":"Action","r":"November 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Return_to_Castle_Wolfenstein","i":"images/steam-9010.jpg","s":"World War II rages and nations fall. The Nazis are twisting science and the occult into an army capable of annihilating the Allies once and for all. As US Army Ranger William “B.J.” Blazkowicz, pierce the black heart of the Third Reich and stop them...or die trying."},{"t":"Rival Ball","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Longbow Digital Arts","u":"Longbow Digital Arts","g":"Breakout clone","r":"March 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rival_Ball","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5y1q.jpg"},{"t":"Road to India","p":["Windows"],"d":"Microïds","u":"Microïds","g":"Adventure","r":"May 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Road_to_India_(video_game)"},{"t":"RoboCop","p":["Game Boy Color"],"g":"Arcade","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RoboCop_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5x49.jpg","s":"OCP Technologies has developed an organic and mechanical hybrid supercomputer which they've named \"The Mind\". Unknown to the scientists however, The Mind has secretly been infiltrating computer networks around the world. When the project is finally deemed complete, OCP puts The Mind in control of the security systems of Delta City. At the same time, a gang war erupts in the city and several laboratories are broken into and looted. The police recieve a lead on a gang leader named \"Nexx\" who may be involved and assign RoboCop, a cyborg law enforcement officer, to bring him in."},{"t":"Roboforge","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roboforge"},{"t":"Robopon 2","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Red Company","u":"Hudson Soft, Atlus","g":"Role-playing","r":"September 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robopon_2"},{"t":"Robot Wars: Advanced Destruction","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Crawfish Interactive","u":"BBC Multimedia, Vivendi Universal Games","g":"Action","r":"November 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robot_Wars:_Advanced_Destruction","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2xzx.jpg","s":"In just over 4 years the Robot Wars TV series has grown from cult status to become a massive worldwide phenomenon, with the BBC show now attracting a weekly audience of around four million viewers. Taking its cue from the latest series, this GBA Robot Wars game cranks up the action with new events, new robots, new arenas and a wide range of new game features.\n\nThe game brings together all the elements of the TV show - players can design and build their own robots, drawing upon a database of body parts, armour, engines, wheels and weapons. Once complete, battle can commence!\n\nThe game also features a quick start `pick up and play' function, so gamers can select from a range of pre-built robots, and get straight to the realistic 3D action."},{"t":"Robot Wars: Arenas of Destruction","p":["PlayStation 2","Windows"],"d":"Climax Development","u":"BBC Multimedia, Vivendi Universal Games","g":"Action","r":"November 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robot_Wars:_Arenas_of_Destruction","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/lauirokc0thi1drzv3rv.jpg","s":"Robot Wars: Arenas Of Destruction is the first in the series of the 3 Robot Wars games. It was created for the PC and PS2. In this game, players can fight as their own created robots, or famous robots from the series, such as Razer, Behemoth and others in many arenas and tournaments in order to earn money and build robots."},{"t":"Rock Manager","p":["Windows"],"d":"Monsterland Produktion","u":"PAN Interactive, DreamCatcher Interactive, Mindscape","g":"Business simulation game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_Manager","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7xk1.jpg","s":"Rock Manager puts you in complete control of a rock band. Your task is simple; make them stars! Bribe the local radio station to get more airplay for your band, or buy up all your records to create increased demand. Use any means necessary to make the band climb the charts - anything goes in this strung-out world of swindle, lies and rock n' roll.\nStart by putting a band together. You get to choose from a diverse list of musicians with all tastes and styles. Record a demo in studio, adding effects to the song to make is fit your image. With a demo in hand, it's up to you to bag a record deal, arrange gigs, promote your record and go on the road. But, keep an eye on your musicians and keep them happy or the band might self-destruct!"},{"t":"Rocket Power: Dream Scheme","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 3"},{"t":"Rocket Power: Gettin' Air","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"March 27"},{"t":"Romance of the Three Kingdoms VIII","p":["Windows"],"d":"Koei, Kou Shibusawa","u":"Koei, Koei Tecmo","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"June 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romance_of_the_Three_Kingdoms_VIII","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5x94.jpg","s":"KOEI beckons PS2 strategy gamers to return to second-century China with this eighth full release in the Romance of the Three Kingdoms series. Following the style of the series, this edition is set amongst the confusion and chaos at the end of the Han dynasty. As in earlier games, the ultimate goal is to reunite the sundered nation, and players can take the roles of diverse characters as they work to do so.\n\nStrategy gamers can take control of up to eight different characters in a single game. Over 700 characters are available, 100 of which are customizable originals. Other new features include the chance for more than four forces to meet on the same battlefield and the ability to set the game's starting point to any time during the 50-year period between the Yellow Turban Rebellion in 184 A.D. and the death of Zhuge Liang in 234 A.D.\n\nWork your way through 54 scenarios and one fictional story featuring every officer in the game\n\nUse commands such as \"Marriage\" and \"Sworn Brother\" to alter the relationships of your empire\n\nCreate an army by selecting rulers, warlords, viceroys, free officers, and bands"},{"t":"Rugrats: All Growed-Up","p":["Windows"],"g":"Platform, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rugrats:_All_Growed-Up_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9te7.jpg"},{"t":"Rugrats: Castle Capers","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Software Creations","u":"THQ","g":"Platform","r":"September 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rugrats:_Castle_Capers","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coa070.jpg","s":"An all-new, side-scrolling platform style game featuring six huge levels based on classic fairytales with a Rugrats twist. This imaginative title features all of the Rugrats babies including Kimi, Chuckie's new stepsister."},{"t":"Rumble Racing","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"EA Redwood Shores","u":"EA Games","g":"Racing","r":"April 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumble_Racing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7k2y.jpg","s":"Rumble Racing is muscle car mayhem for the next millennium. Race outside the lines and push custom cars to the limit with big air stunts and nasty tricks as you race for the Rumble Championship. Unlock new worlds and new tracks with each Gold Cup victory, racing alone or partnering up with a friend."},{"t":"Rumbling Hearts","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumbling_Hearts"},{"t":"Run=Dim as Black Soul","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Role-playing (RPG), Turn-based strategy (TBS)","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Run=Dim_as_Black_Soul","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5ud1.jpg","s":"A Turn-Based Strategy RPG based on the anime series Run=Dim, set in a dystopian future where 1/10th of Japan has sunk due to global warming and an evil military organization named \"JESAS\" has set it's sight on controlling the future of Japan with mechas. Being the second game in the series (the first being \"The Mech Smith Run=Dim\" for the PlayStation 2) developed by Idea Factory."},{"t":"Runaway: A Road Adventure","p":["Windows"],"g":"Adventure","r":"July 6","l":"https://store.steampowered.com/app/7210","i":"images/steam-7210.jpg","s":"New York, 2000......Without knowing how or why, Brian, a student on the verge of graduating from college, is attacked by Mafia gangsters. During his desperate getaway, in the company of a mysterious striptease dancer, he ends up meeting a wide range of unusual characters."},{"t":"Rune: Viking Warlord","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"July 28","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rune_(video_game)"},{"t":"RuneScape","p":["Windows"],"g":"Massively Multiplayer, RPG, Free To Play","r":"January 4","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RuneScape","i":"images/steam-1343400.jpg","s":"RuneScape is a high fantasy open world MMORPG. Explore an ever changing and evolving living world where new challenges, skills, and quests await. Featuring unprecedented player freedom, you choose how to play, adventure, and grow."},{"t":"S.W.I.N.E.","p":["Windows"],"d":"StormRegion","u":"Ravensburger Interactive Media","g":"Real-time tactics","r":"November 9, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S.W.I.N.E.","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5x9c.jpg","s":"S.W.I.N.E. (Strategic Warfare In a Nifty Environment) is a real-time tactics game designed by FishTank Studios. The player chooses to fight as either the brutal invading pigs or the pacifist rabbits, and plays a series of missions. There are many units, including mortar, artillery, and tank units. There are also 10 multiplayer campaigns, each with many modes of play, including capture the flag and deathmatch."},{"t":"Sakura Taisen Online","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sakura_Taisen_Online"},{"t":"Sakura Wars 3","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"Red Company, Overworks","u":"Sega, Dysin Interactive","g":"Tactical role-playing, dating sim, visual novel","r":"March 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sakura_Wars_3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8kz3.jpg","s":"Sakura Wars 3 takes place following the events of Sakura Wars 2. Series protagonist Ichiro Ogami joins the newly formed Paris Assault Troupe to train their own Flower Division, an all-female unit dedicated to fighting supernatural threats against the city while maintaining their cover as a cabaret act. The Paris Assault Troupe must learn to work together to defend their city against a series of mysterious phantoms seeking vengeance against Paris for their fallen ancient civilization."},{"t":"Samurai Romanesque","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samurai_Romanesque"},{"t":"Sayonara o Oshiete ~Comment te Dire Adieu~","p":["Windows"],"d":"CRAFTWORK","u":"CRAFTWORK & Asoberu! BD-GAME","g":"Visual novel","r":"March 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sayonara_o_Oshiete_~Comment_te_Dire_Adieu~"},{"t":"Scan Command: Jurassic Park","p":["Windows"],"d":"Knowledge Adventure","u":"Knowledge Adventure","g":"Fighting, strategy","r":"October 11, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scan_Command:_Jurassic_Park"},{"t":"Schizm: Mysterious Journey","p":["Windows"],"d":"LK Avalon, Detalion","u":"LK Avalon, The Adventure Company, Mindscape","g":"Adventure","r":"June 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schizm:_Mysterious_Journey","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co403m.jpg","s":"As two survivors of a spaceship crash, you must solve insidious puzzles to rendezvous with each other and uncover the mystery of the abandoned planet Argilus."},{"t":"Scooby-Doo and the Cyber Chase","p":["Game Boy Advance","PlayStation"],"r":"October 1","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scooby-Doo_and_the_Cyber_Chase_(video_game)"},{"t":"Scooby-Doo! Classic Creep Capers","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"February 20","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scooby-Doo!_Classic_Creep_Capers"},{"t":"Scorched 3D","p":["Windows"],"g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scorched_3D","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1pd8.jpg","s":"Scorched 3D is a turn-based artillery game modeled after the classic DOS game Scorched Earth \"The Mother Of All Games\". Unlike Scorched Earth however, it is available free in its entirety. Also, Scorched 3D adds, amongst other new features, a 3D island environment and LAN and internet play. Scorched 3D is an open source game for both Microsoft Windows and Unix-like (Linux, FreeBSD, Mac OS X, Solaris, etc.) operating systems."},{"t":"Sea-Doo Hydrocross","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Vicarious Visions, Engine Software","u":"Vatical Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"June 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea-Doo_Hydrocross","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coa4zm.jpg","s":"Sea-Doo HydroCross offers players five different models of fast maneuverable jet skis and eight varied courses on which to race them. Zip across the winding canals of Venice or speed through twisting sewers beneath Paris. For the truly adventurous jet skier there is even a Loch Ness course, complete with sea serpent.\n\nThe game has two different physics model settings - a simulation mode for a realistic feel and an arcade mode for fast, forgiving gameplay. Seadoo HydroCross is built on the tried and true Polaris SnoCross game engine."},{"t":"Secret Agent Barbie","p":["Windows","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Gigawatt Studios, Digital Illusions Canada","u":"Vivendi Universal Interactive Publishing","g":"3D adventure, 2D platformer","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secret_Agent_Barbie","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cob3h1.jpg","s":"Help secret agent Barbie catch a jewel thief; Decode puzzles and solve mysteries as you interact with many colorful characters"},{"t":"Secret Service","p":["Windows"],"d":"Fun Labs","u":"Activision Value","g":"Tactical shooter","r":"November 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secret_Service_(2001_video_game)"},{"t":"Sega Bass Fishing 2","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"WOW Entertainment","u":"Sega, Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"August 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sega_Bass_Fishing_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3kcx.jpg"},{"t":"Segagaga","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"Hitmaker","u":"Sega","g":"Role-playing","r":"March 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segagaga","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6yxb.jpg","s":"One of Sega's final titles for the Dreamcast, Segagaga is a genre-defying experience where Sega pokes fun at themselves which was given a release outside of Japan."},{"t":"Sengoku 3","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"Noise Factory","u":"SNK","g":"Beat 'em up, hack and slash","r":"July 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sengoku_3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1w4y.jpg","s":"The game takes a greater departure from its predecessors in both story and presentation. The heroes are now members of a ninja clan who have to dispatch an evil emperor before he is released. Players can choose which character they want for the first time, each having their own strengths and weaknesses. Two bosses that confront the player can also be playable after a certain chapter of the game, allowing players to re-select their character if they wish."},{"t":"Serious Sam: The First Encounter","p":["Windows"],"r":"March 21"},{"t":"Severance: Blade of Darkness","p":["Windows"],"r":"February 23"},{"t":"Shadow Hearts","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"December 12","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow_Hearts"},{"t":"ShadowFlare","p":["Windows"],"d":"Denyusha","u":"Emurasoft","g":"Action role-playing, hack and slash","r":"October 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ShadowFlare","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co948i.jpg","s":"Diablo-like game with elemental based stats and animal companions."},{"t":"Shattered Galaxy","p":["Windows"],"d":"Kru Interactive","u":"Tri Synergy","g":"Massively multiplayer online real-time strategy","r":"August 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shattered_Galaxy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2su0.jpg","s":"An MMORTSRPG with explosions, depth and a still solid community."},{"t":"Shaun Palmer's Pro Snowboarder","p":["PlayStation 2","Game Boy Color","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Dearsoft, ITL, Natsume Co., Ltd.","u":"Activision O2","g":"Snowboarding","r":"November 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaun_Palmer's_Pro_Snowboarder","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1p01.jpg","s":"Shaun Palmer's Pro Snowboarder is real life racing and freestyle snowboarding featuring living legend and six-time Winter X-Games gold medallist Shaun Palmer and 9 of the most bad ass names in the sport. Players race down the slopes of real world locations pulling hundreds of authentic trick combos. The game has expansive interactive environments allowing players to not only explore hidden and secret areas but stick tricks off anything in sight. Immediately playable yet challenging, it is a competitive, authentic and aggressive snowboarding game that will keep even the most hardcore Snowboarders and gamers hyped."},{"t":"Sheep, Dog 'n' Wolf","p":["PlayStation","Windows"],"d":"Infogrames Lyon House","u":"Infogrames","g":"Puzzle-platformer, stealth","r":"March 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sheep,_Dog_'n'_Wolf","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2hjq.jpg","s":"Stealth, humour and tactical skills - the scene is set for the ultimate showdown between Sam Sheep-dog and a greedy wolf whose only thought is where his next meal is coming from. Take control of Ralph Wolf as he uses his ACME arsenal to solve perplexing platformer puzzles all in the name of stealing a sheep — live on Daffy Duck's new game show: Sheep, Dog, and Wolf."},{"t":"Shenmue II","p":["Dreamcast"],"u":"Sega","r":"September 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shenmue_II"},{"t":"Shikigami no Shiro","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Alfa System","u":"Arcade, Taito, XS Games, MediaQuest","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shikigami_no_Shiro","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co22vb.jpg","s":"Castle of Shikigami is a dark, supernatural shoot-'em-up classic. Choose one of six characters and utilize their unique abilities to defeat the demons infesting modern-day Tokyo in this vertically scrolling shooting game. Use each character's shot, special Shikigami attack and bomb to conquer each stage and it's boss to ultimately discover who or what is behind \"The Castle of Shikigami Incident\"."},{"t":"Shiren the Wanderer GB2: Magic Castle of the Desert","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Chunsoft","u":"Chunsoft, Sega","g":"Roguelike, role-playing","r":"July 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shiren_the_Wanderer_GB2:_Magic_Castle_of_the_Desert","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2ram.jpg","s":"Shiren the Wanderer GB2: Magic Castle of the Desert is a roguelike role-playing video game developed by Chunsoft. It is part of the Mystery Dungeon series, and is a sequel to Shiren the Wanderer GB: Moonlight Village Monster. It was originally released for the Game Boy Color by Chunsoft in 2001. A Nintendo DS remake was released by Sega later in 2008."},{"t":"Shogun: Total War : Mongol Invasion","p":["Windows"],"r":"August 8"},{"t":"Shrek","p":["Xbox"],"r":"November 14","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shrek_(video_game)"},{"t":"Shrek: Fairy Tale Freakdown","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"May 29"},{"t":"Silent Hill 2","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Konami","r":"September 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silent_Hill_2"},{"t":"Silent Hill 2: Director's Cut","p":["Xbox"],"r":"December 21","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silent_Hill_2"},{"t":"Silent Hunter II","p":["Windows"],"d":"Aeon Electronic Entertainment, Ultimation Inc.","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Submarine simulator","r":"November 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silent_Hunter_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coaloo.jpg","s":"Silent Hunter II is a World War II submarine simulation that puts players in command of German U-boats throughout various theaters of war. Players can engage in single missions or embark on a career-spanning campaign across seven maritime regions, from the Eastern United States to the Indian Ocean, facing both American and British naval forces. The game features 12 different U-boat types, historical accuracy in ranking against actual WWII submarine aces, and interoperability with its companion game Destroyer Command for online multiplayer warfare. After completing missions, players earn medals and commendations based on their performance and tonnage of enemy ships sunk."},{"t":"Silpheed: The Lost Planet","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"April 23"},{"t":"Skateboard Park Tycoon","p":["Windows","Mobile phone"],"d":"Cat Daddy Games, Airborne Entertainment","u":"Activision","g":"Business simulation game","r":"September 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skateboard_Park_Tycoon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cwytvsd4bkw1da6ohola.jpg","s":"Skateboard Park Tycoon lets players create their own skate parks in hopes of becoming profitable. Players can also skate their custom parks, once created."},{"t":"Ski Resort Tycoon II","p":["Windows"],"d":"Cat Daddy Games","u":"Activision Value","g":"Business simulation","r":"October 8, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ski_Resort_Tycoon_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8tcm.jpg","s":"Ski Resort Tycoon II the sequel to Ski Resort Tycoon. The game combines the best features of the original with new elements and improvements. Add ski runs and buildings to your resort, while managing your income and trying to keep the visitors happy. Better graphics, bigger maps and a wide variety of animations distinguish this product from its predecessor.\n\nThe game has the same general atmosphere as the original, but with improved graphics and user interface. More equipment, additional views including 360 degrees, better and more complete buildings and a new and improved sound."},{"t":"Sky Sports Football Quiz","p":["PlayStation","Windows"],"d":"Hothouse Creations","u":"THQ","g":"Quiz","r":"December 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky_Sports_Football_Quiz"},{"t":"Smuggler's Run 2","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Angel Studios","u":"Rockstar Games","g":"Racing","r":"October 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smuggler's_Run_2"},{"t":"Snoopy Tennis","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"April 6","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snoopy_Tennis"},{"t":"Sonic Adventure 2","p":["Dreamcast"],"u":"Sega","r":"June 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_Adventure_2"},{"t":"Sonic Shuffle","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"March 9","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_Shuffle"},{"t":"Soul Reaver 2","p":["PlayStation 2","Windows"],"r":"October 31","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soul_Reaver_2"},{"t":"Spec Ops: Ranger Elite","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Runecraft Ltd.","u":"Take-Two Interactive","g":"Action","r":"April 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spec_Ops:_Ranger_Elite","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8mkp.jpg","s":"Spec Ops: Ranger Elite is a tactical shooter with an emphasis on strategy. As in earlier releases in the Spec Ops series, the player takes control of two operatives from a small group of highly trained Army Rangers in this next title from Runecraft and Take 2. Each member of the group has a specialty so choosing the right duo for a given mission is an important factor of success.\n\nThe action takes place in 3D-rendered environments as the player guides the characters from a third-person perspective. In the single-player version of the game, the player takes the role of one soldier while the computer guides the other."},{"t":"Spider-Man","p":["Dreamcast"],"r":"April 19","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spider-Man_(2000_video_game)"},{"t":"Spider-Man 2: Enter: Electro","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Vicarious Visions","u":"Activision","g":"Action-adventure","r":"October 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spider-Man_2:_Enter:_Electro"},{"t":"Spider-Man 2: The Sinister Six","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"May 30"},{"t":"Spider-Man: Mysterio's Menace","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Vicarious Visions","u":"Activision","g":"Action","r":"September 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spider-Man:_Mysterio's_Menace","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5qh9.jpg","s":"Set in New York, Spider-Man: Mysterio's Menace enables players to assume the role of Spider-Man as they take on super-villain Mysterio and his illusory tricks for control of Manhattan. Spider-Man must fight a slew of Mysterio's sinister conspirators and gadgetry to get to the bottom of their plot and free New York from Mysterio's mind-warping grip."},{"t":"Splashdown","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Rainbow Studios","u":"Infogrames, Inc.","g":"Racing","r":"November 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Splashdown_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1p19.jpg","s":"Splashdown is a water racing video game for the Xbox and Sony PlayStation 2. It can be played by one or two players. Splashdown is quite similar to Nintendo's Wave Race series. Players take control of a personal water craft as they race against each other on various courses. Players must pass the correct side of buoys on the way or the personal water craft may stall as a penalty or they get splashed by a whales tail."},{"t":"SpongeBob SquarePants: Legend of the Lost Spatula","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"March 15"},{"t":"SpongeBob SquarePants: Operation Krabby Patty","p":["Windows"],"r":"September 24"},{"t":"SpongeBob SquarePants: SuperSponge","p":["PlayStation","Game Boy Advance"],"r":"September 21"},{"t":"Spy Fox: \"Operation Ozone\"","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Humongous Entertainment","u":"Infogrames","g":"Adventure","r":"May 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spy_Fox:_%22Operation_Ozone%22"},{"t":"SpyHunter","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"September 24","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SpyHunter"},{"t":"Spyro: Season of Ice","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 29"},{"t":"Squad Battles: Vietnam","p":["Windows"],"d":"HPS Simulations","u":"HPS Simulations","g":"Computer wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Squad_Battles:_Vietnam"},{"t":"SSX Tricky","p":["PlayStation 2","Xbox","GameCube"],"u":"EA Sports","r":"November 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SSX_Tricky"},{"t":"Star Monkey","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Monkey"},{"t":"Star Ocean: Blue Sphere","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"tri-Ace, Sonic Powered","u":"Enix, Square Enix","g":"Action role-playing","r":"June 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Ocean:_Blue_Sphere","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6f17.jpg","s":"Star Ocean: Blue Sphere is a Role-Playing game, developed by Tri-Ace and published by Enix Corporation, which was released in Japan in 2001. It is the direct sequel to Star Ocean: The Second Story."},{"t":"Star Trek: Armada II","p":["Windows"],"d":"Mad Doc Software","u":"Activision","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"November 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek:_Armada_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2e93.jpg","s":"Star Trek: Armada II is a real time strategy video game published by Activision in 2001, based upon the Star Trek universe. The game was developed by Mad Doc Software. It is the sequel to Star Trek: Armada. Star Trek: Armada II was released by Activision a year after they acquired the full rights to all the franchise holding of the video game's franchise from Viacom.\n\nLike its predecessor, Armada II is set in the Star Trek: The Next Generation era of the Star Trek universe. The game showcases events in the Alpha Quadrant between the United Federation of Planets, the Klingon Empire, the Romulan Star Empire, the Cardassian Union, Species 8472, and the Borg."},{"t":"Star Trek: Away Team","p":["Windows"],"d":"Reflexive Entertainment","u":"Activision","g":"Real-time tactics","r":"March 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek:_Away_Team","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2e94.jpg","s":"A real-time tactical combat game set in the Star Trek universe, in which the player endeavours to make sure his team of Starfleet officers doesn't meet the gruesome fate of so many redshirts before them."},{"t":"Star Trek: Deep Space Nine: Dominion Wars","p":["Windows"],"d":"Gizmo Industries","u":"Simon & Schuster","g":"Real-time tactics, space combat","r":"June 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek:_Deep_Space_Nine:_Dominion_Wars"},{"t":"Star Wars Episode I: Jedi Power Battles","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 21"},{"t":"Star Wars Rogue Squadron II: Rogue Leader","p":["GameCube"],"u":"LucasArts","r":"November 18, 2001"},{"t":"Star Wars: Episode I: Battle for Naboo","p":["Windows","Nintendo 64"],"r":"March 12"},{"t":"Star Wars: Galactic Battlegrounds","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"LucasArts, Ensemble Studios, Westlake Interactive","u":"LucasArts, Aspyr","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"November 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Wars:_Galactic_Battlegrounds","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7nlh.jpg","s":"The destiny of a galaxy hangs in the balance, and you are in command. Lead the great armies of the Star Wars: Episode II: Attack of the Clones saga in intense real-time strategy clashes. Enter the fray as the Galactic Empire, Rebel Alliance, Wookies, Trade Federation, Gungans or Royal Naboo to determine the course of the Galactic Civil War."},{"t":"Star Wars: Obi-Wan","p":["Xbox"],"r":"December 19"},{"t":"Star Wars: Starfighter","p":["Xbox"],"r":"November 26"},{"t":"Star Wars: Super Bombad Racing","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Lucas Learning","u":"LucasArts","g":"Racing, vehicular combat","r":"April 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Wars:_Super_Bombad_Racing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co47v8.jpg","s":"Jar Jar throws his Bongo racing ride into warp speed with his very own exciting big-headed kart racer Star Wars: Super Bombad Racing! Everybody from Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace has put their intergalactic troubles aside to enter into a wild racing adventure -- Queen Amidala, Anakin, Yoda, Obi-Wan, Sebulba, Bos Nass, Sebulba, and even Darth Maul are all out for that checkered flag. Each racer gets to ride special racing vehicles (a Pod Racer, a Sith Interceptor, a Naboo Fighter), and with the different vehicles comes individually geared racing controls.\n\nOnce the green light flashes, it’s a crazy dash for the finish across famous STAR WARS locations, with flying missiles and colorful power-ups blasting all over the map. Get mad air off jumps and crack open secret locations to find all of the game’s hidden special features. All this action explodes off the screen with incredible special effects and dazzling graphics from the force of the PlayStation 2 computer entertainment system. Meesa can’t wait to play this!"},{"t":"Starships Unlimited","p":["Windows"],"d":"ApeZone","u":"Matrix Games","g":"4X Real-Time Strategy","r":"May 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starships_Unlimited"},{"t":"Startopia","p":["Windows"],"r":"June 19","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Startopia"},{"t":"State of War","p":["Windows"],"d":"Cypron Studios, Cinemax","u":"Crystal Interactive","g":"Real-time strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_of_War_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1ms7.jpg"},{"t":"Stepmother's Sin","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stepmother's_Sin"},{"t":"Stretch Panic","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Treasure","u":"Conspiracy Entertainment, Swing! Entertainment, Kadokawa Shoten","g":"Action","r":"July 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stretch_Panic","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6tv5.jpg","s":"Stretch Panic is an action/platform video game designed by Treasure Co. Ltd. It was a landmark title for the developer as it was their first game to feature movement on a 3D plane; prior to Stretch Panic they had exclusively been a developer of 2D titles. The game revolves around a girl named Linda who has the power to pinch, stretch, and bend objects and enemies with a magic scarf as she battles her evil sisters."},{"t":"Strikers 1945 II","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"March 27","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strikers_1945_II"},{"t":"Stronghold","p":["Windows"],"r":"October 21","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stronghold_(2001_video_game)"},{"t":"Stuart Little: The Journey Home","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Tiertex Design Studios","u":"Activision","g":"Platform, racing","r":"August 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_Little:_The_Journey_Home","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7i6c.jpg","s":"This little mouse has one big journey ahead of him, and you're just the one to help him out. Unfortunately, the Little family didn't foresee the possible conflict of interest in keeping a cat and mouse on the same household when they adopted Stuart, and that's proving to be a bit of a problem. Between newfound enemies and domestic obstacles, the tiny mouse is having one heck of a time! There are eight gigantic levels to conquer in The Journey Home, and each one is based on a scene from the box office smash STUART LITTLE movies (which are, of course, based on the book by E.B. White). You'll end up tackling a mini-golf course, Central Park, a model boat race, a bunch of toy cowboys, and more in this epic quest for peace. In addition, here are plenty of platforms and hazards to run, jump, and flip through, and three difficulty settings ensure that younger gamers and experienced veterans alike will find the game appealing."},{"t":"Stunt GP","p":["Windows","Dreamcast","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Team17","u":"Eon Digital Entertainment, Titus Interactive","g":"Racing","r":"April 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stunt_GP","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co44uq.jpg","s":"And RC car racing game where doing spins, flips and other stunts earns you points that can be exchanged for upgrades."},{"t":"Sub Command","p":["Windows"],"d":"Sonalysts","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Submarine simulator","r":"September 11, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sub_Command","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co42jw.jpg","s":"Take charge of the most deadly modern-day submarines in the world - three distinct submarines across two unique and challenging campaigns. Utilize cutting-edge sensor and weapon technology to locate, track and destroy the enemy - even deliver Tomahawk missiles to inland targets. Whether transiting, diving, or surfacing you control the smartest, stealthiest and most feared subs on the water today.\n\nThree submarines to command - Seawolf, 688(I) and Akula. Each contains authentic naval systems including Sonar, Radar and Target Motion Analysis. Test your skills while firing Tomahawk missiles, rescuing crippled subs, rising through the polar ice cap to communicate information and much more.\n\nEasy to use game interface - With straightforward controls and automated crewmen, controlling a submarine has never been easier.\n\nEnhanced sound and 3D graphics engine - 3D Objects (over 250), from Russian trawlers to France's Mirage fighters - plus exacting sound modes pull you in like never before. You won't just hear the torpedos, you will feel them."},{"t":"Suika","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suika_(2001_video_game)"},{"t":"Summoner","p":["Windows"],"r":"March 20","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summoner_(video_game)"},{"t":"Sunny Garcia Surfing","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Krome Studios","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Sports","r":"October 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunny_Garcia_Surfing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/vrfn8jzbraadxhwx3lts.jpg","s":"Sunny Garcia Surfing was released in October 2001 to mediocre reviews. It was developed using an updated version of the Championship Surfer engine. Critics complained that in updating the engine developers did not update the graphics enough, leaving the game looking dated from its launch. Other aspects which received negative attention include a limited trick system and poor camera angles."},{"t":"Super Dodge Ball Advance","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Million Co. Ltd.","u":"Atlus, Atlus USA, Ubi Soft","g":"Sports game","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Dodge_Ball_Advance","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4i3k.jpg","s":"The year is 20XX and dodge ball has become the number one sport in the world. Teams from all over the world now compete in dodge ball tournaments to prove once and for all who is the best dodge ball team ever."},{"t":"Super Mario Advance","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"June 11","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Mario_Bros._2#Super_Mario_Advance"},{"t":"Super Mario World: Super Mario Advance 2","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"December 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Mario_World"},{"t":"Super Monkey Ball","p":["GameCube"],"u":"Sega","r":"September 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Monkey_Ball_(video_game)"},{"t":"Super Robot Wars A","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"AI, Banpresoft","u":"Banpresto, Bandai Namco Games","g":"Tactical role-playing game","r":"September 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Robot_Wars_A","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cobvfb.jpg","s":"Super Robot Taisen A (Advance) is the first Super Robot Wars on the Game Boy Advance. The Londo Bell forces battle against the Shadow Mirror."},{"t":"Super Robot Wars Alpha Gaiden","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Banpresoft","u":"Banpresto","g":"Turn-Based Strategy RPG","r":"March 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Robot_Wars_Alpha_Gaiden","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2whe.jpg","s":"Shortly after the events of Alpha, the Earth Federation has covered up many of the events in the climatic battle with the antagonist Aerogaters, but the gravity shock wave from the detonation of the Excelion (from Gunbuster) in the Battle of the Solar System's Absolute Defense is rapidly approaching the Earth, and threatens to wipe out the space colonies and the planet. The Earth's only hope is the Aegis System, which could shield the Earth Sphere from the shock wave.\n\nUnfortunately, the heroes are sent to an alternate future, where the Earth has been ravaged by the wave. Faced with a new threat in the form of the Ancestors, the heroes must find a way to return to their time and prevent this alternate, dystopic future from happening."},{"t":"Super Smash Bros. Melee","p":["GameCube"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"November 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Smash_Bros._Melee"},{"t":"Super Street Fighter II Turbo Revival","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Street_Fighter_II_Turbo_Revival"},{"t":"Supercar Street Challenge","p":["PlayStation 2","Windows"],"d":"Exakt Entertainment","u":"Activision","g":"Racing, Simulation video game","r":"November 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supercar_Street_Challenge","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8hso.jpg","s":"Climb in, get behind the wheel and buckle up for the ride of your life! Street Lethal is no ordinary racing game. Rampage through the streets of spectacular interactive city envrionments such as London and Paris. Hold on tight as you drive the hottest modern concept cars. If you've ever dreamed about driving high-class speedsters like the Callaway C12, the Pagani Zonda C12-S, and the Fiorvanti F100, you're in luck. Travel through the streets of the world's most prominent cities in a race of the ultimate driving machines. You'll hit speeds above 200 miles per hour as you dodge through street fairs, back alleys, and more in an attempt to win. Experience the power of driving these previously inaccessible vehicles, enjoying 3D graphics and an awesome soundtrack all the while."},{"t":"Supercross Freestyle","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Velez & Dubail Dev. Team","u":"Infogrames Europe","g":"Sports; racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supercross_Freestyle","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co7mjg.jpg","s":"Supercross Freestyle is a dirt bike racing game. The game features three game modes: Freestyle, Supercross and Arcade. Freestyle is all about making impressive jumps and tricks with your bike. Supercross and Arcade are racing modes, where Arcade has different courses and power-ups that can change the odds. The game features 24 different tracks and 16 different tricks."},{"t":"Survivor","p":["Windows"],"r":"November 12","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Survivor_(2001_video_game)"},{"t":"Survivor: The Interactive Game","p":["Windows"],"d":"Magic Lantern","u":"Infogrames","g":"Adventure game","r":"November 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Survivor:_The_Interactive_Game","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/k0aidljzcwggzxlvkxjz.jpg"},{"t":"Syphon Filter 3","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Sony Computer Entertainment America","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Third-person shooter","r":"November 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syphon_Filter_3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9457.jpg","s":"Syphon Filter 3 is a third-person shooter developed by Bend Studio and published by Sony Computer Entertainment. Released in November 2001 for the PlayStation, it is the third instalment in the Syphon Filter series. The game continues the story of secret agents Gabe Logan and Lian Xing as they are interrogated by a Senate committee about their previous missions. The narrative unfolds through flashbacks, revealing their battles against international conspiracies and bioterrorism."},{"t":"Tactics Ogre: The Knight of Lodis","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"June 21, 2001"},{"t":"Taisen Net Gimmick: Capcom & Psikyo All Stars","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Card & Board Game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taisen_Net_Gimmick:_Capcom_&_Psikyo_All_Stars","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co960j.jpg","s":"Psikyo and Capcom's top dogs clash in an epic battle of... mahjong!?"},{"t":"Takeda","p":["Windows"],"d":"Magitech","u":"Xicat Interactive, Inc.","g":"Real-time tactics","r":"November 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takeda_(video_game)"},{"t":"Tarzan: Untamed","p":["PlayStation 2","GameCube"],"d":"Ubi Soft Montreal","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Action-adventure","r":"November 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarzan:_Untamed","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co55z0.jpg","s":"Picking up quite a while after the defeat of Clayton, Jane and Professor Porter now speak Gorilla-language fluently and Jane is married to Tarzan. However, their lives are threatened once again by a brutal band of British explorers led by the unscrupulous Oswald Gardner, who becomes fascinated with Tarzan and strives to capture the ape-man and take him back to England as a media attraction."},{"t":"Tear Ring Saga","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Tirnanog","u":"Enterbrain","g":"Tactical role-playing","r":"May 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tear_Ring_Saga","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3z5e.jpg","s":"Tear Ring Saga is a tactical RPG with gameplay very similar to the gameplay found in the Fire Emblem series of video games. The player is tasked with certain objectives, commonly fighting and defeating an entire opposing faction, or a particular member or leader of a faction."},{"t":"Tech Deck Skateboarding","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Handheld Games","u":"Activision","g":"Sports","r":"March 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tech_Deck_Skateboarding","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/cobw4i.jpg","s":"Tech Deck Skateboarding challenges you to pull off the same tricks that regular skaters do just smaller. You play as the inimitable Thumb Guy, a lovable mascot who can pull off the fingerboard moves with the best of 'em. Featuring arcade-style play and animations, the game lets you whip out grinds, ollies, and flips with extreme flair. All of the licensed decks from real manufacturers are here, so you'll be skating in style on your Tony Hawk deck. You'll have access to a limited number of boards at the game's outset, but as you get further and further along the road to glory you'll be able to unlock more responsive outfits and other hidden goodies. With cool graphics, real decks, and totally original gameplay, it's hardcore skating for hardcore fans!"},{"t":"TechnoMage","p":["PlayStation","Windows"],"d":"Sunflowers","u":"Sunflowers","g":"Role-playing video game","r":"June 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TechnoMage","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5cvh.jpg","s":"Enter into an action-packed and thrilling adventure! Gothos, a world full of secrets and strange creatures and of two cultures; one technically gifted and inventive, the other blessed with knowledge and magical powers. Yet both face a common threat. You assume the role of Meliv, the TechnoMage. In your eventful travels through fascination worlds, you will meet curious peoples, bizarre characters and the enchanting Talis, who shares your destiny.\nIt was only ever released in the PAL region."},{"t":"Tekken Advance","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Eighting","u":"Namco, Namco Hometek, Infogrames","g":"Fighting","r":"December 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tekken_Advance","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co45q0.jpg","s":"Tekken Advance is a fighting game released for the Game Boy Advance. It is non-canonical to the Tekken storyline, but follows the events of Tekken 3. It uses sprites based on Tekken 3's 3D models for its characters and was the first Tekken game to be released on a Nintendo platform."},{"t":"Tennis Masters Series","p":["Windows"],"d":"Microids Canada","u":"Microids","g":"Sports","r":"November 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennis_Masters_Series_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3w66.jpg","s":"The official tennis simulation from the early aughts."},{"t":"Test Drive: Off-Road Wide Open","p":["PlayStation 2","Xbox"],"d":"Angel Studios","u":"Infogrames","g":"Racing","r":"August 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Test_Drive:_Off-Road_Wide_Open"},{"t":"Tetris Worlds","p":["Game Boy Advance","Windows"],"d":"Blue Planet Software, 3d6 Games, Radical Entertainment","u":"THQ","g":"Puzzle","r":"September 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetris_Worlds","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3a75.jpg","s":"For over a decade, the Tetris series has made an appearance on every game system. Tetris Worlds continues that streak, bringing their distinct brand of simple, yet addictive gameplay to the current generation of systems. Small shapes called Tetraminoes fall from the top of the screen to the bottom and you must rotate them and fit them together to complete lines. The game features six different variations of the classic Tetris, multiplayer mode for up to four players, and six different worlds. Sound easy? Once you start, you can't stop."},{"t":"Tex Atomic's Big Bot Battles","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tex_Atomic's_Big_Bot_Battles"},{"t":"The Adventures of Cookie & Cream","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"April 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Cookie_%26_Cream"},{"t":"The Bouncer","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"March 6","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bouncer_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Cameron Files: Secret at Loch Ness","p":["Windows"],"d":"Galiléa","u":"Wanadoo Edition, DreamCatcher Interactive/The Adventure Company","g":"Adventure","r":"November 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Cameron_Files:_Secret_at_Loch_Ness"},{"t":"The ClueFinders: The Incredible Toy Store Adventure!","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"The Learning Company","g":"Educational/adventure/science fiction/mystery","r":"August 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_ClueFinders:_The_Incredible_Toy_Store_Adventure!"},{"t":"The Corporate Machine","p":["Windows"],"d":"Stardock","u":"Take-Two Interactive","g":"Business simulation","r":"February 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Corporate_Machine","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5xw3.jpg","s":"The Corporate Machine is Stardock's classic 2001 real-time economic strategy game, available now on Steam. Dive back into the timeless battle for one of the most ruthless environments ever created: international trade.\n\nCorporations exist in a constant state of war: Plotting to destroy their rivals, CEOs chase world domination through economic strategies less deadly but more complex than any military maneuver. Control the market, or face the prospect of buyout and bankruptcy. Build your business empire, dominate the industry, and amass a fortune you will need that private island to fully enjoy."},{"t":"The Fish Files","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"7th Sense","u":"MC2-Microïds","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Fish_Files","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4za5.jpg","s":"The player takes control on Dante, a college student in search of his kidnapped fish, Ramada. His quest takes him to various cities, towns and other time periods."},{"t":"The Flintstones: Big Trouble in Bedrock","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"December 9"},{"t":"The Italian Job","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Pixelogic","u":"Sales Curve Interactive, Rockstar Games, SCi Games, Global Star Software","g":"Racing","r":"October 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Italian_Job_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2lcg.jpg","s":"The Italian Job is a video game based on the 1969 film of the same name. The game features a story mode based on the movie, and a multiplayer \"party\" mode where players compete through several different circuits in London and Turin, as well as a single player practice mode where the player can develop skills needed for completing the story mode."},{"t":"The King of Fighters 2001","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Eolith, BrezzaSoft, Playmore","u":"SNK, Sun Amusement, Playmore, SNK Playmore","g":"Fighting","r":"November 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_King_of_Fighters_2001","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4zxo.jpg","s":"The King of Fighters 2001 is the eighth game in The King of Fighters series, the third and final part of the \"Nests Chronicles\" story arc, and the first game produced following the closure of the original SNK.\n\nKOF 2001 kept the Striker assist system from the previous two KOF games, but added a new twist to it - the ability to adjust how many Strikers a team could have. While teams in 1999 and 2000 were limited to selecting one teammate as an assist, now teams could have up to 3 assist characters, or none at all."},{"t":"The Land Before Time","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Eclipse Entertainment","u":"Swing! Entertainment, Conspiracy Entertainment","g":"Platform","r":"June 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Land_Before_Time_(2001_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9bz6.jpg","s":"There's a rumbling at your feet. Is it a vicious T-Rex? Maybe it's an erupting volcano! No time to panic. Littlefoot, Cera, Spike, Ducky and Petrie need you! Your friends are trapped in the Mysterious Beyond and it's up to your to get them back to the safety of The Great Valley. Predators are lurking everywhere. The terrain is treacherous. Teamwork is the key. It's an amazing adventure that takes you through The Land Before Time!\n\nThe Land Before Time is a 2D platforming game developed by Eclipse Entertainment for the Game Boy Color. It was first released in Europe in June 2001, with a North American release the following month, both published by Conspiracy Entertainment. The game received mostly lower reviews, earning a 40% average from aggregate review website GameRankings."},{"t":"The Land Before Time: Great Valley Racing Adventure","p":["PlayStation"],"g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Land_Before_Time:_Great_Valley_Racing_Adventure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8cvd.jpg","s":"The Land Before Time: Great Valley Racing Adventure is a cartoon-styled racing game designed to offer nonviolent fun and challenges for kids of all ages. Favorite characters from the series of animated films speed over prehistoric courses stocked with tricky obstacles and special Treestar power-ups. Players can choose to challenge a computer-controlled dinosaur opponent or race against a friend as they guide Littlefoot, Cera, Ducky, Petrie, or Spike towards the finish line, in sunny weather or rain, through 3D environments set in The Land Before Time."},{"t":"The Legend of Alon D'ar","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Stormfront Studios","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Action role-playing game","r":"December 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Alon_D'ar","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4hn6.jpg","s":"The Legend of Alon D'ar is a role-playing video game. The game plays somewhat like the Final Fantasy series' Active Time Battle, with the player having to wait certain time for their turn.\nThe game features a vast world to explore; there are several towns and a variety of monsters to combat. The game also features multi-player experience after unlocking the second character in the story."},{"t":"The Legend of Mir 2","p":["Windows"],"d":"WeMade Entertainment, ActozSoft","u":"Digital Bros, Shanda, Gamepot, Softworld","g":"Fantasy MMORPG","r":"September 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Mir_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/fbrata4qjxb8q0d6np1g.jpg","s":"The Legend of Mir 2 (Korean: 미르의 전설 2) is a sprite-based isometric 3-D massively multiplayer online role-playing game developed by Korean-based WeMade Entertainment"},{"t":"The Legend of Zelda: Oracle of Ages","p":["Game Boy Color"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"February 27, 2001"},{"t":"The Legend of Zelda: Oracle of Seasons","p":["Game Boy Color"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"February 27, 2001"},{"t":"The Legend of Zelda: Oracle of Seasons and Oracle of Ages","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"February 27"},{"t":"The Mystery of the Druids","p":["Windows"],"d":"House of Tales","u":"cdv Software Entertainment","g":"Adventure","r":"March 23, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Mystery_of_the_Druids","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2o60.jpg","s":"In this classic adventure, you will take on the role of young Scotland Yard Detective Brent Halligan, assigned to solve the puzzle surrounding a mysterious order of Druids and a series of gruesome murders. It appears that the neo-Druids are conducting ritual killings in order to conjure up incredibly sinister forces."},{"t":"The Nations","p":["Windows"],"d":"JoWooD Vienna","u":"JoWooD Productions","g":"Real-time strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Nations","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8hkg.jpg","s":"The Nations is a real-time strategy game for Windows based on building a nation and conquering others.\nThe player may choose from 3 races - the Pimmons, the Amazons and the Sajikis. Select a tribe and build up a harmonious community, ruling over them honestly and fairly, discovering new lands and skills, fighting for survival and power.\nYou are now in control of the lives of tribes within The Nations. Take into account that everyone is an individual with their very own habits and characteristics that need to be nurtured and developed."},{"t":"The New Addams Family","p":["Game Boy Color"],"g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_Addams_Family_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5w6d.jpg","s":"They're creepy, kooky, mysterious, spooky, and in trouble up to their ears! It seem that a nefarious real estate agent has designs on turning the family mansion into a high-rent amusement park, and the only way the Addams family can save their old haunt (no pun intended) is to win a court battle. Since courtroom dramas don't make for good video games, the object here is to help the well-meaning Addams clan locate the original ownership papers for the house in order to prove that the land is rightfully theirs. You'll be able to explore 30 massive levels of spooky territory making your way through the mansion, the high court, town hall, a greenhouse, a mineshaft, city streets, and more. There are plenty of power-ups available throughout the game, and each level sports a movie sequence featuring characters from the popular television series. You don't have to be even remotely strange or morbid to get plenty of thrills and chills from The New Addams Family."},{"t":"The Oregon Trail 5th Edition","p":["Windows"],"d":"Broderbund","u":"The Learning Company, Selectsoft","g":"Educational","r":"April 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Oregon_Trail_5th_Edition"},{"t":"The Outforce","p":["Windows"],"d":"O3 Games","u":"Pan Interactive, Strategy First","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"January 4, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Outforce","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2si5.jpg","s":"The Outforce is a real-time strategy game set in the depths of space. There are three races from which to choose, and more than 120 different units available, including tow ships, phasing units, and self-destructing units. The 3-D environment features a free-floating camera with a variable zoom field. The game's artificial intelligence has a genetic learning algorithm, and the physics system includes pressure waves, elastic collisions, gravity, and push-and-pull force. Up to eight players can battle it out over the Internet or on a LAN."},{"t":"The Powerpuff Girls: Chemical X-Traction","p":["PlayStation","Nintendo 64"],"d":"VIS Entertainment, Asylum Entertainment","u":"BAM! Entertainment","g":"Fighting","r":"November 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Powerpuff_Girls:_Chemical_X-Traction","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2f14.jpg","s":"Join the Powerpuff Girls to stop the villains from eating their Chemical X pie in an action packed adventure."},{"t":"The Settlers IV","p":["Windows"],"d":"Blue Byte","u":"Ubi Soft, Gameloft","g":"Real-time strategy, city-building","r":"February 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Settlers_IV"},{"t":"The Shadow of Zorro","p":["PlayStation 2","Windows"],"d":"In Utero","u":"Cryo Interactive, DreamCatcher Interactive","g":"Action-adventure","r":"November 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Shadow_of_Zorro_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3ma6.jpg","s":"The Shadow of Zorro is a video game based on the character Zorro for PlayStation 2 and PC.\n\nShadow of Zorro has received generally poor reviews. Futuregamez.net awarded the game 49% stating that the game \"seems very underdeveloped with poor level structures.\"\n\nA 3D action/adventure game, The Shadow of Zorro is a stealth-based romp through 28 different locations and sports over 700 different animated cut scenes."},{"t":"The Simpsons Wrestling","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"April 12","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Simpsons_Wrestling"},{"t":"The Simpsons: Night of the Living Treehouse of Horror","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"March 26"},{"t":"The Simpsons: Road Rage","p":["PlayStation 2","Xbox","GameCube"],"d":"Radical Entertainment","u":"Electronic Arts, Fox Interactive","g":"Racing","r":"November 19, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Simpsons:_Road_Rage","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co48x8.jpg","s":"The Simpsons: Road Rage is similar to Sega's 1999 video game Crazy Taxi, in that the main objective is to drive picked up passengers to their destinations as quickly as possible. The player receives money upon successfully dropping off each passenger, and bonuses are obtained when they are transported in a short amount of time. Players have to avoid certain hurdles, such as other vehicles and Burns' nuclear transit buses.\n\nThe game contains sixteen different collectible vehicles, six starting locations, and ten different missions. In order to collect the vehicles and unlock starting locations, players must earn progressively more money for each vehicle or starting location they want to get."},{"t":"The Sims: Hot Date","p":["Windows"],"d":"Maxis","u":"Electronic Arts, Aspyr Media","g":"Social simulation","r":"November 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sims:_Hot_Date"},{"t":"The Sims: House Party","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Maxis","u":"Electronic Arts, Aspyr Media","g":"Social simulation","r":"March 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sims:_House_Party"},{"t":"The Sting!","p":["Windows"],"d":"Neo Software","u":"JoWooD Productions","g":"Strategy, Adventure","r":"July 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sting!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2np5.jpg"},{"t":"The Watchmaker","p":["Windows"],"d":"Trecision","u":"Got Game Entertainment","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Watchmaker_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/yjuueifsqq5gnfnwg5hk.jpg","s":"The Watchmaker is a real-time 3D adventure of mystery and magic. Lawyer Victoria Conroy and paranormal expert Darrel Boone are charged with the mission of recovering a magical, pendulum-shaped device that has fallen into the hands of a religious cult. Within 24 hours, the pendulum's power will become great enough to threaten the world, and Conroy and Boone must unravel the secrets of a strange castle to find the device and prevent catastrophe. The player has complete control of both characters who must work in tandem to investigate their surroundings and solve puzzles. The player can also toggle between third- and first-person perspectives."},{"t":"The Weakest Link","p":["PlayStation","Windows","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Traveller's Tales","u":"Activision","g":"Game show","r":"September 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Weakest_Link_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co98cw.jpg","s":"The Weakest Link is an interactive adaptation of the NBC game show made famous by host Anne Robinson's cold, no-nonsense demeanor and catchphrase, \"You are the weakest link. Goodbye!\" Robinson contributes to the game through video clips and voiceovers, while the actual gameplay is rendered using polygon graphics.\n\nPlayers choose from 24 existing characters to represent themselves, ranging from a 17-year-old high school senior to a 66-year-old retired bank manager. The rules are the same as in the TV show with one exception: On the show, players provide answers off the top of their head, but here you given are four answers to choose from. Initially, only the first letter of each answer is given, but after ten seconds the full text of the answers are revealed. In Easy mode, the full text is always available, and in Junior mode, players choose from only two answers."},{"t":"The Wild Thornberrys: Chimp Chase","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"October 5"},{"t":"The World Is Not Enough","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"September 17","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_World_Is_Not_Enough_(2001_video_game)"},{"t":"Theme Park Inc","p":["Windows"],"d":"Bullfrog Productions, Climax Group","u":"EA Games","g":"Construction and management simulation","r":"January 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theme_Park_Inc"},{"t":"This Is Football 2002","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Team Soho","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment, 989 Sports","g":"Sports","r":"September 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/This_Is_Football_2002","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coctt0.jpg","s":"World Tour Soccer 2002 continues the World Tour Soccer (This is Football) series. Features include a full season mode with promotion, relegation and qualification for European tournaments, as well as 320 Club and International teams. The game is endorsed by FIFPro, the International Federation of Professional Footballers.\n\nThe game has four levels of difficulty, matches can range from four to twenty minutes and can be played during the day or at night in a variety of weather conditions. The player can play one or two player exhibition matches or compete in either national seasons or international competitions. However the player cannot compete in all national seasons, only the English, Spanish, Dutch, French, Italian, German, Scottish, Belgian and Portuguese are available. The international competitions are the World Cup, Euro Cup, South America Cup, America Cup, Africa Cup, and Asia Oceania Cup; plus there are six Special Competitions for superteams, timewarp teams, custom and school teams."},{"t":"Thomas & Friends Railway Adventures","p":["Windows"],"d":"Minds Eye Productions","u":"Infogrames","g":"Educational","r":"August 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_&_Friends_Railway_Adventures"},{"t":"Throne of Darkness","p":["Windows"],"d":"Click Entertainment","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Action role-playing, hack and slash","r":"September 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Throne_of_Darkness","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co226f.jpg","s":"Japanese mythology and fast-paced multiplayer mayhem combine to make Throne of Darkness a must-have title for action oriented role-playing fans. Players will control a team of seven unique samurai against a massive single player experience or in online cooperative and competitive play modes.\n\nGuide your seven unique samurai as they seek to overthrow the evil that has overtaken the kingdom and avenge their slain Daimyo. From the gates of the clan citadel to the spire of the Dark Warlord's castle, you'll face more than 30 varieties of fantastic creatures from Japanese myth, including legions of undead warriors, fearsome demons, and giant dragons - all animated in breathtaking 16-bit color. Ten unique levels and dozens of side quests combine to create a truly original combat experience.\n\nThrone of Darkness introduces an innovative multi-character control interface that allows for the simultaneous command of up to four characters through a series of tactical \"plays,\" or formations. Each play is designed to combine the unique strengths of each samurai - such as the ranged attacks of the archer and the two-sword technique of the berserker - into a distinct tactical advantage. Play calling allows for a high level of control and coordination between the player and computer-controlled samurai never before seen in the genre.\n\nIn Throne of Darkness' multiplayer mode, up to 35 simultaneous players vie for the mantle of Dark Warlord in a king-of-the-hill style competition. Through competition and cooperation, four clans of seven samurai challenge the Dark Warlord, his seven lieutenants, and his countless minions, all of which can be controlled by a human player. Once the Dark Warlord is defeated, the conquering team becomes the new warlord."},{"t":"Thunderbirds","p":["Game Boy Color","Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Pukka Games","u":"SCi Games","g":"Action, racing","r":"December 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunderbirds_(2000_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9twy.jpg","s":"SCi Games published a Game Boy Color title of Thunderbirds in 2000."},{"t":"Thunderhawk: Operation Phoenix","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Core Design","u":"Eidos Interactive","g":"Combat flight simulation","r":"October 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunderhawk:_Operation_Phoenix","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/scz53v2plcfrx5o4i340.jpg","s":"Thunderhawk: Operation Phoenix, known as Thunderstrike: Operation Phoenix in North America and Japan, is a 2001 combat flight simulator video game developed by Core Design and released by Eidos Interactive for the PlayStation 2. It is the sequel to Firestorm: Thunderhawk 2."},{"t":"Time Crisis II","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"October 1","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_Crisis_II"},{"t":"Time Crisis: Project Titan","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"June 20"},{"t":"Tintin: Destination Adventure","p":["PlayStation","Windows"],"d":"Infogrames, Runecraft","u":"Infogrames","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tintin:_Destination_Adventure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8xzd.jpg","s":"Tintin: Destination Adventure is a video game loosely based on the series The Adventures of Tintin."},{"t":"Tiny Toon Adventures: Buster Saves the Day","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Warthog","u":"Conspiracy Games, Swing! Entertainment","g":"Platformer","r":"June 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiny_Toon_Adventures:_Buster_Saves_the_Day","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4zck.jpg","s":"Can these cartoon cuties ever catch a break? Not in this tough game for the Game Boy Color. It seems that the nefarious Max has kidnapped the TINY TOONS bunch and imprisoned them somewhere on the campus of ACME Loonyversity. Buster is the only Toon that has managed to evade Max, and now you've got to help him rescue the other characters before it's too late. Guide everyone's favorite wisecracking rabbit through multiple stages of adventures, armed only with his ability to think quickly and hop. ACME Loonyversity makes for an excellent video game setting, using the campus as a springboard to utter hilarity. There are plenty of familiar faces along the journey, as well as a handful of power-ups to make your task a little less daunting. Designed specifically to appeal to younger gamers, cartoon fanatics, and folks with an offbeat sense of humor, Buster Saves the Day is one rollicking good time."},{"t":"Tiny Toon Adventures: Dizzy's Candy Quest","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Formula","u":"Swing! Entertainment","g":"Platform game","r":"October 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiny_Toon_Adventures:_Dizzy's_Candy_Quest","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/e3mnk81rwj0n4gzbowou.jpg","s":"This is a puzzle solving game in which the player (as Dizzy from the Tiny Toons Cartoon) has to collect and return different types of candy to a friendly robot that needs the candy to free himself from Montana Max.\n\nThe game is divided in 5 worlds (The Studios, Ice World, City, Gogo Dodo's world, Looniversity) with varying numbers of sublevels.\n\nGetting to the candy is made complicated by conveyor belts, trap doors and fans as well as dissolving bridges etc. To add to the trouble, clones of popular Tiny Toon characters (Plucky Hamton, Furrball etc.) are programmed to attack the player.\n\nExtras can be collected for extra energy, time etc. The game is presented from a top down perspective. Estimated playtime approx. 30 hours."},{"t":"Tiny Toon Adventures: Plucky's Big Adventure","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Warthog","u":"Conspiracy Games, Swing! Entertainment","g":"Platform","r":"September 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiny_Toon_Adventures:_Plucky's_Big_Adventure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5y4q.jpg","s":"Players head out for an adventure with the Tiny Toons gang in Tiny Toon Adventures: Plucky's Big Adventure for PlayStation. In this title, based on the Tiny Toons animated series kids can play as Plucky, Hamton, Buster and Babs as they explore twenty-plus levels of Acme Looniversity in search of parts for a time machine for Plucky. While avoiding the incorrigible Elmyra and Montana Max, players help the gang seek out the time machine parts in order for Plucky to travel back in time to finish his homework.\n\nSearching all over the Looniversity in Acme Acres, players explore many places including the music room, history room, tennis courts and hallways. Items will vary from a bicycle pump to a potato and once an item is found, players can proceed with the level or return to the controlling character's respective locker to store the item. Each character can carry only three items at one time and players must decide which items are the most useful for each current level. Certain items are not time machine parts but rather will be used to acquire parts later in the game."},{"t":"Tiny Toon Adventures: Wacky Stackers","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Warthog","u":"Conspiracy Entertainment, Swing! Entertainment","g":"Puzzle","r":"December 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiny_Toon_Adventures:_Wacky_Stackers","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2d6y.jpg","s":"What happens when you take classic puzzle gaming and toss in some of the most lovable characters in cartoon history? You get one undeniably awesome game. Buster Bunny, Plucky Duck, and the rest of the TINY TOONS crew are ready for fun, and you're in the driver's seat. The basic premise of this addicting, original title is to arrange pairs of colored eggs, dropping them onto the screen to create combinations. Of course, there are numerous obstacles and would-be detractors to make things more difficult (UFOs, Tasmanian Devils), and time is never on your side. There are plenty of characters from the animated series along for the adventure including Hampton J. Pig and Fifi La Fume and each is prepared for puzzle-style battle. As many as four players can link up to see who has the quickest reflexes, and there are plenty of single-player modes to keep you busy as well. If you're a TINY TOONS fan, this one is a must-have. If you're not a fan well, you should be."},{"t":"Toki Tori","p":["Game Boy Color","Linux","macOS","Windows Mobile"],"d":"Two Tribes","u":"Two Tribes, Capcom, Chillingo, ModRetro","g":"Platform, puzzle","r":"September 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toki_Tori","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4l7s.jpg","s":"He may be a small, yellow chicken, but Toki Tori is no coward. He's the hero of this egg-cellent puzzle platformer. He must rescue all of his kidnapped brothers and sisters, who are still in their fragile eggs."},{"t":"Tokimeki Memorial 3: Yakusoku no Ano Basho de","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Konami Computer Entertainment Tokyo","u":"Konami","g":"Dating sim","r":"December 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokimeki_Memorial_3:_Yakusoku_no_Ano_Basho_de","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5tyc.jpg","s":"The third entry in the popular dating series from Konami! Tokimeki Memorial 3 was the first dating-sim to use fully 3-D cel-shaded girls."},{"t":"Tokyo Xtreme Racer: Zero","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Genki","u":"Genki, Crave Entertainment, Crave/Ubi Soft","g":"Racing","r":"March 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokyo_Xtreme_Racer:_Zero","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2hrz.jpg","s":"Tokyo Xtreme Racer Zero is a racing game developed by Genki for PlayStation 2. Despite its name, it is set between Tokyo Xtreme Racer 2 and Drift, and has enhanced sound and graphics. The game was released in Japan as Shutokou Battle 0, but was also a release in North America. The game was released in a PAL version in Europe and Australia under the title Tokyo Xtreme Racer (not to be confused with the Dreamcast title of the same name).\n\nThis is the first game in the series that has been released on a platform other than the Dreamcast. Zero was originally to be released on the Dreamcast but was then canceled and moved to the PlayStation 2. The Tokyo Xtreme Racer series has produced a total of six games, the first four being U.S. localizations of the first four Shutokou Battle series games and the final two being U.S. localizations of the first and third Kaido Battle series games."},{"t":"Tom Clancy's Ghost Recon","p":["Windows"],"g":"Action","r":"November 13","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom_Clancy%27s_Ghost_Recon_(2001_video_game)","i":"images/steam-15300.jpg","s":"Eastern Europe, 2008. War has broken out on the borders of Russia and the fate of the world hangs in the balance. That's when the call goes out for the Ghostsan elite handful of specially trained Green Berets, armed with the latest technology and trained to use the deadliest weapons."},{"t":"Tom Clancy's Rainbow Six: Rogue Spear: Black Thorn","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom_Clancy's_Rainbow_Six:_Rogue_Spear:_Black_Thorn"},{"t":"Tom Clancy's Rainbow Six: Take-Down – Missions in Korea","p":["Windows"],"d":"Kama Digital Entertainment","u":"Kama Digital Entertainment","g":"Tactical shooter","r":"June 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom_Clancy's_Rainbow_Six:_Take-Down_%E2%80%93_Missions_in_Korea","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2c7n.jpg","s":"Tom Clancy's Rainbow Six: Take-Down – Missions in Korea is the third installment in the Tom Clancy's Rainbow Six Franchise. It was released exclusively to South Korea."},{"t":"Tomb Raider: Curse of the Sword","p":["Game Boy Color"],"r":"July 1"},{"t":"Tony Hawk's Pro Skater 2","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"u":"Activision","r":"June 11, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_Hawk%27s_Pro_Skater_2"},{"t":"Tony Hawk's Pro Skater 2x","p":["Xbox"],"d":"Neversoft, Treyarch","u":"Activision O2","g":"Sports","r":"November 15, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_Hawk's_Pro_Skater_2x","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2g2f.jpg","s":"Tony Hawk's Pro Skater 2x was designed to take full advantage of the technical capabilities of Xbox. The game features the franchise's signature gameplay, intuitive controls, all of the levels from both Tony Hawk's Pro Skater 2 and the original Tony Hawk's Pro Skater, in addition to more populated environments, brand new levels, realistic special effects and smoother lifelike animations. The inclusion of system link gameplay brings the skateboarding experience to life."},{"t":"Tony Hawk's Pro Skater 3","p":["PlayStation 2","GameCube","PlayStation"],"u":"Activision","r":"October 30, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_Hawk%27s_Pro_Skater_3"},{"t":"Top Gear GT Championship","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Kemco","u":"Kemco","g":"Racing","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Top_Gear_GT_Championship","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co82qf.jpg","s":"If realistic racing is your cup of tea, get behind the wheel of the most complete hand-held racer ever. You'll speed through six of Japan's most grueling circuits, blowing past the competition and taking the checkered flag. If the real deal doesn't do it for you, use the game's built-in Track Editor to put together a course that will meet your standards. The All Japan Grand Touring Circuit takes center stage in this detailed extravaganza, as 22 of the Circuits best drivers are the stars here. The vehicles can all be tweaked and upgraded allowing you to truly control your performance and style. Several modes of play make for high replay value and a huge fun factor, while the 32-bit graphics are good enough to pass for those of a home platform game. It's not every day that you get the chance to challenge the world's best GT racers, so you'd better come with your wits sharpened and your seatbelt on."},{"t":"Top Gun: Combat Zones","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Digital Integration, Titus Interactive Studio","u":"Titus Interactive, Mastiff","g":"Combat flight simulation","r":"October 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Top_Gun:_Combat_Zones","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5x9a.jpg","s":"Jump into the cockpits of the Navy's top planes with Top Gun: Combat Zones. With the F-14, F-18, and F-22 under your control, you must embark on dangerous low-level missions throughout hazardous canyons and across vast cityscapes. Eliminate ground or air targets in Southeast Asia, Gulf States, Arctic Circle, and Miramar Base."},{"t":"Toy Story Racer","p":["Game Boy Color","PlayStation"],"d":"Traveller's Tales, Tiertex Design Studios","u":"Activision","g":"Racing","r":"March 2, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toy_Story_Racer","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co91zm.jpg","s":"The race is on! For the first time ever, join Woody, Buzz Lightyear and the gang in a crazy, turbo-blasting remote-controlled racing competition through the action-packed world of Disney's Toy Story!"},{"t":"Trade Empires","p":["Windows"],"d":"Frog City Software","u":"Eidos Interactive, Take-Two Interactive","g":"Business simulation game","r":"September 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_Empires"},{"t":"Tranquility","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"William A. Romanowski","u":"TQworld, LLC.","g":"Platformer, Music, Art","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tranquility_(video_game)"},{"t":"Tribes 2","p":["Windows"],"u":"Sierra Entertainment","r":"March 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tribes_2"},{"t":"Triple Play Baseball","p":["PlayStation","PlayStation 2","Windows"],"d":"Treyarch","u":"EA Sports","g":"Sports","r":"March 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triple_Play_Baseball","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/hixznw6ttfkvvwnj3qjw.jpg","s":"Triple Play Baseball is part of the EA Sports line of MLB Baseball sims. This edition was released in 2001 and would have been called Triple Play 2002 if they had not dropped the year off the title.\n\nLike previous editions you can pick from any of the teams in the National League or American League. Mostly little has changed with this edition except for a world tournament that has been added. You have to win the World Series however to see this option.\n\nThe EA Sports' well respected series of baseball games returns in this release for the hardy PlayStation console. As in previous incarnations, gamers can control nearly all aspects of the Big League action, which takes place in virtual representations of real-life baseball venues and features all of the Major League teams, players, and updated statistics from the 2000 season."},{"t":"TripleA","p":["Windows"],"g":"Strategy, Tactical","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TripleA","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co9s89.jpg","s":"TripleA is a free and open-source turn based strategy game based on the Axis & Allies board game."},{"t":"Tropico","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"PopTop Software, Feral Interactive","u":"Gathering of Developers, MacSoft, Feral Interactive","g":"Construction and management simulation","r":"April 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropico_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2hl1.jpg","s":"As the newly instated dictator of an obscure Caribbean Island, you will build a path of progress for your sapling nation, overcoming poverty, strife and other evils.\nCombining building-oriented gameplay with a healthy dose of Latin American political intrigue - Tropico bundles it all up in a fun, totally original and utterly addictive package."},{"t":"Trouballs","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Paragon Five","g":"Puzzle","r":"September 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trouballs","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2vg9.jpg","s":"If you're ready for explosive, mind-twisting puzzle-action mayhem then grab a copy of TROUBALLS from Capcom. In this massively addictive game developed exclusively for the Game Boy Color, it's up to you to gain as many points as you can while the clock ticks down. Here's how it works: different colored balls are attached to moving gears; you rotate the gears in an attempt to get four balls of the same color aligned (in a 2x2 square grid at the bottom of the screen), which blows them to smithereens. This clears the stage and advances you to the next level. There's no time to rest though, as each successive round of sphere-blasting fun is more difficult than the last. You'll listen to five original songs in the challenging, pop-and-bop gameplay that confronts you on more than 100 fast-moving levels. If you couldn't get enough of TETRIS or BUST-A-MOVE, then you'll love TROUBALLS to pieces."},{"t":"Tsugunai: Atonement","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Cattle Call","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment, Atlus USA","g":"Role-playing","r":"February 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsugunai:_Atonement","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1p3o.jpg","s":"Tsugunai is the story of Reise, a warrior who has inadvertently angered the Goddess of Light by stealing the mystical Treasure Orb. As punishment, the Goddess of Light forever separates his soul from his body unless he atones for his sins by helping the people of Walondia. Without a physical body of his own, the only way to change the fates of the people is to possess them."},{"t":"Tux, of Math Command","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tux,_of_Math_Command"},{"t":"Tweenies: Doodles' Bones","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tweenies:_Doodles'_Bones"},{"t":"Tweenies: Game Time","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tweenies:_Game_Time"},{"t":"Tweety and the Magic Gems","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"July 30","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tweety_and_the_Magic_Gems"},{"t":"Twisted Metal: Black","p":["PlayStation 2"],"u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","r":"June 18, 2001"},{"t":"Twisted Metal: Small Brawl","p":["PlayStation"],"r":"November 26"},{"t":"Typing of the Date","p":["Dreamcast"],"g":"Simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typing_of_the_Date","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3rlv.jpg","s":"Is a Sega Dreamcast game. It is built to use the Dreamcast Keyboard.\n\nThe game is a dating simulation but in order to progress the player has to type in certain words within specific time frames. Not only the concept but also the name of the game are referencing The Typing of the Dead."},{"t":"UEFA Challenge","p":["PlayStation","PlayStation 2","Windows"],"d":"Infogrames Sheffield House","u":"Infogrames Europe","g":"Sports","r":"April 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UEFA_Challenge","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co8tjc.jpg","s":"UEFA Challenge is a Sports game, published by Infogrames, which was released in Europe in 2001."},{"t":"Ultimate Mortal Kombat 3","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"December 21","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultimate_Mortal_Kombat_3"},{"t":"Ultimate Ride","p":["Windows"],"d":"Gigawatt Studios","u":"Disney Interactive","g":"Simulation video game","r":"September 26, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultimate_Ride","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1qpg.jpg","s":"Ultimate Ride is a video game by Disney Interactive for Microsoft Windows in which players design, engineer and ride virtual roller coasters."},{"t":"Universal Studios Theme Parks Adventure","p":["GameCube"],"r":"December 18","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Studios_Theme_Parks_Adventure"},{"t":"Unreal Tournament","p":["Dreamcast"],"u":"Infogrames","r":"March 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unreal_Tournament"},{"t":"Uplink","p":["Windows"],"d":"Introversion Software","u":"Introversion Software, Strategy First, Ambrosia Software","g":"Hacking simulation","r":"October 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uplink_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co220r.jpg","s":"You play an Uplink Agent who makes a living by performing jobs for major corporations. Your tasks involve hacking into rival computer systems, stealing research data, sabotaging other companies, laundering money, erasing evidence, or framing innocent people.\n\nYou use the money you earn to upgrade your computer systems, and to buy new software and tools. As your experience level increases you find more dangerous and profitable missions become available. You can speculate on a fully working stock market (and even influence its outcome). You can modify peoples academic or criminal records. You can divert money from bank transfers into your own accounts. You can even take part in the construction of the most deadly computer virus ever designed."},{"t":"Vampire Night","p":["Arcade","PlayStation 2"],"d":"Wow Entertainment","u":"Namco, Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Rail shooter","r":"November 13, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vampire_Night","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co68w4.jpg","s":"Vampire Night is a light gun game produced by Sega's Wow Entertainment, distributed through Namco and released in 2000 for video game arcades. It was later ported to PlayStation 2 in November 2001. Two players can team up to take down on screen enemies."},{"t":"Vanishing Point","p":["Dreamcast","PlayStation"],"d":"Clockwork Games","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Racing","r":"January 3, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanishing_Point_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co28f6.jpg","s":"You race against the clock and through traffic in this arcade style racing game which features a variety of cars from SUV's to Vipers and everything from a stunt mode to Swiss Alp Mountain racing."},{"t":"Versailles II: Testament of the King","p":["Windows"],"d":"Cryo Interactive","u":"Cryo Interactive, Réunion des Musées Nationaux","g":"Adventure","r":"November 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Versailles_II:_Testament_of_the_King","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6j56.jpg","s":"The game is set in 1700, 15 years after the previous game, at the time of the death of King Charles II. The player assumes the role of Charles-Louis de Farevolles, who turns up at the Court of Louis XIV, with neither influence at Court nor money.\n\nA 25 piece orchestra, led by Skip Sempé, performs an hour of music for the game."},{"t":"Vietnam 2: Special Assignment","p":["Windows"],"d":"Single Cell Software","u":"ValuSoft","g":"Shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam_2:_Special_Assignment","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co92wu.jpg","s":"This sequel to Vietnam: Black Ops is a first person shooter geared towards casual gamers. Running on the LithTech 2 engine, the game features twelve levels representing various combat arenas of the Vietnam War, including jungle and urban outdoor environments and an indoor level set in an ancient temple. The game plot is straight out of Apocalypse Now - you're an American soldier sent into the jungles of Vietnam and Cambodia to find and kill a rogue American special forces operative. Gameplay is standard FPS, with several difficulty settings from which to choose."},{"t":"Virtua Tennis 2","p":["Dreamcast"],"u":"Sega","r":"October 24, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtua_Tennis_2"},{"t":"Virtual Kasparov","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Titus Interactive Studio","u":"Titus Interactive","g":"Chess","r":"April 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_Kasparov","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/kzxfotstuaxjhsnqvqj2.jpg","s":"Kasparov in your pocket! The most brilliant chess player ever will be your personal trainer, sharing with you his technical knowledge and the history of the game... Can you raise the challenge and play head to head against him?"},{"t":"Volfoss","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Tactical role-playing game","r":"February 22, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volfoss","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5rlu.jpg","s":"Volfoss is a tactical role-playing game developed by Namco for the original PlayStation. It was released in Japan in 2001"},{"t":"Wario Land 4","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"u":"Nintendo","r":"August 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wario_Land_4"},{"t":"WarJetz","p":["PlayStation","PlayStation 2"],"d":"The 3DO Company","u":"The 3DO Company","g":"Air combat simulation","r":"May 25, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WarJetz","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4obs.jpg","s":"World Destruction League - Warjetz, the second title in 3DO's World Destruction League series is set in the same post-apocalyptic future as the first game. Citizens from around the world tune in to watch violent, destructive contests of skill and endurance as mighty war machines go to battle for fun and profit.\n\nWhile the series' first Thunder Tanks release focused on ground combat, WarJetz features combat high in the skies above. Players take to the air in modified fighter craft to do battle in 3D-rendered, destructible environments set all across the globe, each warrior hoping to defeat all opposition, survive the contest, and earn the exclusive rank of WDL: WarJetz Air Lord."},{"t":"Wave Race: Blue Storm","p":["GameCube"],"d":"Nintendo Software Technology","u":"Nintendo","g":"Racing","r":"September 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave_Race:_Blue_Storm","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1pet.jpg","s":"Racing atop a bed of dynamically changing waves, gamers must navigate their way through eight different environments under random weather conditions, including the powerful and visually impressive Storm setting.\n\nPlay through, tutorial stunt, time trial challenges, and Championship mode, which offers shortcut-laden Normal, Hard, and Expert circuits, sending you and seven other racers around eight different course environments loaded with buoys."},{"t":"Wave Rally","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Opus Studio","u":"Eidos Interactive","g":"Racing","r":"November 27, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave_Rally","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/pxgzp1qoswewoskj3skz.jpg","s":"Wave Rally, also known as Jet Ski Riders, is a 2001 Jet Ski racing game developed by Opus Studio Inc. and published by Eidos Interactive exclusively for the PlayStation 2. It was released in North America and Europe in 2001, and in Japan in 2002"},{"t":"Wendy: Every Witch Way","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"WayForward Technologies","u":"TDK Mediactive","g":"Action, platform","r":"September 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wendy:_Every_Witch_Way","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2rv7.jpg","s":"She's been around for roughly 50 years, and Wendy the Good Little Witch finally has her own handheld video game. The laws of gravity have been upset, and Wendy is to blame! Help her trek through level after level of maddening obstacles and deadly enemies in order to set things right. There are hidden Moon Stones that Wendy must find in order to get things back to normal, but they're spread out over four vast levels of harrowing action. Packed with strange locations and tough mysteries to solves, WENDY: Every Witch Way features four bonus levels hat have you earning points by flying through the air on a broom, avoiding flying objects, and taking out would-be deterrents with a magic wand. Boasting detailed, cartoon-style graphics and some of the most original gameplay you've ever laid eyes on, WENDY: Every Witch Way is a truly magical experience for gamers of all ages."},{"t":"Where in the World Is Carmen Sandiego? Treasures of Knowledge","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"Riverdeep","g":"Educational/adventure","r":"November 12, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Where_in_the_World_Is_Carmen_Sandiego%3F_Treasures_of_Knowledge","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/poohopt6lpfeqged0jgf.jpg","s":"Where In the World is Carmen Sandiego Treasures of Knowledge is a point-and-click adventure game for the PC released in 2001."},{"t":"White Day: A Labyrinth Named School","p":["Windows"],"d":"Sonnori","u":"Wizard Soft, 4AM Entertainment","g":"First-person, Survival horror, Adventure","r":"September 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Day:_A_Labyrinth_Named_School","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4a9s.jpg","s":"White Day: A Labyrinth Named School is the story of a young man 's trip into a school at the dead of night to deliver some candy in preparation for the Eastern holiday of the same name, White Day, to So-yeong, a girl at school he likes. Finding himself locked up soon after entering, escaping the school now becomes the main objective."},{"t":"Wild Card","p":["WonderSwan","WonderSwan Color"],"d":"Square","u":"Square","g":"Role-playing","r":"March 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_Card_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co58cq.jpg","s":"Wild Card is a Card Based Role-Playing game, developed and published by SquareSoft, which was released in Japan in 2001. Assemble a group from 9 playable characters, and take on randomised quests and special events, which change every time you play!"},{"t":"Williams F1 Team Driver","p":["Windows"],"d":"KnowWonder","u":"THQ","g":"Racing game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Williams_F1_Team_Driver"},{"t":"Williams Pinball Classics (2001)","p":["Windows"],"d":"Redoubt Ltd.","u":"Encore Software","g":"Pinball","r":"September 29, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Williams_Pinball_Classics_(2001)"},{"t":"With Authority!","p":["Windows"],"d":"Genetic Anomalies","u":"THQ/Jakks Pacific","g":"Strategy","r":"February 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/With_Authority!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/twkwmszmyebhtxzqzw5h.jpg","s":"WWE with Authority! is a discontinued online card game featuring the WWE license."},{"t":"Wizardry 8","p":["Windows"],"r":"November 15","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wizardry_8"},{"t":"Wizardry: Tale of the Forsaken Land","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"December 19"},{"t":"World Championship Snooker 2002","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Blade Interactive","u":"Codemasters","g":"Sports","r":"September 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Championship_Snooker_2002_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/r7m20rxuyo27ugtgcjq8.jpg","s":"World Championship Snooker 2002 is the second sports game in the World Championship Snooker franchise."},{"t":"World Series Baseball 2K2","p":["Dreamcast"],"d":"Visual Concepts","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","r":"August 14, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Series_Baseball_2K2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3uwu.jpg","s":"World Series Baseball 2K2 features a complete 2002 roster list and various game modes. Players can compete in a standard exhibition game, go for a franchise, skip directly to the playoffs, compete in a home run derby, or play online.\n\nThe game features several new options compared to its predecessors. Players can take total control of their fielding and lineup positions. It is possible to create, sign, and trade free agents. The game allows players to manage their batting and pitching rotation. It is also possible to set up a custom batting order and take control of every player on the field.\n\nThere are many statistics to keep track of, including hot and cold zones for each player, drafts, injuries, trades and fines.\n\nThe title was also released on Xbox under the title \"World Series Baseball\"."},{"t":"World War II Online","p":["Windows"],"d":"Cornered Rat Software","u":"Strategy First","g":"Massively multiplayer online first-person shooter","r":"June 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II_Online","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3khd.jpg","s":"WWII Online isn't just another WW2 game—it's a massive, player-driven battlefield where your tactics and strategy shape the outcome of a global conflict. Dive into the action on a single server capable of holding thousands of players, hosting epic World War II battles. Join our dedicated community, form your squad, and experience the most immersive and authentic WWII combat simulation ever.\n\nOVERVIEW OF WWII ONLINE\n\nWorld War II Online is a Massively Multiplayer FPS simulation game set in the Western Front of WWII. It’s hosted on a huge single map, and there’s virtually no limit to the number of players who can join the battlefield and fight as infantry, tankers, pilots, or captains.\n\nBEWARE: WWII Online is a simulator classified as a serious game with a mature community. You cannot play this game solo; cooperation between all online players is required to create action and achieve objectives. The game recommends at least 10 hours of practice, sticking with game veterans or an active squad. Ideally, your game session should last more than 1 hour since player-led operations require time and patience."},{"t":"World War III: Black Gold","p":["Windows"],"d":"Reality Pump","u":"JoWood Productions, TopWare Interactive","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"October 16, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_III:_Black_Gold","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1rra.jpg","s":"World War III: Black Gold takes the player to the near future. The political crisis has been going on for months, leading to worldwide turmoil. A secret conference of the UN Central Geological Commission reveals that worldwide oil reserves have reached dangerously low levels: Oil reserves will run out within 8-10 years. This proves too short for even the most highly industrialized countries to switch their economies to alternative energy sources. Soon, mass protests erupt all over the world, effectively shutting down the global economy. The Great Powers have declared WW III..."},{"t":"World's Scariest Police Chases","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Unique Development Studios","u":"Activision, Fox Interactive","g":"Action, driving","r":"June 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World's_Scariest_Police_Chases_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co3yw0.jpg","s":"World's Scariest Police Chases puts the player in the driver's seat of a powerful cop car with the goal of chasing down and apprehending a variety of criminals with diversely dangerous driving styles. The game offers over 50 miles of road and highway to patrol.\n\nA story mode follows the player's virtual law enforcement career and he goes up against more devious criminals and gains access to more powerful vehicles. Other modes of play allow more freeform law enforcement. When driving for law and order doesn't fit the mood, players also have the option of evading their fellow officers in the game's \"Bad Boy\" mode. Television host Sheriff John Bunnel adds commentary as players race toward mission goals."},{"t":"Worms World Party","p":["Windows","Dreamcast","PlayStation"],"d":"Team17, The Code Monkeys, Fluid Studios, Paragon 5","u":"Titus Interactive, Ubi Soft, JAMDAT Mobile, THQ Wireless","g":"Artillery, Strategy","r":"April 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worms_World_Party","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1xjg.jpg","s":"Worms World Party is an artillery game, which is a type of Turn-based strategy game. Like its predecessors, Worms World Party involves controlling a team of worms and using a collection of weaponry to eliminate the opposing team(s). The worms can walk and jump around and use tools such as the ninja rope and parachute to move to otherwise unreachable locations. The worms have an arsenal of dozens of weapons, ranging from longbows to bazookas and from fireballs to Holy Hand Grenades."},{"t":"WWF Betrayal","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"August 7","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWF_Betrayal"},{"t":"WWF Road to WrestleMania","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"November 18","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWF_Road_to_WrestleMania"},{"t":"WWF SmackDown! Just Bring It","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"November 19","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWF_SmackDown!_Just_Bring_It"},{"t":"X-COM: Enforcer","p":["Windows"],"d":"Infogrames Interactive Hunt Valley Studio","u":"Infogrames","g":"Third-person shooter","r":"April 17, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-COM:_Enforcer","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2cpj.jpg","s":"The Place: America - early 21st century. The Mission: Eradicate the extraterrestrials\n\nAlien forces have invaded Earth and are terrorizing the population. To combat this sinister threat, a lone X-COM scientist has created the Enforcer, a high-tech fighting machine made of earthly robotics and scavenged alien technology.\n\nArmed with the latest high-powered weapons, the Enforcer is sent into alien hot spots to fight in adrenaline-charged battles that rage on city streets, across rooftops and in the sewers. YOU are the Enforcer."},{"t":"X-Men: Mutant Academy 2","p":["PlayStation"],"d":"Paradox Development","u":"Activision","g":"Fighting","r":"September 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-Men:_Mutant_Academy_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4kzy.jpg","s":"X-Men: Mutant Academy 2 is a PlayStation video game, the sequel to X-Men: Mutant Academy. Both feature the X-Men in a 3-D fighting arena similar to Mortal Kombat 4.\n\nLike its predecessor, the game allows the player to select from several heroes and villains and includes many of the signature moves from the comic. The game also includes a behind-the-scenes look at X-Men: The Movie concept sketches, costumes and other similar material."},{"t":"X-Men: Reign of Apocalypse","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"r":"September 25"},{"t":"X-Men: Wolverine's Rage","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Digital Eclipse","u":"Activision","g":"Action","r":"May 18, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-Men:_Wolverine's_Rage","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co980z.jpg","s":"X-Men: Wolverine's Rage is a side-scrolling video game designed for the Game Boy Color. The game's plot centres around Wolverine as he pursues Lady Deathstrike, Sabretooth, and Cyber.\n\nUpon discovering schematics that enable her to construct a weapon capable of melting Wolverine's adamantium skeleton, Lady Deathstrike resolves to proceed with the machine. Wolverine must undertake any necessary actions to locate her and prevent the impending threat."},{"t":"X-treme Express","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Syscom Entertainment","u":"Midas Interactive Entertainment","g":"Trains","r":"February 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-treme_Express","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co6t7c.jpg","s":"X-Treme Express is the sequel to the Japanese-only title Tetsu 1: Densha de Battle!. Players race against five other trains in high-speed point-to-point races, being careful not to derail by taking curves too quickly and switching between tracks to knock opponents off the track. The game features ten different courses and eighty different trains."},{"t":"Xtreme Wheels","p":["Game Boy Color"],"d":"Spike","u":"BAM! Entertainment","g":"Sports, racing","r":"April 17, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xtreme_Wheels","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1u7v.jpg","s":"Are you bad enough to handle the best? The world's top BMX racers are ready to take you on, and you have no choice but to answer the call. You'll start at the bottom of the rankings, trying to work your way up the ladder by mastering 20 grueling courses and nailing difficult tricks. The object is to earn as many licenses as possible, which will earn you the right to compete in higher classes. Needless to say, the competition will get smarter and quicker as you advance through the ranks, so you'll need to bring your sharpest skills to the table. There are four different classes to stretch your abilities, and you can actually earn new riders by notching a few victories. XTREME WHEELS is packed with varied hills, jumps, and terrain, so you might want to start off in Exclusive Training mode to get a grip on the handling and realistic physics that the game engine delivers. If you want to be the ultimate Xtreme racer, you'd better get cracking."},{"t":"Yanya Caballista: City Skater","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"October 1"},{"t":"Yoake no Mariko","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Sony Computer Entertainment Japan","u":"Sony Computer Entertainment","g":"Simulation","r":"December 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoake_no_Mariko","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/jqc2ph6huzzsfm2a2hnp.jpg","s":"This PlayStation 2 release is a voice acting simulator that allows you to play with a microphone or with a controller, which turns the gameplay into a more rhythm-game-like experience."},{"t":"You're Under Arrest","p":["PlayStation"],"g":"Visual Novel","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You're_Under_Arrest_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/coaoff.jpg","s":"Taiho Shichau zo is a police-themed adventure base on the manga and anime with the same title. The player takes on a role of a 24-year old rookie police officer who just graduated. The game starts with you coming to your new place at work, and the moment you arrive, a small incident occurs and you get misunderstood for a thief. After situation gets resolved, you get introduced into your new place of work and meet your colleagues, among others the two heroines from manga and anime TV series, Natsumi Tsujimoto and Miyuki Kobayakawa. The game consists of two gameplay elements. Adventure mode which resembles a typical visual novel style with fully voiced dialogues for all but the main character, and animated portraits of characters currently speaking, as well as action choices during dialogues. Patrol mode features a patrol map of town where the player, along with Natsumi and Miyuki, is performing routine patrol work which may trigger an event scene or one of the mini-games. The points are also awarded for various different elements during patrol work."},{"t":"Yu Suzuki Game Works Vol. 1","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yu_Suzuki_Game_Works_Vol._1"},{"t":"Yu-Gi-Oh! Dungeon Dice Monsters","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"March 21, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yu-Gi-Oh!_Dungeon_Dice_Monsters","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5ztw.jpg","s":"In Dungeon Dice monsters, there is a tiled board of 13 x 19 tiles (13 tiles wide) on which play is conducted. On either end of the board sits a \"Monster Lord\" (on the 7th tile from each side on the row closest to the players) representing either player. Each Monster Lord has 3 Life Points. The first player to lose all their Life Points loses. In the Yu-Gi-Oh! Anime, these were referred to as \"Heart Points\"."},{"t":"Yu-Gi-Oh! The Duelists of the Roses","p":["PlayStation 2"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Digital collectible card game","r":"September 6, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yu-Gi-Oh!_The_Duelists_of_the_Roses","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co1ujr.jpg","s":"Yu-Gi-Oh! The Duelists of the Roses is a video game developed by Konami based on the Yu-Gi-Oh! manga and anime series. Its main characters, Yugi Mutou and Seto Kaiba, play opposing teams known as the Lancastrians and the Yorkists. Each team is playable, battling various characters throughout the plot to prevent a ritual summoning by using sorcerous rose cards."},{"t":"Yu-Gi-Oh! The Eternal Duelist Soul","p":["Game Boy Advance"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Digital collectible card game","r":"July 5, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yu-Gi-Oh!_The_Eternal_Duelist_Soul","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5xdx.jpg","s":"Yu-Gi-Oh! The Eternal Duelist Soul is a Yu-Gi-Oh! video game for the Game Boy Advance. It is the international version of Yu-Gi-Oh! Duel Monsters 5 Expert 1. Gameplay is similar to standard dueling rules, except that the Side Deck allows less than 15 cards, and is more for convenience of getting to cards for your Deck than switching between duels, as CPU duels are single duels rather than matches (with some exceptions in events)."},{"t":"Z: Steel Soldiers","p":["Windows"],"d":"Bitmap Brothers","u":"Eon Digital Entertainment, SCi Games","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"June 8, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Z:_Steel_Soldiers","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co48s4.jpg","s":"Lead your Z: Steel Soldiers into the next century rebooted for the rocket fueled fans of the classic hard-core strategy game. In the bloody aftermath of ‘Z’ comes Z: Steel Soldiers. All your favourite strategies and gameplay from the classic version, with graphics that have powered into the 21st century, vivid 3D landscapes, and a state of the art engine that brings your game to life. Meet the new breed of robots, with a taste for war and a warped sense of humour. Z: Steel Soldiers."},{"t":"Zax: The Alien Hunter","p":["Windows"],"d":"Reflexive Entertainment","u":"JoWooD Productions","g":"Action","r":"September 28, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zax:_The_Alien_Hunter","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2mth.jpg","s":"Zax: The Alien Hunter is a PC action game from Reflexive Entertainment."},{"t":"Zero Comico","p":["Windows"],"d":"GMM Entertainment, Oogo S.r.l.","u":"Medusa Games, Oogo S.r.l.","g":"Graphic Adventure","r":"November 7, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero_Comico","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4b23.jpg","s":"Zero Comico is an Italian video game featuring three famous Italian comedians: Aldo Giovanni and Giacomo as the main characters.\n\nThe player is in control of all three characters as they try to free the world from an evil force that has taken control over mankind through television.\nThe game features a variety of puzzles with a comedy theme."},{"t":"Zero Gunner 2","p":["Arcade","Dreamcast","Windows"],"d":"Psikyo, Zerodiv","u":"Psikyo, Zerodiv, Mobirix","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"May 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero_Gunner_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co4vl4.jpg","s":"In the near future, scientists working for the powerful Asian conglomerate known as Igem have discovered a new energy source. Refusing to share their technique with the rest of the world, Igem eventually developed a weather control machine named ONI, which they intend to use for the purpose of global domination. A squadron of ace helicopter pilots, called Zero Gunner, is assembled to destroy ONI - but Igem fights back with everything they've got...\n\nZero Gunner 2 is a top-down shooter. All backdrops and vehicles in this game are modelled in 3D, though the gameplay mostly follows the conventions of 2D shooters. Since your craft is a helicopter, a \"target and spin\" feature has been added, whereby you can both rotate your craft to attack an enemy more efficiently, or alternatively lock on a particular point in space and rotate around it while still firing. These actions are not automatically executed, and are controlled by the player; in order to succeed, mastering this feature is imperative.\n\nThe usual power-ups and boss enemy battles are included in the game, along with a selection of helicopters to choose from."},{"t":"Zone of the Enders","p":["PlayStation 2"],"r":"March 26","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zone_of_the_Enders_(video_game)"},{"t":"Zoo Tycoon","p":["Windows"],"r":"October 17","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zoo_Tycoon_(2001_video_game)"},{"t":"ZuPaPa!","p":["Arcade"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK","g":"Platform","r":"September 1, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ZuPaPa!","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co2dtx.jpg","s":"An action puzzle game where you play as a Zupapa, a strange creature that hurls tiny Zupipi characters at your foes. The more Zupipi on an enemy when he is trampled, the larger the explosion."},{"t":"Zwei: The Arges Adventure","p":["Windows"],"d":"Nihon Falcom","u":"Nihon Falcom, Taito, Xseed Games","g":"Action role-playing","r":"December 20, 2001","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zwei:_The_Arges_Adventure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_small/co5w90.jpg","s":"Boasting a completely standalone story set on a mysterious “low fantasy” island floating amongst the clouds, Zwei: The Arges Adventure plays out as a classically styled action-oriented 2D dungeon crawler with oodles of optional content and unlockables, player-defined challenge through a unique food-based experience system, and a pervading tongue-in-cheek humor style that helps set it apart from other entries in the genre."}];
